window.LfcsText = {"Introduction/1":["Hello and welcome to code cloud's Linux Foundation certified system administrator. LF CS course. I'm excited to have you join me on this journey into the world of Linux System Administration. My name is Jeremy Morgan. And throughout this course, we'll be immersing ourselves in the essential principles of managing Linux systems effectively.","Now, Linux is a versatile and widely adopted operating system known for its stability, security and flexibility. It's the preferred platform for developers due to its robust command-line interface, extensive package management system and a vast array of open source software with its Scalability and reliability. Linux powers everything from personal computers to enterprise servers and embedded systems making it a cornerstone of modern computing infrastructure according to Stack overflows insights, Linux stands out as the most common and love platform for development work further solidifying its reputation as a cornerstone of modern computing infrastructure. Now, let's talk about the structure of the course. This course is thoughtly divided into several parts to accommodate varying levels of expertise.","The first section on essential commands covers logging into Linux systems and working with files and directories. The operations, deployment section focuses on automating system maintenance tasks, managing startup processes and services, diagnosing and managing processes, scheduling tasks and adjusting kernel runtime parameters. The users and groups section covers creating, modifying and managing local user accounts and groups configuring systemwide environment profiles, setting user resource limits and controlling access to the root account. The networking section covers configuring IP B four and IP B six networking, hostname, resolution, bridge and bonding devices, packet filtering port redirection N A reverse proxies, load balancers, system time synchronization and SSH servers and clients. The storage section encompasses managing physical storage partitions, swap, space file systems, mount configurations, remote file systems, network block devices, NBD storage, performance, monitoring and advanced file system permissions.","Now we understand the importance of hands on learning and that's why we provide you with just in time access to the infrastructure that you need to practice what you learn. Our browser based hands on labs offer interactive and practical learning experiences. Ensuring you gain real world skills, you'll have access to labs where you can gain hands on experience with all the concepts taught in the course. Since the exam is practical and requires you to perform tasks similar to those of a system administrator practicing in the labs is crucial. These labs can be launched directly from your browser eliminating the need to set up your own infrastructure.","Each lab contains a series of questions or exercises and a terminal for you to execute the necessary tasks. Now, while hints and solutions are available, if you get stuck. It's encouraged to try solving problems independently as the exam won't provide hints, instant feedback from the lab system is provided. When you check your answer, allowing you to work through the labs multiple times until you're comfortable with the task at code cloud community matters. We've got a vibrant forum where you can post questions, share your insights or lend a hand to fellow learners, join the code cloud community and be a part of this learning ecosystem.","Whether you're starting your journey in Linux System Administration or aiming to enhance your existing skills. The LF CS course is your pathway to success. Let's embark on this exciting adventure into the world of Linux System Administration together."],"Introduction/2":["Hello and welcome to the Linux Foundation Certified System Administrator preparation course. I'm Jeremy Morgan. And together with Alexandre Andre, together, we're going to guide you through the Linux System Administration concepts that you need to know for this exam. Linux Foundation certified CIS admin certification was developed by the Linux Foundation to help meet the increased demand for Linux administration talent. LF CS helps you validate your system administration skill set and stand out in the market.","During the course, we'll cover each of the topics you'll need to know for the LF CS exam from the five domains of knowledge. It contains essential commands which will cover logging into Linux systems and working with files and directories operations deployment where we'll explore the system boot, process, task, automation and managing critical resources and processes, users and groups which will not only cover creating and managing user and group accounts but also setting resource quotas and configuring advanced authentication options, networking where we'll learn to work with network services, routing and packet filtering and storage management where we'll learn to set up logical volume management, raid and encrypted storage as well as advanced file system permissions. Now, this is primarily a hands on course with videos, labs and mock exams that will help you prepare for the certification. The LF CS exam is a hands on exam and you need enough practical experience to pass it. So this course is designed accordingly, 30% of videos, 60% of labs and 10% mock exams.","And that is around 30 hours of lab time alone. And you can go through these as many times as you want."],"Essential Commands/3":["There will be many commands that we'll use in Linux and each command has a lot of command-line switches. Now, how are we supposed to remember them all as we use command repeatedly, we'll learn everything about it and memorize what each option does. But in the beginning, we might forget about these options just after one or two uses. That's why Linux gives you multiple ways to access help, manuals and documentation right from the command-line. Let's say you want to see that long listing format with LS to get a look at the file permissions, but you forgot what the correct option was.","Was it dash P for permissions? We can get a quick reminder with LS dash dash help. This will show us a lot of output. But if we scroll up, we'll find what we're looking for the dash L flag in this case. Now, you can see here how command-line options are sorted alphabetically and described with short text.","That's why the dash dash help option for commands will be very helpful when we forget about these options and we will because there are so many of them for each command for LS. That's OK. Because it's a pretty simple command, other commands, however, are very complex and we need to read longer explanations to understand what they do and how we use them. That's why the dash dash help option for commandss will very often be helpful when we forget about these options. And we will because there are so many of them for each command.","Let's take journal CTL as an example, a command that lets us read system logs. We'll type in journal CTL dash dash help and it will show us this. Now, notice this opens in a slightly different way. Look at where it says lines one through 27 in the bottom left corner. This opened what Linux calls a pager.","It's simply a text viewer of sorts that lets us scroll up and down with our arrow keys or page up, page down to exit this help page press Q. Now all important commands in Linux have their own manuals or man pages to access a command's manual, enter man, name of command. Now, in our case, we'll use man journal CTL. And now we get a short description of what the command does in name general syntax of the command. In synopsis, detailed description of the command, how it works and so on and description and some manual pages even have examples near the end of the manual.","Now, sometimes you will have two man pages with the same name. For example, printf is a command but PRINTF is also a function that can be used by programmers. Manual pages can fall into one of these categories. And we can see these by looking at the manpage for man itself by typing in man man. Now if you want to read the manpage about printf the command, you tell man that you want to consult printf from section one like this type in man.","One printf. If you want to read about printf the function, then you tell man, you want to look at section three man three printf. Now it's useful to know that during online exams, the Linux Foundation will let you use man and dash dash help. So try to use dash dash help if you forget a command-line option because that gives you the fastest results diving deep into a manual page will eat up some time. Now this is all well and good when we know what command we want to explore.","But what if we can't even remember the name of the command that we need to use? Imagine you forgot the name of the command that lets you create a new directory. How would you search for it? Apropos is a command that lets you search through man pages. It looks at the short description of each manpage and tries to see if it matches the text that we enter.","For example, with the next line, we can search for all man pages that have the word director in their short descriptions. We'll use director and not directory director will match commands that contain the word directory, but also the ones that contain directories. So we'll keep it more generic this way. Now, the first time we run Apropos director, we'll probably get an air. That's because Apropos relies on a database.","A program must refresh it periodically. Since we just started this virtual machine, the database hasn't been created yet. Now, in servers that have already run for days, there should be no need to do this as it will be done automatically. And now the Apropos command should work. We'll type in Apropos director and if we scroll up, we can see the entry, we're looking for mkdir, but those are a lot of entries.","It makes it hard to spot what we're looking for. You see Apropos doesn't just list commands. It also lists other things we don't need. Currently, we see stuff like two that signals the entry is in section two of the man pages and section two contains system calls provided by the Linux kernel. That's just a little too advanced for our purposes.","Commands are found in sections one and eight. So we can tell a proposes to only filter out results that lead to commands from these categories. We do this by using the dash S option followed by a list of the sections we need. For example, a proposes dash S one comma eight director and now we can spot what we're looking for more easily notice how MKD IRS description contains the word directories. If we would have used the word directory in our apropos search, this command wouldn't have appeared since directory wouldn't have matched directories.","This is something to keep in mind when you want to make your searches as open as possible and match more stuff. Another thing that will save a lot of time is auto completion type in system C and I press tab and you get SYSTEMCTL. Although this is not technically system documentation, it can be helpful. Many commands have suggestions on what you can type next. For example, try this type system CTL.","Add a space after the command. Don't press enter and now press tab twice. You'll get a huge list of suggestions. This can help you figure out what your options are for that command. You shouldn't always rely on this.","It's not necessary that absolutely all options are included in the suggestion list. Now to add to that type in system CTL list dash dep, press tab and now dependencies will get added on to the end and you get system CTL list dependencies. This is tab, auto completion and many commands support it when you press tab twice. If your command interpreter can figure out what you want to do, it will automatically fill in the letters if there are many autocomplete suggestions and it can't figure out which one you want. Press tab again and it will show you the list of suggestions that we observed earlier.","These will be huge time saver in the long run and they might even help you in the exam to shave off a few seconds here and there, which might add up and let you explore an extra question or two. Now, tab suggestions and autocompletions also work for file names or directory names, try LS dash U and press tab or LS dash us R tab tab. And now we can see directories available in slash us R without even needing to explore this directory with LS beforehand. And if we have a long file name, like wordpress underscore archive dot TGZ, we might be able to type wor and press tab and that long name will be autocompleted. Well, manuals and help pages are super useful.","The first few times you use them, it might be hard to figure out how to do something with just that info alone. So we recommend you take a command that you know nothing about and try to figure it out with just man and dash dash help and try to figure out how to do something. This practice will help you develop the ability to quickly look for help. When you're taking the LF CS exam, there will be questions about theory that you either don't know about or just forgot. If you know how to quickly figure out the answer with a manpage or a help page, you'll be able to pass the exam much more easily."],"Essential Commands/5":["In this lecture, we'll look at how Linux manages hard links to understand hard links and soft links. First, we must learn some very basic things about bioss systems. Let's imagine a Linux computer is shared by two users, Aaron and Jane Aaron logs in with his own username and password and Jane logs in with her own username and password. This lets them use the same computer but have different desktops, different program settings and so on. Now, Aaron takes a picture of the family dog and saves it into slash home slash Aaron slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G.","Let's simulate a file like this. Now, with this, we created a file at pictures slash family underscore dog dot JPG and stored the text picture of Milo the dog inside. Now there's a command in Linux that lets us see some interesting things about files and directories. Now we'll notice an I node number. What is this file systems like XFSXT four and others keep track of data with the help of I nodes.","Our picture might have blocks of data scattered all over the disk but the I node remembers where all those pieces are stored. It also keeps track of metadata, things like permissions when this data was last modified, last accessed and so on. But it would be inconvenient to tell your computer. Hey, show me inode 52946177. So we work with files instead the one called family underscore dog.jp G.","In this case, the file points to the I node and the I node points to all of the blocks of data that we require and we finally get to what interests us here. Now we notice this in the output of our Stack command. There's already one link to our I note, yes, there is. When we create a file, something like this happens, we tell Linux, hey, save this data under the file name family underscore dog.jp G and Linux says, OK, we'll group all the files data under I node 52946177 data blocks and inode created, we'll hardlink the file family underscore dog.jp G to I node 52946177. Now, when we want to read the file, we'll say, hey, Linux, give me the data for family underscore dog.jp G file.","And Linux says, OK, let me see what I node, this links to, here's all the data you requested for I node 52946177. So the number shown as links in the output of the Stack command is the number of hard links to this I node from files or file names. Easy to understand. But why would we need more than one hard link for this data? Well, Jane has her own folder of pictures at slash home slash Jane slash pictures.","How could Aaron share this picture with Jane? Now, the easy answer is to just copy family underscore dog.jp G two slash home slash Jane slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G. No problem. Right. But now imagine we must do this with 5000 pictures.","We would have to store 20 gig of data twice. Why use 40 gig of data when you could just use 20 gig. So how can we do that instead of copying slash home slash Aaron slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G to slash home slash J slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G, we could hard link it to slash home slash J slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G. And the syntax of the command is Ln path to the target file, then path to the link file. The target file is the file that you want to link with.","The link file is simply the name of the new hard link that we create. Technically the hard link created at the destination is a file like any other. The only special thing about it is instead of pointing to a new I node, it points to the same I node as the target file. So in our imaginary scenario, we would use a command like Ln slash home slash Aaron slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G space slash home slash J slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G. Now our picture is only stored once but the same data can be accessed at different locations through different file names.","If we run the Stack command, now we can see the links are now too. This is because this I node now has two hard links pointing to it. Now, another beautiful thing about hardlinks is this Aaron and Jane share the same 5000 pictures through hard links, but maybe Aaron decides to delete his hardlink of slash home slash Aaron slash pictures slash family underscore dog.jp G. Now what will happen with Jane's picture? Nothing.","She'll still have access to that data. Why? Because the inode still has one hard link to it, it had to, but now it has one. But if Jane also decides to delete her hard link to slash home slash Jane slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G, the I node will have zero links to it. When there are zero links, the data itself will disappear from the file system.","Now, the beauty of this approach is that people that share hard links can freely delete what they want without having a negative impact on other users that still need that information. But once everyone deletes all their hard links to that data, the data itself will be deleted. Technically, the data blocks are not actually erased, but they're just marked as unused so that the system can overwrite them with new data. But from the user's perspective, the data is gone so it is intelligently removed only when everyone decides that they don't need it anymore. Now, there are some limitations of hard links.","You can only hard link to files, not directories, and you can only hardlink the files on the same file system. If you had an external drive mounted at slash M and T slash backups, you would not be able to hardlink a file from your SSD at slash home slash and slash file to some other file on slash M and T slash backups. Since that's a different file system. And there are some things to take into consideration when you hardlink first, make sure that you have the proper permissions to create the link file at the destination. In our case, we need write permissions at slash home slash J slash pictures.","Second, when you hardlink a file, make sure that all users involved have the required permissions to access that file for Aaron and Jane, this might mean that we need to add both their usernames to the same group. For example, family, then we'd use a command to let the group family read and write to this file. You only need to change permissions on one of the hard links. That's because you're actually changing permissions stored by the IOE. So once you change permissions at slash home slash slash pictures slash family underscore dog.jp G then slash home slash Jane slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G and all other hard links will show the same new sets of permissions."],"Essential Commands/6":["Let's look now at how Linux manages soft blinks. You know how when you install a program on windows, you might get a shortcut on your desktop. Now you double click on that shortcut and an application gets launched. Now, the application is obviously not installed on your desktop. It may have its files stored in C program files in my cool app directory.","And when you double click the shortcut, this only points to an executable file at program files, my cool app application dot exe. So the double click on that shortcut basically redirects you to the application dot exe which gets executed soft links and Linux are very similar. Now, a hard link is pointed to an I node, but a soft link is nothing more than a file that points to a path. Instead it's almost like a text file with a path to a file or directory inside the syntax of the command to create a soft link, which is also called a symbolic link is the same as before. But we need to add the dash S or symbolic option, for example, to create a symbolic link that points to the pictures.","And this is Ln dash S path to the target path to the link file. Now, the path to the target is our soft link will point to this path which is the location of a file or directory. The path to the link file is our softlink file that will be created, for example, to create a symbolic link that points to our family dog picture, we can run the following command. And now if we list files and directories in our long listing format with the LS L command, we'll see output like this. Now, the L at the beginning shows us that this is a soft link.","And LS dash L even displays the path that the soft link points to. Now, if this path is long, LS dash L may not show the entire path. An alternative command to see the path stored in a soft link is readlink. So in our case, it would be readlink, family dog shortcut.jp G. Now you may also notice that all permission bits RWX seem to be enabled for this file.","That's because the permissions of the soft link do not matter. Now, if you try to write to FSTAB shortcut, this will be denied because of the permissions to the destination file apply. And Etsy FSTAB does not allow regular users to write here in our first command. We used an absolute path slash home slash Aaron slash pictures, family underscore dog.jp G. Now, if we ever change the directory name Erin in the future to something else, this softlink will break and you can see a broken link highlighted in red in the output of the LS dash L command.","Now to tackle this, you would create a soft link with a relative path. Say for example, you were in the home directory of Aaron, you could create a soft link using the relative path of the family dog file. Instead of specifying the complete path. When someone tries to read relative picture shortcut, they get redirected to pictures slash family underscore dog.jp G relative to the directory where the soft link is now. Since soft links are nothing more than paths pointing to a file, you can also softlink to directories or you can soft link to files and directories on a different file system."],"Essential Commands/9":["Now, let's look at how to search for files in Linux. After you get a little bit familiar with the Linux, OS, you learn that files are very nicely organized. If you want to configure your SSH daemon, you know, you'll find the relevant config files in slash Etsy slash SSH. You need to find logd, you can go to slash bar slash log most of the time you'll know where everything is at least approximately. So why would you need to search for files?","Well, let's look at some typical scenarios. Imagine you have a website and you want to find all of your image files. If your website's directory would be slash user slash share, you could quickly get a list of all.jp G files with a command like this. Now, in a different scenario, you're almost running out of disk space, this servers hosting virtual machines. And you notice that most of the virtual machines require files under 20 gig.","You figure out that you can search for files that are larger than 20 gig to filter out the abnormally large ones. Now, we don't have such large files available, but here's how you would look for files larger than 10 megabytes or let's say you've just updated an application and you're curious to see what files were changed. You can quickly look at all the files that have been modified in the last minute with a command like this. Now, of course, this applies to many other scenarios like you could use a similar command to see what configuration files your administration team changed in the last hour. Now, from these examples, it's clear that the command to search for files is fine.","Let's take a look at the syntax we'll use throughout this lesson. For example, to find a file named file one dot text in the directory slash bin, run the command, find slash bin, dash name, file one dot text. Now name is a search parameter used to specify the name of the file you're looking for. You can sometimes skip specifying the path to the directory you want to search though and when you do that, it searches in the current directory. Now the first few times you use this command, it may happen that you mix up the directory path with the search parameters, meaning instead of writing find slash bin, dash name, file one dot text, you may write, find dash name, file one dot text slash bin.","If you find yourself falling into this trap, just think about it this way. First, I have to go there, then I will find it. You have to enter your room and only after that, you can search for your keys. This will remind you that you first have to specify the search location and then the search parameters. Now, with this basic knowledge, out of the way, let's focus on what makes the real magic happen.","Let's look at some other parameters. Now, we just saw the name parameter being used already. It's used to find files with a specific name. In this case, Felix. This however is case sensitive, meaning it won't find a file named Felix with a capital F.","If you'd like to find the command to not be case sensitive or case insensitive add an I in front of the option to make it I name at times, you may want to find multiple files that have a pattern in their names. For example, I want to find all files that start with a lowercase F for this, I use a wildcard expression which is a starting expression followed by a star. The star is like a joker card for text. It will match anything even if it's zero characters or 100. In this case, it matches all the names starting with F.","We already saw in the examples. A command that looks for files modified in the last minute to remember dash Mmin. Think about modified minute. But let's try to understand what minus one means here as we can use things such as one minus one and plus one and they mean different things Now how find works with time stamps is not exactly intuitive. So let's make it easier to understand.","First, let's imagine the time is 1205 with dash mmin five. We'd see files created exactly at 1201 and not 12 o'clock as we'd expect. That's because when we backtrack from the current time, we step through 1205, 1204, 1203, 1202 and end up at 1201. Exactly five values. Now with dash Mmin dash five, we'd find files modified in the last five minutes.","That means files were modified at 1205, 1204, 1203, 1202 and 1201. Now, 12 o'clock was not included for the same reason as before from 1205 to 1201. We have exactly five values to 12 o'clock. It would be six values. So we'd need to bump up the dash mmin to dash six to also include that timestamp.","Now with dash Mmin plus five, we'd see files created from 12 o'clock to 1159 1158 and beyond, you might notice there's a twist this time, we see 12 o'clock here instead of 1201. When you use a plus sign, the count skips the current time. So it starts counting from 1204 instead of 1205. And we have five minutes from 1204 to 12 o'clock. So another way to think about this find dash mmin plus five command is that it shows us any object that was created more than five minutes ago and 1201 is not more than five minutes ago, but rather exactly five minutes ago.","So more than five minutes is 12 o'clock or earlier. Another similar option is MTIME, but instead of working with minute units, it works with 24 hour units. Dash M times zero matches what was modified in the last 24 hours. Dash mtime. One matches what was modified between 24 and 48 hours ago and so on.","It's worth noting that modification might mean either when the file was last edited, but also when it was created, if we edit a text file at 1205, it will show up as modified at 1205. But if we create a new file at 1206, this will also count as a modification time. So it will show up as modified at 1206 even though that's when it was created. Linux also has a thing called change time for files, which might sound like the same thing as modify time, but it's actually different. Modify time refers to the time when the contents have been modified.","Change time refers to the time when the metadata was changed. Now, metadata is data about data. So in this case, data about your file, this might mean something like file permissions or other similar information. And this is where change time could be useful. Imagine you suddenly get errors in some application and you suspect it's because someone changed some file permissions in the wrong way.","You could find files with permissions changed in the last five minutes with a command like this. In our initial examples, we use dash size to search for files based on their size to find files of a size. Exactly 512 KB, we can run the five command with the size parameter set to 512 KK stands for kilobytes. Here's a quick table showing the different values. Now, C stands for bytes, K for kilobytes, M for megabytes and G for gigabytes note that the M and G must be capital letters to search for files greater than 512 KB.","We can use plus 512 K and for files less than 512 KB, we can use minus 512 KB. We saw how we can find all files or directories that start with the letter F with a command like find dash size F star. We also learned you could find files of a certain size with a command like find dash size 512 K. Now size 512 K and name F are search parameters. But in the command's manual, we'll also see that the part where we tell, find what to look for is called a search expression.","That's because just like in mathematics, we can combine multiple parameters and build a more complex search expression. For example, assume we want to find files that start with the letter F but also have a size of exactly 512 K. We can combine our previous two parameters to build the search expression. And to do that, we simply enumerate both of them. When we write it like this, there's an implied and operator between the dash name parameter and the dash size parameter.","This means that both conditions must be true for the files to show up in the results. But what if we want an or operator between these parameters, files or directories that start with the letter F but also files that have a size of exactly 512 K, not necessarily meeting both conditions at the same time. All we need to do is add a dash o where we want our or operator to be. So we'd end up with this command. Now, another interesting thing we can insert into a find expression is the not operator to make it easier to understand.","Let's look at another example, say we want to find all files that do not begin with the letter F to exclude files, beginning with the letter F from our results. We would use the dash not option before the dash name option. This would return a list of file names that do not begin with the letter F. Another way to write the knot operator is to use the exclamation sign. One important note.","However, for a command interpreter, bash, the exclamation sign has a special meaning. And when it sees that in a command, it will interpret it a certain way. Thinking we want to perform special action in that spot to tell bash to ignore the special meaning of this character and just consider it a regular character. We can do what's called escaping it to escape it. We just add a backslash in front of the exclamation sign.","We can also search for files or directories based on their permissions. We'll use the 664 octal notation for our permissions. 664 means this set of permissions. The user can read or write, the group can read and write and others can read to search for files based on their permissions. We can use commands like these and this will look for files which have exactly these permissions.","Now, if we put a dash before that 664, it will look for files that have at least these permissions, which means that even if the file has some extra permissions set, it will still show up in the search results. But if it has less than these permissions, it won't show up. For example, 664 denotes that a user should have read and write permissions. If they only have read permissions, but no right, then find will not show this in the search result. Think of it as a bare minimum permissions are these.","Now, we can use a front slash 664 to look for files which have any of these permissions. Unlike the bare minimum condition above this one's more inclusive. For example, if a user can read the file but cannot write to it, it will still show up in search results as one permission has been matched U equals R. So it doesn't matter if the other permissions exist or don't exist. Now, here's an alternative way to write each of these.","Now imagine we have a group of files. We want to find files which only the owner can read and write with no other permissions to do that. We would run find dash perm 600. Now this would match the files, Felix James and Bob to find files that the owner can execute at least. But the rest of the permissions can be anything.","We would run find dash perm dash 100 which would match only Freya and Jacob. Now imagine you want to make sure that nobody else can read these files except the users and groups that own them. In this case, we would use the not operator to look for files that others cannot read. We would run this command which matches Felix James, Bob, Freya, John and Bean. Finally imagine that we want to find files that can only be read either by the user, the group or others.","It doesn't matter who it is, but at least one of them should be able to read it to do this. We would write a command like this. In this case, all our files match the condition. If no one can read it, it won't show up in the results."],"Essential Commands/10":["As we can see when it comes to Linux, we deal with a lot of text. Our SSH session is all text. We see text output and we input commands as text and later on we'll find out that configuring applications or the system itself is also done through text files. This means we'll have to know how to view text files, edit text files, transform text files and compare text files. Let's start out with the most basic thing.","When the file you're trying to view is very small. You can use the cat command followed by the name of the file that you want to inspect. Here. We have cat slash home slash users dot text. Now to view the file reversed from the bottom up, we use the T command.","But what if the file is very long? This might happen when you want to view a log file where a program records all status messages about what it did, what succeeded, what failed et cetera. Many times when you deal with logs, you'll want to see what happened in the last few seconds or minutes. You'll find those log lines at the end or the tail of this file, you can view the last lines with a command like this. Now by default, the tail command will show you the last 10 lines.","But you can tell it how many lines you want to see with the dash N option. The opposite of this command to view the first few lines instead of the last ones is the head command. We have nine lines of text here. But the first one to log is an empty line, bringing the total to 10. We have nine lines of text here.","But the first one in the log is an empty line, bringing the total number to 10 to specify how many lines to return from the start of the file. We use the same dash N option. Now, sometimes we'll have to deal with large files that require many changes in many places. Manually editing and changing the same thing in many lines of text can be tedious, but we have tools that can help automate these tasks. Now imagine you have a file with this content.","A list of people, their addresses, phone numbers and citizenships. We see that Canada is spelled incorrectly in some places showing up as KDA instead missing an A in the middle. We would like to replace all occurrences of Canada to Canada. There's a utility called said that can search and replace all the required text. The names comes from stream editor, a stream of text energy utility.","It gets transformed in some way and then it outputs that modified stream with this command. We can preview what said would do without actually changing the file. And it's recommended that you first preview what would happen to make sure that there's no mistakes. Let's break down how our command works. Now, here we have said S slash can slash Canada slash G userinfo dot tech.","Now, the easiest part we see here at the end, we pass the name of our file or the path to that file if it's not in our current directory. Now, S slash can slash Canada slash G is obviously where the magic happens. We must wrap this between single quotes to make sure that our command interpreter does not interfere with the content. In here, we can have special characters like an asterisk. And if we don't wrap it between these quotes, bash, our command interpreter might transform that into something else because bash interprets what we want that asterisk to do into something special by wrapping our content between these single quotes.","Bash knows that it should not interpret anything between those quotes. Now, the first S here tells, said that this a substitute command for search and replace. Then we tell it to replace it with Canada. And finally, the last G is for global search and replace, normally said would only replace the first thing it finds on each line by passing global. Here we tell it to search and replace all occurrences even if there are multiple per line.","So in this command, for example, without the global option, we see that only the first occurrence of Canada has changed to Canada, the remaining occurrences of Canada on each line are left as they are. That's what the global option helps with to change all occurrences on each line. Not just the first one. Now up to this point said just showed us what it would do but not edit the file. If we look at the contents of the file, after running the S command, we see it's still the same.","When we're happy with the preview, it shows us we can tell it to actually edit the file by passing in the dash I command-line option dash I is short for in place to edit in place directly in the file. We won't see any change on the command-line. But if we were to run cat on user info, we could see that the changes were made to the file. Now, let's say we would like to extract the names alone from this file. The first column in the file for this, we would use the cut command.","So cut is a utility that can extract the parts we need from a file. For example, if we only want the first column that appears on each line, we would specify cut with the dash d to specify the delimeter words are separated by a space. So we add a space wrapped between single quotes. This tells us that the space character is the one separating the parts we need. We can use anything as a delimeter.","For example, if we add numbers such as 10.22 or 12.34 and so on, and we want to extract only the integer part. We would use a dot as a delimeter and we would be able to extract 10 and 12 from those numbers with dash F. We specify the fields we want to extract. In this case, each word is a field and we want to extract field one, the first word on each line. Let's look at another example.","Let's say that this other file separates different entries with commas and we would like to extract all of the countries from this content. Now, countries are the third field in this case. Also, after we extract our country names, we want to tell our command to save this output in a new file called countries dot text. We'll do that with a simple redirection. Now we'll learn more about redirections in a later lesson for.","Now, all that you need to understand is that we can redirect with a greater than sign and that will save the output to a file. Now, for this purpose, we run the command cut dash D, then we follow up with the delimeter A comma and with the slash F three, we extract the third field, then we pass the name of our file, userinfo dot text and we redirect the output to countries dot text and that creates a file with this content. Now, in the countries dot text file, we have a list of countries but they are duplicates. We'd like to get a list of the unique countries from this file so that we know the total number of different countries the users originate from. For this purpose.","We can use the unique command and provide the file name to get unique entries from. But check that out when we look at the output that our command generated, we can still see some duplicates. But why all that the unique command does is remove repeating lines that are next to each other. In this case, it removed the two lines that mention Canada at the end, but it didn't remove the duplicates that aren't right next to each other. So how do we get all similar lines to be adjacent with the sort utility?","This sorts the entries in the file alphanumerically, which means countries with the same name will be next to each other. Next, we can pipe the output of the sort command to the unique utility. We'll learn more about piping output in a later lesson. Piping simply passes the output of one command to another command. This command will finally give us the result we were looking for.","Now imagine that an upgrade changed an old configuration file, the old configuration file had 250 lines of text. The new one has 268 lines. We could open them both and analyze it line by line to see what changed. But it would be hard to do this. And that's why we have tools that can analyze files for us and quickly show the differences.","Imagine the files on the right lines, one and four differ but lines two and three are identical in both files with the diff tool. We can see the differences between these files with a command like this. And we'd see output like this. Since it focuses on differences, the identical lines are not shown here, but we see some useful output. One.","C one tells us that line one from file one is changed. That's the C in line one from file two. Now, otherwise line one in file one is different from line one and file two. Now, the less than sign tells us that this content exists in the first file, the file at the left, the greater than sign tells us that this content exists in the second file, the file at the right now in large files, we might have no idea where these lines are situated. So we can tell diff to give us a little bit of context.","This shows us what other text is around these areas and where the lines are different to show context. We use the C option. We'll see lines that differ marked with an exclamation point, A plus or a minus sign. The identical lines won't be marked in any way. The exclamation point signals, the difference between lines while plus or minus signals content that was added or removed.","And probably the easiest way to spot differences is by doing a side by side comparison with the Y option, an equivalent command that is easier to remember is Sdiff file one file two, think of the expression side by side diff and you'll remember the Sdiff command. We'll see the differences marked with this pipe symbol."],"Essential Commands/11":["In this demo, we'll be looking at pagers and the Vim text editor. Now, pagers are programs that allow you to open multiple pages of text and navigate through them while on the terminal and the two pagers that we'll be looking at are called less and more. The first one we'll look at is less and it has a little bit more features than more. So now we've opened the file in a pager. Here's how you can tell you're in a pager.","Sometimes other programs will put you into a pager. So this is good to know one way to tell is that at the bottom left corner right here, we have the file name highlighted. But with the less pager, you can use the arrow keys to move up and down through the file. And you also have things like search functions. For example, to search for text, you can press the slash key and then you'll see that down in the bottom left hand corner, enter the term you'd like to search for, I'll search for system and this will search for it and show every time it's mentioned.","And you can see here it's highlighted where it occurs to get to the next instance on a line where it would occur, you press the end key and that will continue to move through each new instance of the search term. But you'll notice it's skipping some of them. And that's because it's case sensitive to make it case, insensitive, you need to pass in the dash I option to ignore case and then they will start getting all instances of system and to move backwards through the previous results, you would hold the shift key and press N. So A capital N will move upward through the previous results. And lastly to exit the pager, you need to press Q to quit.","So that's a brief overview of the less pager. Now more doesn't have as many features but you access it the same way type in more and then the file you want to read and you'll see now that you're in more because it will let you know down at the bottom, it will say more with a percentage and with more, you move through things by pressing the space bar and that moves at a page at a time and you can see the percentage of the file that you're going through and to get out of more, you press the Q key so thats it for pagers. Next, we'll move on to the Vim text editor and Vim stands for V I improved. So when you first open up Vim, it'll give you this nice little greeting screen and it shows you a couple of things that you can do with it and a few of the commands you can have now Vim is mode sensitive. So it has different modes to be in and different keys and functions that are available depending on the mode you're in.","In order to write text into a file, you need to press the I key to go into insert mode and you'll notice down at the bottom left hand corner, it now says insert and you can start typing text. So we have this as our text for our Vim demo. And I'll put a couple of blank lines here, right? There are some things you can do in insert mode. You press the escape key to go back to the default mode or command mode.","Let's look at how we can create or edit files with them. I want to create a new file called test dot text. So we type in Vim test dot text and we'll create a new file. Now, if I want to go and save the contents of this file, I'll press escape to go back to command mode and type colon W to write to the file. And one thing that folks like to joke about on the internet is how to exit Vim how to close the program.","Well, here's how you do that press escape, then colon WQ or write and quit and it wrote to our new text file and here's another useful tip. Let's open our file again. Let's say we put in a bunch of stuff in here and we decide that, you know, we don't want to save it. Let's just start over again. Well, you can quit them without writing type in escape cue with an exclamation point.","And then we'll quit the file without saving any changes to open an existing file. You type in Vim and then the file name, here's a text that we created earlier. And in this file, you can navigate with your arrow keys or you can press L to move right, H to move left K to move up and J to move down. So there's a couple different ways to navigate and you don't need a mouse now to search for something. You use the slash key just like you did in less and you do this in default mode.","So I'll search for this and it doesn't find much because that's case sensitive. If you want it to be case insensitive, you can do this by following it with an escape character. So what we're doing is putting in our search term, then we put a slash the other way and put the letter C think of C for case and that'll make it case insensitive. And there we go, here's how you can go to a specific line number by typing in a colon followed by the line number great. And now I want to go back up to line three.","Awesome to copy an entire line of text. When you're in command mode, you can hit the Y key two times, think of Y like Yank. So we've copied this line and then to paste that line of text, you press in P while you're still in command mode and it will paste that wherever you want it, you can press P as many times as you want. You can also cut a line of text by using the D key two times. So press DD and that will cut it.","And then you can also paste things from anything you have cut. So these are just some of the useful commands that you can use in Vim. So that is a brief overview of the functions of less, more and Vim. And I'll see you in the next lecture."],"Essential Commands/12":["In this lesson, we'll learn how to search for specific text with the grep utility. Sometimes we have to deal with very large files that contain thousands and thousands of lines of text and we need a way to filter out and find the information. We need one important Linux command that lets you search through text files is grep. Let's see how it works. Now, the general syntax we use with grep is grep options, search pattern and file.","It's useful to make it a habit to wrap the search pattern between the single quotes to prevent bash from interpreting special characters. Because in complex searches, we might have characters like the asterisk. And when we'd execute those commands, we'd run into an expected behavior. If we forget to wrap it between the single quotes, we can omit command-line options, but we must specify a search pattern and a file that we want to search for. For example, to search for any line mentioning the word password in the configuration file of the SSH daemon, we can run this command search is case sensitive.","So we'll only find occurrences of the word password with all lowercase characters to find lines that contain the word password with a capital P, we could run this instead and now we'll see different outputs since different lines match our search. But in many situations, we'll see case sensitive searches are counterproductive because they give us partial results. For example, imagine we want to find any line that mentions anything about password configuration. We don't care if the word appears in the text file with a capital P or a lowercase P. So, grep has a useful option that tells it to ignore if the text has lowercase or uppercase letters, it will match every line that contains that word no matter what the case of the letters.","So here we'll use the dash. I ignore case option. Now, instead of searching through a specific file, we can also search through all files that exist under a directory and its subdirectories to do this, we add the dash R as in recursive option. And instead of specifying the path to the file, we specify the path to a directory. Now by default, grep uses different colors to highlight important stuff and make the output easier to scan through.","In this case, the matched files are also highlighted. So we can see at a glance where this text was found. Also note the lines that mention permission denied. We'll get to those soon. We can group the recursive search option with the dash.","I ignore case option. We can also invert search results. For example, we can search for lines that don't contain the text password by adding the dash V option. Now consider this command, this matches two words, password singular, but also passwords plural, simple. Because the plural form contains the same word inside, so to speak.","This matches two words, password, singular, but also passwords plural, simple because the plural form contains the word inside, so to speak, but just hasn't added letter S at the end. But what if we're only interested in matching the word password specifically? We can pass the dash W word option to grep. Now we'll notice an unexpected thing. We have just two lines that match the search expression.","Now, why is that, for example, why does the line password authentication? Yes, not appear in the results here because password authentication as far as grep is concerned, is a different word just like the word book does not mean the same thing for us as the word booking. So when we use the dash W word option, grep will only match the exact word without any extra letters before it or after it. Although as we can see here, the word can have a minus sign before it or a period which ends a sentence, any punctuation sign basically but not a letter. Throughout these examples, we can notice how grep displays the entire line that was matched.","This is useful as it displays the entire context showing us where these matched characters or words have been found. But sometimes we only want to extract the results themselves, not caring about the rest of the line, we can tell grep to do this by passing the dash O only matching option. We can see that as an output to simply look at this isn't very useful as humans. But it can be useful in tasks that automatically extract and process data. Also, when we use this with more complex search patterns, it becomes more useful as we won't see the same word repeated in the results list but rather different results.","For example, we can have a search pattern that shows us what settings are enabled for an application. We'd see the setting names that are enabled. But without the rest of the content on the line, since that would be of no importance in this case."],"Essential Commands/13":["In this lesson, we'll explore regular expressions also called regex for short. Now, in our previous commands, we use simple search patterns looking for some specific pieces of text like password. But what if we need more complex search conditions? Imagine we have some application code scattered in hundreds of files and we need to extract all the IP addresses that are used in this app that would require more advanced search instructions because an IP has a form like 203.102 0.3 0.5. But we can't just make a search pattern look for numbers with a period between them as this would also match numbers like 5.23 which are not IP addresses.","Now, in math, we can say something like X is an integer and X is bigger than three X is smaller than eight. And this would mean that X is either 456 or seven. Now, regular expressions work in a similar way. We can specify some conditions, tie them all together and our search pattern only matches what perfectly fits within those conditions. Let's start out with some super simple examples and then we'll build up to slightly more advanced expressions.","All regular expressions are built with the help of operators like carrot, dollar sign, period, asterisk plus sign braces, question mark, vertical pipe brackets, parentheses, square brackets with Carrot. Now, let's see what each of them does. Now, in Linux configuration files can have lines that begin with a pound sign. Now, these are called commented lines, they're inactive and they're comments. The program looking for settings in the file will ignore all lines that begin with a pound sign.","But comments are useful for humans as they let us see examples of config settings in that file descriptions for what they do, things like that without interfering with the program that reads them. And this means that we can search for commented lines specifically by creating a regular expression that looks for all lines that begin with a pound sign. But this doesn't seem to be terribly useful. We just see the commented lines that we see above. However, combined with Grep's option to invert results, it becomes useful by inverting.","We tell grep to show us lines that don't begin with a pound sign such as grep dash V carrot pound sign at Z login depths. And now we can see exactly what we wanted settings actively used. Now imagine how useful this would be in a very long file with hundreds of comments that make it hard to spot what we're looking for. And to show another example, we could look for lines that start exactly with these four letters pass to do that, we proceed our search pattern with the carrot sign as in grep Carrot passed. Now, let's imagine a different scenario.","Let's say we need to change a setting that is currently set to seven days. Now, it's easy enough, we could look for a seven, right? But this shows us some stuff we don't need. However, we know that our file uses this syntax variable name followed by a space, then a variable value, the variable value is last, which means that if some variable is set to have a value of seven, this number will be the last character on that line by using $7 sign. We also added the dash W match word option to grep.","Otherwise another line would also match one that has multiple sevens at the end. But with dash W, we essentially told grep to match only what contains a single seven digit at the end to look for all lines that end with the text mail, we would do mail dollar sign. Now take note of how these operators are placed differently. If you mix up their location, you won't get any results which can lead to confusion like why your regex isn't working to easily remember the locations. Think about it like this.","The line begins with operator, the carrot should be placed at the beginning of your search pattern. The line ends with operator, the dollar sign goes at the end of your search pattern. Now, anywhere you put a dot or a period in your expression, it will match any character in that spot. For example, C dot T will match cat cut C RT and even C one T or C pound T, but it won't match CT. There must be exactly one character between C and T with C dot dot T.","It will only match what has two characters in the middle. And we can see here that even execute is a match because that sequence fits inside the word. Now, if we only want to match whole words with this red X and not parts of bigger words, we can once again use Greps dash W option and this brings us to an interesting problem. Now, the period or dot has a special meaning in reject. But what if we need to search for an actual period in our text?","This won't work because this red X will basically match each character one by one. The solution however, is simple. We look for a regular period by escaping. This escaping is how we tell our grep command. Hey, don't consider this period a match for any one character operator instead treat it as a period that to escape some special character, we just add a backslash before it.","So instead of the period, we write backslash period and this is our grep command. Now an expression like let Asterisk Wool match le let le tt and so on, no matter how many T letters are at the end. Another way of saying this is that the asterisk allows the previous element to be omitted entirely, appear once or appear two or more times. And here's how we'd use it in a grep command. The asterisk operator can be paired up with other operators, for example, to look for something for sequences that begin with a front slash and have zero or more characters between then end with another front slash.","We could use this since the period matches any one character. And it says the previous element can exist 012 or many more times. We basically allow any sequence of characters to exist between those two front slashes. Now let's say we want to find all sequences of characters where zero appears one or more times, we might be tempted to use something like this. But this also matches lines that contain no zeros at all.","And why is that because the asterisk lets the previous character exist one or more times, but also zero times, it basically allows that element to be optional in our search. So we need another operator that forces the element to exist at least one time or many more. And the plus sign does that for us? Now zero plus would find strings like zero, 00000 and so on, we might think we can use this regular expression and grep like this. But this doesn't look like the result we want our plus sign works like a literal plus sign instead of an operator.","Why is that? Because by default grep uses basic regular expressions and its manual page has this to say in basic regular expressions, the metacharacters lose their special meaning. Instead use the backslashed versions of them. OK? So that means to use a plus operator here, we have to add a backslash before it to make it backslash plus sign and our command becomes this, but this can become confusing fast, right?","So we already saw something like backslash to turn the period operator into a regular period. Now we're using the backslash to turn a regular plus sign into the plus sign operator. It's really hard to keep track of what to backslash and what not to backslash. So we can pick an easier route and use extended red X instead which doesn't require us to backslash any of these characters. And we'll explore that in our next lesson."],"Essential Commands/14":["In this lesson, we'll explore extended regular expression. Now, in a previous lesson, we mentioned how we can escape special characters and grep but sometimes escaping turns a period operator into the regular period character. Other times it works in reverse, escaping a plus sign turns it from a regular plus sign into the plus sign operator. So to avoid confusion, we can simply use extended regular expressions which won't require us to remember what characters need to be escaped for, to consider them for regex operators. Now we'll only need to escape characters when we want them to turn from operators into regular characters that we want to look for like a plus sign or a period.","We use extended red X by adding the dash E option to grep. That's E with a capital letter. Now to find all lines that contain the digit, 01 or more times we can use something like this or even easier. We can use the equivalent EGREP command using EGREP is the same as typing grep dash E. So you can make it a habit to always use EGREP instead of grep to avoid mistakes where you might forget to escape a regex operator with a backslash to find all strings that contain at least three zeros.","We can use the brackets operator within, we specify two values separated by a comma. The first value specifies the minimum amount of repetitions for the previous element. And the second value specifies the maximum amount of repetitions. Now, in our case, we need to specify the minimum amount of three repetitions. But since we don't want to place a limit on the maximum amount of repetitions, we can omit the second value.","So our command looks like this to find all strings that contain one, followed by most three zeros. First of all, we add the first one into our search pattern, then we follow that up with the zero digit and add a regular expression. This time in reverse, we omit to specify a minimum in the first field, but we specify our maximum number of repetitions in the second field. Not, this will also match ones followed by no zeros. Since we didn't choose any specific minimum amount of repetitions.","And to find all strings that contain exactly three zeros, we use the operator in a different way instead of two fields with a minimum and a maximum, we just place one value between our curly brackets. Now the question mark character will let the previous element exist precisely zero or one times. And this basically makes it optional. It can exist once or not at all. Now, let's say we're trying to find all text that says disabled or disable.","This means that the last D is optional so we can write an expression like this in ree that this also matches the word disables. This is a case where the letter D did not come at the end and disable still matches because it's part of the word. But just like before, we can add the dash W word option to grep to get rid of that. So only disable or disabled specifically match if we'd want to match, enabled or disabled, we could use the or operator. This basically matches what it finds on its left side or its right side.","And we could combine this with our previous trick where we made the last D letter optional to also find variations like enable, enabled, disable disabled. We'll also make the search case insensitive to also match variations where the words have upper case letters in some spots. Now it's time to see how we can put all this knowledge to use and combine multiple regex operators to fine tune our searches. But first, let's learn about ranges and sets. Now, a range is specified in the form of brackets A through Z.","Like here, this will match any one lowercase letter from AB CD, et cetera. All the way to Z and brackets zero through nine will match any one digit from 0 to 9. And A set is specified in this form AB Z 954 will match any one character specified with an AB Z 95 or four. So to find all the strings that contain the text cat or cut, we do something like this with ranges and sets, we can make our searches both wide and specific. Even at the same time.","For example, let's ask ourselves, how would we find all config files that mention special device files that I have with names like slash DEV slash SD A one or similar. We could think like this, find all the strings that contain slash DEV slash, followed by any random characters. But this matches some weird stuff dot asterisk is greedy matching and it matches way too many things after it captures the slash DEV part that we're looking for. So we need to make our search wide enough to catch all the DEV device names, but specific enough to only capture the parts we need. Now, we can do this with ranges, we can say after slash DEV match any number of lowercase letters from A to Z.","And as we learned, we can do that with the asterisk operator. In this case, the asterisk operator will apply to the set specified before it A through Z. Now the output looks a little better, but we still see some things are missed slash DEV slash TW A is matched instead of the entire slash DEV slash TW A zero. How can we catch the digits at the end too easy, we specify that a digit from 0 to 9 should exist there. But now we run into another problem.","Only things that have a digit at the end are matched with this new red X. We'll only find slash DEV, slash SD A one but not slash DEV slash SD A. This is an easy fix. Once again, we just make the digit at the end optional with the question mark operator. And this looks much better.","Now, now let's talk about subexpressions in math. We can see this. Now, this is 1.6 equals seven. That's because first multiplication will be done between two and three and only after that the addition. But what if we first wanna add one plus two and then multiply by three, we would write this.","Now first, the addition between one and two will be done and only afterwards the multiplication, this will be three times three, equaling nine in Rx. We do a very similar thing. Let's take a look at our last expression. Now, if we scroll up in our output, we can see we still don't match everything we need. Now, in this line P zero is left out.","Why is that? Because our expression after it finds slash DEV matches any number of A to Z characters and then a digit at the end and that's it. And that's where the match ends. So in slash DEV slash TT Y zero P zero after that first zero is hit. Our regex is happy with the partial result.","So how can we correct this? We could tell it that after slash DEV, we have some letters and a digit at the end. But after that, the same thing can repeat 012 or three or more times. There can be other sequences of letters followed by a digit. This way, slash DEV slash TT Y zero would match first and then P zero would be added to this match by that repetition.","So we basically want to say that this part of the regex should look for this pattern, existing 0123 or many more times so that it can match things like TT Y zero P zero. Now what makes red X look for something that exists zero or more times the asterisk operator. But if we add it to the end, we get something like this and this isn't as good as the star would apply to the previous element only. And our asterisk would only be placed right after the question mark. However, we want to apply our asterisk operator to our whole construct here.","Again, easy solution. We just wrap our construct in parentheses. And this way asterisk will apply to our entire subexpression wrapped in parentheses instead of the last element only. Now, we can tell our expression to look for lowercase letters or uppercase with the or operator. But writing it like this would be a mistake because now the asterisk operator would only apply to capital A through Z and we need it to apply to our entire A through Z construct.","Once again, we can wrap in parentheses and make it a subexpression and fix this and we end up with this command. Now, tt Ys zero matches and if we would go on, we could fix things like slash DEV slash term slash A not matching because our regex stops when it encounters the next front slash and so on. This is the kind of logic and fine tuning. We would go through when fixing our regular expressions or making them laser focused on what we need to find. We saw sets are in the form of AB C 123 and ranges A through Z.","If we add a carrot operator in there, we can negate them that tells redex that the elements in this set of range should not exist at this position. Now imagine we want to search for the text, HTTPS, but we don't want to find links to websites since a link is usually in the form of HTTPS colon sign, we can build a red X like this. It's basically saying look for the HTTPS string, but make sure it's not followed by a colon sign. And we'd end up with this grep command to find the string, HTTP and not followed by the S letter or the colon character. We just negate the set otherwise said we simply enumerate what we want to negate.","Now, up to this point, we use the set with only one or two characters. But as mentioned, we can negate ranges as well. For example, we could tell our pattern after a front slash. There should not be any lowercase letter. Now keep in mind for any pattern you're trying to match.","There are multiple regex solutions you may find to get this right. You should practice until you feel comfortable with regular expressions. It's also worth noting that regex is not limited to grep, you can use regular expressions in a lot of programs that deal with search patterns. For example, the said utility also supports regular expressions if you want to try out different regular expressions and see their effects. Websites like Reg exr.com can be very helpful."],"Essential Commands/19":["In this lesson, we'll take a look at SSL certificates, what they are used for and how we can create and inspect them. But first a clarification, what we called SSL in the past is nowadays actually TLS, but they're often still referenced with the old name that doesn't apply anymore. Let's explore the terminology. So SSL stands for secure sockets layer and TLS stands for transport layer security. So why do we still use the wrong old name because SSL was used for a very long time and the name kind of stuck around in various tools and documentation even after most certificates migrated to TLS.","So why do we still use the wrong old name because SSL was used for a very long time and the name kind of stuck around in various tools and documentation even after most certificates migrated TLS. Now we can think of TLS as an upgrade over SSL. SSL had many security issues and TLS closed a lot of those security holes. Now with this clarification out of the way, let's get to our next topic. What are these certificates used for?","What are SSL certificates? Well, let's think about what happens when we use a password on a website or when we enter credit card details or anything like that. There are two big problems. One, how can we be sure that we are sending this data to the legitimate website and not some clone created by a malicious hacker. And two, how can we be sure that no one can steal these details as they're being sent through the networks?","Well, certificates solve both of these issues. They authenticate the website and they encrypt network traffic between the user and the website authentication means that the website can cryptographically prove that a browser is legitimate. It proves it's actually code cloud.com and not some clone. And the certificate is used also to make the connection private between the user and code cloud.com. All data exchanged within the website is encrypted.","Now, let's see how we can actually create such certificates. Now, the utility that's normally used on Linux to create managed TLS certificates is called OpenSSL. And we can already notice an area where the old SSL name is still hanging around from the old days while it's called OpenSSL, we'll actually create TLS certificates with it. If we consult the manual of OpenSSL with the man OpenSSL command, we'll see that the documentation points out. The utility can be used for a lot more than just certificates.","It can deal with many Cryptography related operations. But what interests us in this lesson is the creation of X 0.509 certificates. These are the certificates that we can use on websites to do the authentication and encryption that we mentioned before. Unfortunately, the OpenSSL command supports a very large number of subcommands and options and it could be hard to remember what we actually need to type in for a specific task. But the good thing is we can use a few tricks to orient ourselves.","Let's learn how to do that right on the command-line. First, to get an idea about the subcommands supported, we can type openssl oh or simply OpenSSL. This will display the following output. Let's pay attention to the first section titled standard commands. Notice the wreck and X 509 subcommands.","These will be the focus of our lesson. Now since openssl is a complex tool, it has multiple manuals, one manual for every important subcommand that we see here to see the manuals available. We can type in man, OpenSSL and press tab twice. And we'll see this. If we want to inspect the rex subcommand, we can type in man OpenSSL rec.","Another way we can do this is man OpenSSL dash R and it will show us the same thing. We'll see that commands to generate certificates can get quite complex as a beginner. It can be quite hard to get started just by looking at this manual and all these options that the R subcommand supports. However, we can get a few very useful tips here. If we press slash and type in example, in all capital letters, it'll help us find the example section where we can learn how to get started.","Note these two commands here as we'll actually adapt these in our demonstration to generate a certificate signing request and create a self signed certificate. Now, let's press Q to quit this manual. The same procedure can be followed to examine the openssl X 509 commands. Let's quickly explore certificate signing requests. The R subcommand deals with certificate signing requests.","That's because the digital certificates used to secure website traffic are not enough on their own. Example.com can use a certificate to secure web traffic between a user and a website that's no issue. But when a user visits example.com, their browser also needs to trust the certificate. It needs a way to be sure that the certificate is legitimate, the real deal and not some fake created by a malicious hacker. So how can it be sure by checking if something called a certificate, authority signed example.com certificate, so we can send our certificate that we generate locally to some company like Google, then they use a special private key and sign our certificate.","Once that certificate is signed, any browser can then check the signature and notice that indeed Google validated it so it's legitimate. And now let's see how to generate two things. A private key and the certificate signing request certificates are normally used in conjunction with such a secret key, we'll generate both of these things with a single command. The one we actually saw on Emanuel earlier dash new key, RS A colon 2048 is the option that lets us instruct OpenSSL to generate a key of this type RS A on 2048 bits dash key out key dot pim tells the utility to save the private key in a file called key dot pe M and dash out RQ dot pe saves our certificate signing, request the CS R to a file called Rec dot Pim. Now we'll get a few questions.","First, we have to pick a password to protect and encrypt our private key file. Next, we'll be asked a few details which will be included in the signing request. We're basically telling the certificate authority what we want to sign a certificate for a company called code cloud with its location in New York and so on. We'll explain these properties in our next example, ok? And we'll see that they were created.","And if we type in cat, he dot P, we can see the content of the key file. It's base64 encoded, so it won't make any sense. But at least the first and last lines tell us that this is a private key and it's encrypted. If we type in CREQ dot pe M for re, we can also see the base64 encoded CS R we generated. So it's useful that if we look at these files, we can at least know what they represent in the real world.","We would send the CS R file to a certificate authority. They would check and verify our details. We would prove our identity, pay a fee and get a certificate. Here's how we can generate a self signed certificate. When we want the whole world to be able to validate and trust our certificate.","We need the services of a certificate authority. But other scenarios exist. For example, we might only want to use the certificate internally in our office network and we can configure all the computers to trust all of our local certificates. In this case, we can do what's called self signing a certificate. So we can skip generating a CS R and sending it to an authority.","We can generate the final signed certificate ourselves to generate our self signed TLS certificate. We'll use a few extra options in the command just to show an example of how we can further customize our certificate. These are the options. We'll use dash X 5 0 9 dash no A and C new key RS A 4096 days 365 key out my private dot key and dash out my certificate dot C RT. Now let's take a look at what all of these mean dash X 509 tells the command to generate an X 0.509 type of certificate instead of creating a certificate, signing request, OpenSSL REQ on its own.","Without this option would simply create a request and not generate the certificate dash no enc tells OpenSSL not to ask us for a password to encrypt this key. In a real scenario. This isn't recommended. But for experimental purposes, we don't need a password dash, new key RS A colon 4096 tells OpenSSL to use the RS A algorithm for this key and use 4096 bits for it. The current default is RS A 2048.","But we wanted to show you how you can pick a different algorithm or a bit number. If you need to dash days, 365 makes a certificate valid for 365 days after which it's considered expired dash key out. My private dot key saves the private key to a file called my private dot key. Dash out my certificate dot C RT saves the certificate to a file named my certificate dot C RT. In conclusion to sign and create our certificate in one go.","We use this command after the command generates some cryptographic secrets. We get a few prompts. Now let's explain these prompts as promised. Now, if we press enter the default choice shown between square brackets will be picked. For example, at the first prompt here.","If we press enter, then A U will be picked as a country code. But if we want to pick the United States instead, we would type us here. Same goes for all the other prompts for a website. The organization name and common name are important. The organization name can be the name of the company and the common name should include the address of our website.","For example, code cloud.com, all of these values will be stored in a certificate, but they will usually be abbreviated when we display the content of a certificate. For example, CN will stand for common name on for organization name and ou for organizational unit. In fact, if we try to take a look at the text file itself like we did before with cat my certificate, that cat, we will see the same base64 encoded content. But this time, there's an important difference. It now mentions that this is actually a certificate, not a certificate request.","So we finally have our self signed final certificate. But how can we check details about it since this encoded text is not very helpful since we are dealing with an already existing certificate. This time, we can use the X 509 subcommand. Since its purpose is to interact with X 509 certificates, we can get a little bit of help about the X 509 command by throwing in the dash help option. It seems that text will make the command, print the certificate in text form.","That sounds like what we need. The next option we need to throw in is the dash in option which tells OpenSSL what input file it should look at. So we'll type in openssl X 5 0 9 dash N my certificate dot CRT and then text. This will output a lot of text. But if we scroll back up, we'll see the details that interest us.","C which stands for country code is us. The state is Oregon, L meaning location is Gaston O short for organization is code cloud and CN which is a common name is code cloud.com. And if I would have put an email address that would have shown at the end, there are a few extra details that might interest us such as the expiration date under validity. Not before, usually indicates when the certificate was issued and not after is when it will expire. It can be a bit confusing the first few times about how to use the OpenSSL command, especially when to use the R subcommand and when to use the X 509 subcommand.","But you can think about it this way to serve as a quick reminder. If I need a certificate, I need to request something. So I use the rec subcommand. For example, if I need to work with an already existing certificate, what type of certificate is this? Hmm It was X dot something but I forgot, let me get a quick reminder about the subcommand supported with openssl help.","Ah There it is X 509. So I need an X 509 subcommand, which means I can use a command like this to get some details about the certificate. Great. And remember there's always man OpenSSL Wreck and man OPENSSL X 509. And those will help you out, especially in the examples section.","We hope these mental tricks will aid you in your future OpenSSL tasks. Now let's move on to our next lesson."],"Essential Commands/20":["In today's world, software projects are usually developed by large teams and this comes with an interesting set of problems. Imagine we have 10 developers working on the same project. Each person will add delete and modify code in random places. Now imagine you're part of the same team. If you turn on your computer ready to do some work, how will you know what changed in the last 24 hours?","Now, in today's world, software projects are usually developed by large teams and this comes with an interesting set of problems. Imagine we have 10 developers working on the same project. Each person will add delete and modify code in random places. Now imagine you're a part of the same team. Now you turn on your computer ready to do some work.","How will you know what changed in the last 24 hours? Here are some examples of activities that might have happened during this time while the software testing team reported a bug, a developer changed code in six files to fix that bug. Another developer added a new feature, three new files were added and two were modified. Someone else removed a feature that will not be needed in the next version of this project, two files were deleted and one file was modified. That's quite a large number of changes in a short time.","And it's hard to keep track of everything. Developers could inform each other and chat about what they did. But that would quickly become messy, overwhelming each person with information that they need to remember. That's quite a large number of changes in a short time and it's hard to keep track of everything. Now, developers could inform each other and chat about what they did, but that would quickly become messy, overwhelming each person with information that they need to remember.","So when you begin work, you need a quick way to get updated on all of these changes. You'll want to know things like what files were added, removed or modified, what lines of code were changed, who changed those lines and why did they change them? And these are just a few examples in a nutshell. You need to know what the rest of the team did. And when you make your own changes, you need some way to inform the rest of the team about what you changed and why with an efficient method to do this, everyone can work on the same code and always know what happened since the last time they logged in.","So we need a way to track all of these changes and keep things organized and the solution is to use a distributed version control system like git. Now at its core, Git's job is to allow a large group of people to work on the same software project. Let's take it step by step and discover how it works. Now, first of all, in git, we have the concept of repositories. These are the places where code is stored alongside information about each change.","Now, as a part of the team, we usually have two repositories, a local repository and a remote repository. The local repository is personal just for you and the remote repository is in a shared central location used by the entire team. For example, as you code on your own computer, you work with your local repository. And when you're happy with the results, you can upload your latest changes to the remote repository. If you've ever visited github, you already saw a lot of such remote repositories.","Every project you see there is basically a remote git repository by uploading to this remote repository. Each team member can update the project at a central location and by downloading from the remote repository. Each team member can see what the other people have been working on this way. They all get updated with the latest changes. You can think of the remote repository as the place where everyone assembles all the bits and pieces they've been working on.","Every small change from local repositories is gathered and integrated into this remote repository. When you want to update the remote repository with the latest changes you made, you push the latest code from local to remote and when you want to get access to the latest changes your team members made, you do a pull from the remote repository to your local one. We'll see that the commands for these actions are quite intuitive. And the best way to understand, get better is to just see it in action. So let's go through a few practical exercises.","OK. Now we're at our command-line and we're going to work with git. Now git usually comes preinstalled on most modern versions of Linux. However, if we do need to install it, we can type in Getit, config dash dash, global user dot name and then your name and then we set the email address with a similar command, but it's user dot email instead of user dot name. Now, this is not login information, it's just useful information for other team members.","So when we make changes to the software project and make commits, they will see this username and email and they'll know who changed what we use the dash dash global parameter to set this value system wide. This will apply to all local git repositories that we might create on this machine in the future. If we omit the global parameter, we can set the username and email address on a per repository basis. For example, for one repository, we can use a certain email identity. And for another one, we could use a totally different identity.","Now, let's imagine we intend to store our project's code into a directory. We'll call this directory project and let's go into that directory. Now, we need to inform git that this is where our project will reside to tell it to start tracking everything that will happen in this directory. We use this command get in there and a net is short form for initialize, we can see in the output that it created a hidden directory called dot git. Let's take a quick look at the contents in there.","We can see a few files and subdirectories. Now git will store all the repository information here. Everything from repository settings, all the way to data that it will use to track our code changes. Remember the username and email we picked earlier, we set that globally. But if we want to pick a different identity here non globally, we just remove the global parameter.","That information is now stored inside this dot git directory in a file called config, which means that we have an easy and quick way to look at local repository settings. We just have to display the contents of that file. We just type in cat dot getit slash config and we can see the name in the email that we just set. Now let's pretend we begin our coding journey in our project directory. Let's create two files, file one and file two, we'll store a line of text in each file and imagine that these are our first two lines of code later on these lines of text will let us see how git helps us track code changes to each file.","An important note to make here, git does not continuously track every change to our project directory. Instead, every time we make changes, we have to inform Getit that something happened now is a good time to learn how this process works. And we'll find out in our next lesson and I'll see you there."],"Essential Commands/21":["In this lesson, we'll look at how to stage and commit changes with git. Now there are three steps to modifying code and then tracking changes with git, make changes in the working area, add the changes we want to track with git to the staging area and then committing our changes at this point. The first step is done because the working area is the project directory that we're currently in and we already made our changes to the working area when we added our two files and the text inside them. So every time we change something in our project's directory, we've modified our working area and now we get to phase two, the staging area. This is how we tell git about the changes that we made to our project or more specifically what changes we want to track in the next commit.","We might wonder why doesn't git just check the working area and track all the changes automatically? Why do we have to add these changes to the staging area? Well, one answer is that git can't know when we finish making our changes. For example, maybe we added 50 lines of code today in some file, but we intend to add another 50 lines tomorrow to wrap things up. So we don't want git to track this partial change when we're at 50% progress.","We want to wait until we finish adding all our lines of code. Also, if we modify 10 different files, we don't want it to track 10 different phases for each file. Instead, we want to track this as a single change. Even if we modified 10 files, maybe this was just done to add a new feature to our software project. So we want to track this all as a single modification in our project's life cycle, not 10 separate modifications.","Now, let's go back to our demo and see how this works. OK? We're back to our prompt here with two files in our project. It's easy to know what we changed and what we want to stage. But what if our project is very complex?","Imagine we added a new feature and we had to modify 16 files. We might forget what files we changed in the last few hours. However, git has a command that can show us exactly what we modified. This command is git status. Now, we begin to see the awesome power behind git.","It already noticed what changed and it even shows us the next command that we have to use to add these two files to the staging area. We'll type, get a file one file two. Now let's check the git status. Again, we can see that git is prepared for the third step that is committing changes. So basically file one and file two are now in the staging area.","We told git that these are changed files that we want to track. But nothing will be tracked until we actually commit this staging area to git. Staging something in git is like pointing your phone's camera at something you frame the image you want to capture but nothing is captured yet. However, when you click the camera button, a picture is taken and stored in your phone's memory. Git does the same thing.","When you commit to the staging area, it takes a picture of how these staged files looked at a certain point in time in the status above. We can also see how to unstage a file from git. But it's not recommended to use the git RM command as it can have unintended consequences. If we forget to add the dash dash cached parameter, we might accidentally remove the file from the project entirely instead of just removing it from the staging area. So to unstage a file, we can run a command like this, get reset file two, but in this case, everything looks good.","So we will not execute this command. It's just a reminder that we have this option if we ever need it. It's also worth mentioning that some projects are quite complex and we'd waste a ton of time manually typing the names of tens or hundreds of files. So we can use some shortcuts to stage multiple files. Here are a few examples to add only the files that have the HTML extension in the current directory.","We can type something like this and it's important to use the quotes in this case to prevent our bash shell from expanding the star asterisk character and lead to some unexpected results. Also git can stage entire subdirectories. For example, imagine we wanted to add a subdirectory called products in our current directory. To tell git to add that to the staging area. We could use something like this, get a product slash, all the files and subdirectories within that directory would be staged as well.","And to add only the files with the HTML extension from the product subdirectory, we would run this, we're using the asterisk character again. So we need to use the quotes as well. For the reason we mentioned earlier, make a mental note to remember to always use quotes when you use the asterisk character. Now, of course, we can also use the same tricks to remove multiple files at once from the staging area. So a command like this git reset product start out HTML is also valid.","Now, we can get to the third and final phase of tracking changes with the help of git committing changes. Remember that many team members are possibly working on your project's code. So we want to help them out. When they're looking at the changes that we made for this purpose. It's standard practice to add a comment for each commit.","In this comment, we briefly explain why we made the changes to add a comment to a commit. We use the dash M command option and dash m is short for message. So we'll run this command to commit our changes to file one and file two. Now, as you can see, we needed to add the message between quotes. Otherwise our bash shell would be confused instead of the multiple words separated by spaces.","It would think that we have multiple command-line arguments for the dash M option. So wrapping our message between quotes tells bash that this is a single command-line argument. So what does git do when committing changes? It takes a look at what we added to our staging area. And as we mentioned before, it makes sort of a snapshot this way, it understands what changed between the last time the project was modified.","And this time it's what makes stuff like this possible. This is an example from the github page for the Linux kernel after a git commit was made in a local repository and then uploaded to this remote repository. Now, other people can see exactly what changed and we see a list of files that were modified, how many lines were added and how many were deleted. And we can also notice that the change lines are highlighted, red highlights show what was removed or the old content and the green highlight shows us what was added or the new content. This makes it easy for other team members to check out the latest code changes.","Now in our case, git will notice that our project went from an empty project directory to a project directory with two files containing lines of text. So as a reminder, tracking changes with git consists of three main steps, we make changes to our working area, which is simply the directory where we store our project locally. Step two, we tell git about the changes we want to track by adding files and directories to the staging area. And in step three, when we prepared everything, we tell git to commit all of these changes that we've added to the staging area. In other words, we tell it to track this exact state of the project so that it can remember all the states or commits that this project went through.","It's worth mentioning that we've covered just one type of changes to a project, adding new files, but there are other types of changes as well. For example, sometimes a project can simply have unnecessary files deleted. Let's go through this scenario too. First, we'll create a third file with this text and we'll add it to the staging area. Next, we'll commit this with a change to getit.","Now, at this point, we can imagine noticing that this feature is buggy and it isn't worth fixing it. So we decided to just remove it from the project entirely. Now, if we look at the current state of the project, we see that we have three files to tell git to remove this file from the project, we can use this command, get RM file three. This does two things. It removes the file from the repository or the so called working area.","And we can see this confirmed if we list our current directory. But the git RM command also stages this change for the future commit. So it stages the removal of this file from the project and we can see this with git status and this change is ready to be accepted. So let's commit it with this command. Now, we saw two types of changes that we can track with git, adding new files and removing files from the project.","There's also another type of change, modifying old files, which simply means that git can track what lines of code we change in our files. And this gives us the opportunity to check out another git feature branches and I'll see you in the next lesson."],"Essential Commands/22":["In this lesson, we'll talk about get branches while checking, get status and making commits. We can notice a line like this in the output on branch master. So what are these branches? Well, here's one example, our project can have multiple versions. So maybe we shipped out version 1.0 to our users.","And a part of the team is constantly fixing bugs in version 1.0 So they'll work on the 1.0 branch. But at the same time, we also intend to build new features into our software. So another part of the team is working on version 1.1 on an entirely different branch. This way people can work on two versions, two branches without interfering with each other. And git will track each branch separately.","We can think of branches as different development roads. They can go in similar but slightly different directions and from time to time they can be reunited, which is called merging in git terminology. And we'll see how that works later on the master branch is the default one that git works with. We might use this to track our stable version of our software project. And the one that's actually shipped out to the public, it's up to us what we do with it.","Now, let's imagine that our master branch tracks the 1.0 version, it contains file one and file two and things are looking good. But we want to prepare a new and improved version 1.1. So we start improving our code and we don't want to make changes directly to our master branch. Instead, we wanna create a new branch. We won't even call the branch 1.1.","We'll call it 1.1 testing. This tells our entire team that this 1.1 branch is not even final yet. We're just modifying lines of code and we intend to test the new software before releasing it to the public. But seeing this in practice is easier to understand. So let's dive right into how to use git branches at the command-line.","Now we're back at our terminal window to add our new branch. We'll use this command. Now, if you ever need to delete a branch, you can use this command delete, get branch, dash dash, delete and then the name of the branch to see all available branches, we can type in yet branch dash dash list or simply get branch. Now we notice here that we're currently still inside the master branch. The asterisk symbol shows us the one that is currently selected.","But remember we want to add new features and track them in the 1.1 dash testing branch, not the master one to switch to the 1.1 dash testing branch. We'll run this command. Great. Now it's time to start work on the 1.1 version. Let's open file two in an editor.","We'll press I to start inserting text and we'll make the following modifications. Now, we have changed our line two. This is the improved line of code in file two. I will press escape colon WQ to write and quit. And now if we enter get status, we'll see that.","Git already noticed that file two was modified. We're also offered a useful suggestion here. The git commit a command, meaning we could skip the whole git a file two command again and jump straight to the commit with a command like this getit commit, dash a dash M some message here. But to build healthy habits for now and avoid any future mistakes as beginners. Let's go through the usual routine.","We'll add file two to the staging area and then we'll commit the change. Awesome. We're in branch 1.1 dash testing. So every developer in our team checking this out will see the latest changes here. The master branch is basically unmodified, still containing our clean well tested code that we had previously.","Now up to this point, we did a lot of things in this git repository. Next week, we might not even remember all these changes. We made. But when the multiple team members are involved, it's even harder to know what people modified in the past. That's why we have git and one command we can use to get a glance at past activity is get log.","Now, we can truly see how awesome git is. This is full of details. As a developer, we can quickly see what other team members did in this project. Additionally, we can uncover even more details. For example, maybe we want to see what files were actually changed.","So we can type in git log dash dash raw. Notice this output these lines tell us that the commit added two files, file one and file two. That's what the A stands for D shows us files that are deleted in a commit and M signals modified files. Also notice this weird text next to each commit. This is called the commit hash and it uniquely identifies each entry if we want to dig deeper and see what lines of code were actually changed in this commit.","We can use the first few characters of this hash. For example, we can run git show and the first few characters of that. Hash. Additionally, we can uncover even more details. For example, maybe we want to see what files were actually changed so we can run glog dash dash raw.","Notice this output, this line tells us that we added a file, file three and that's what the A stands for. D shows deleted files in a commit and M signals modified files. Now also notice this weird text next to each commit. This is called the commit hash. And it uniquely identifies each entry.","If we want to dig deeper and see what lines of code were actually changed. In this commit, we can use the first few characters of this. Hash. For example, I'll copy some of these first characters, you will type in git show and those characters. Awesome.","Now, the red text preceded by the minus sign shows us old content that was removed and green text preceded by a plus sign shows new content that was added. So we can see the modification that we made in this commit when we edited that text in the file. If we list commits again, we notice a few things. One is that the master branch is one commit behind since these are sorted from bottom to top, the newest ones at the top and the oldest is at the bottom. So when we improved our code, we committed the change only in the 1.1 dash testing branch.","So this is expected the master branch should not contain that commit yet. The second thing is a mysterious word that says head, what does that tell us? Well, it's easier to understand if we go through a few commands. For example, right now, head points to the commit where we improved code in file two. Now, if we take a look at file two.","We can see that we have the approved line of code in it. But now let's switch back to the master branch and we'll check the log again. We'll spot a couple of differences here. First. Now we only see three commits instead of four.","As we saw previously, the last commit here is when we remove the buggy feature, the commit where we improved, our code is not present. This makes sense because we didn't commit that change in the master branch only in the 1.1 dash testing branch. But now notice the head is pointing to this commit where we removed a buggy feature. Previously, it pointed to the commit where we improved code. So let's check out file two again.","Interesting. Now it shows us the old content of the file. What's the explanation? Well, head currently points to an older commit when we removed our buggy feature in that commit file two had this original content that we see here. So head points to a certain commit, which is not necessarily the last one we did in our project.","And what head points to is the project state that we'll see in our working area by pointing head to a different commit. We can basically travel in time exploring the project state as it was in that commit. So that's another great thing about git. We can essentially explore various stages in the life cycle of our software project. So let's talk about merging branches.","Now, remember how our latest changes in file two are not present in the master branch. In git terminology, we say that the master branches behind our 1.1 testing branch. Now let's imagine we finished improving our code and we tested everything in the 1.1 testing branch time to launch our new and improved software product. But how do we do this? We bring all the changes from the 1.1 testing branch into our master branch and this is called merging branches.","We'll merge a secondary branch into our current branch. We want to merge into master. So let's make sure that that's our current branch. Perfect to merge 1.1 dash testing into our current master branch, we run this command and if we take a look at file two, again, we can see that our master branch finally includes all the latest code changes brought in from the 1.1 testing branch. Now let's get to one of the most important features of git.","Let's talk about working with remote repositories. Now, up until this point, we worked with our local repository only. But as a part of a large team, we'll want to periodically make our code changes available to everybody else. The easiest way to do this is to upload our progress from the local repository to a remote repository. Now, if you've ever used github or gitlab, you already saw two examples of platforms that can host remote repositories.","But uploading our changes to a remote repository is only half the story. Other team members are also working on this software project. So we'll want to be able to see what changes they've made, which brings us to the next git concepts. When we upload our changes to a remote repository, this is called a push operation. When we download changes, it's called a pole operation.","But what we're actually downloading and uploading are all the commits that git knows about. So we either pull in all the available commits from the remote repository or we push all of our commits to the remote repo. Either way this keeps the whole team synchronized with the latest code changes. Let's see how we can push our changes to a remote repository. Since github is the most popular platform for remote git repositories.","Let's see the steps that we need to go through here. After we log into our account, we'll head over to the repositories tab. Next we press the new button to create a new repository. We'll pick a name for it and I'm gonna call this KK Pro and we'll leave the rest of the options with their default settings. Now, the next important part is to get to our connection strings, which we can see here.","We can see that the HTTPS protocol is selected by default. When we intend to make changes to the remote repository, we must prove to github that we're allowed to do that. If we use the HTTPS protocol, we have certain login mechanisms. For example, we can use a username and an access token, but we'll click on the SSH button to log in in a different way. It will be easier for our case since we can log in with SSH keys.","Now let's copy the connection string we see here and back to our terminal. Remember how git stores all information about our local repository in the hidden dot git directory. Well, this is also the place where git will store information about any remote repositories. We might have to check if any remote repositories are associated with this repository. We type in git remote dash VV stands for verbose which is detailed output.","Nothing will be output since no remote repos have been configured yet. So let's add our first one with the next command. We will tell git what remote repository it should use when uploading data from this local repository or when downloading data to it. So we're gonna type in git remote add origin and then that connection string that I copied from the website origin is an alias. It's a name that we pick for the remote repository because it's easier to remember and type origin than an entire connection string.","So with the next commands will simply tell git to push or pull from origin and it will know that origin points to that connection string, we could pick any other name we'd like, but it's usually standard practice to pick this name for the default remote repo. Now, we can check again if our local repository is configured to use any remote repositories. These two lines show us that an origin is associated with this particular connection string. It's configured to both fetch or download from the remote repository but also push or upload to it. Now let's configure the SSH keys that will allow us to log into this remote repo, we can use this command to generate a public and private key pair SSH dash key GM.","And we'll press enter at each prompt to pick the default settings. We'll save the private key in its default location and we'll use an empty passphrase. So we don't have to pick any particular password to encrypt our private key. OK. And it was generated.","Now, we'll need the public key, which is specified here in ID underscore ed 25519 dot pub. So let's display its contents and we'll copy this entire text. Now, we'll go back to the github website and here we'll click on the settings tab and we'll select SSH and GPG keys and will click on new SSH key. And here I'll put in a title and we'll paste in the key here. We can see there's a difference between authentication key and signing key and we want the authentication key.","We'll click add SSH key. Now let's return to our terminal. Now, our remote repository is empty. Let's bring it up to date and upload all the commits that we did locally. And we'll do that with the following command and we'll type in.","Yes, here. So with this command, we basically told git to push our local commits to the remote repository named origin. And we also specify what branch to upload. In this case, the one called master. OK.","Now at a glance, it seems that our changes have been uploaded. But let's check, we'll go back to our repositories. If we go back to the github.com website and navigate to our local remote repository, we will see that we now have some content. Now, let's go back to our terminal to pull in the latest commits to our master branch. The command is also quite intuitive.","We'll say git pull origin master. Finally, let's check out another thing. Now, up to this point, we've assumed that all of our team members already have their local repository set up. But what if a new member comes in? They won't have a local repository with this project available.","They basically have to start from zero. So how can they do that easily? Well, with the clone command. So let's imagine that our new team member wants to start working on our project after we set up the SSH keys, upload their public key to the project and so on, we can give them the connection string. After we set up the SSH keys, upload their public key to the project and so on.","We can give them the connection string that you find here. Now, let's put ourselves in their shoes. We'll exit from our current directory and return to our home directory. Let's imagine. They create a directory called work.","This is where they intend to store all the software projects they'll be working on so they switch to this directory finally, with one command, they can quickly get started. The clone command will do everything that's required. It'll create a local repository and this local repo will be preconfigured with project specific settings. For example, the remote origin will be added automatically and all of the project files and commits will be downloaded as well. So the command that they need to enter is get clone and that connection string.","And the result we can see a directory called KK project was created. And if we take a look at it, we see that it contains all of our project files plus the hidden dot git directory which contains the history of commits and all the other data that's tracked by git. And if we switch to this directory, we can use the same commands as before such as get log. So now our team member has the same repository on their computer that all the other team members are working on. The command is a nice quick way to start from zero.","Once the local repository has been created, though, the rest of the workflow is the same as we saw in this demo. This covers all the essential steps that we need to know when working with git. Of course, git is an incredibly complex tool that can do way more than what we just saw. Remember that when needed, you can enter git followed by a space and then press the tab key twice. This quickly shows you all the subcommands that you can use and you can get help for a subcommand by typing man, followed by git and then the name of the subcommand you see in this list, for example, man, get at and we hope this is a fun lesson to go through.","If you're interested in learning more about git, we have an entire course for it here on our website at code cloud.com and you can check it out there. Now let's continue with our next lesson."],"Operations Deployment/3":["We'll now look at how to use scripting to automate system maintenance tasks. Now, when we log into a Linux operating system, we're automatically dropped into a command-line like this. What actually happens is after we successfully log in a program called bash opens up and we are dropped within its text based environment. All the commands we type are interpreted by bash which figures out what needs to happen to execute them. And that's why bash is also called a command interpreter or a shell.","Now, in this case, we're using it interactively, we write something, we press enter, it gets executed and we get a result. But bash can be used in a different way with scripts. Now, scripts are simply files where we can add multiple instructions for our command interpreter. The interpreter can then read this file and execute the instructions in order first, it will execute the instruction on the first line, then the one on the second line and so on. But it's easier to understand how scripts work if we build one.","So let's start experimenting first, we'll create a file called script dot sh. Now standard practice to add the dot sh extension to this file, it's not mandatory, but it makes it easier when you type in LS in a directory to spot which files or scripts. On rare occasions, we might need to skip adding the dot sh extension if we want other programs to execute them. And we'll see an example of this when we learn about scheduling tasks in a later session. Now let's edit script dot sh and add some content.","This first line is very important and we'll write this here pound sign, exclamation point slash bin slash bash. Now to make our script work, this has to be on the first line, not the second or the third also make sure there's no space before the pound sign. Now this is called a shebang. And what the shebang does is it's the full path to the command interpreter that we want to run the script. And in this case, we chose slash bin slash bash, which is bash.","Now in the third line, we can add this. Now the pound sign at the beginning of the line marks this as a comment. A commented line is something that the command interpreter will ignore. It will not execute anything here. No matter what we type.","A comment is useful when we want to document our script to inform other people that may read it about what our lex line or lines do. Also, it might be you coming back to the script six months later and you're writing comments for yourself. Now, on the next line, we'll add an actual command to this script. The date command prints out a date and time. We can also use redirection to save the output of the date command to the slash temp slash script dot log file.","We're essentially building a simple log from within our script note how we can write these commands the same way we write them at the command-line, we can redirect output errors, pipe to other programs and so on. Now, in the next line, we'll add this command slash proc slash version is a special file that has detailed information about the current Linux kernel version that's running on our operating system. So we're sending the content of the special file to our log file at slash temp slash script dot log in a nutshell. We built a script that can keep track of what kernel versions ran on our server in the past. Now let's press escape then colon WQ and enter to save our file to be able to run this script.","We must now make it executable. If we only want the owner of this file, our current user to be able to execute it, we can add this permission or if we want everyone to be able to execute it, we can just run this. This will grant execute permissions for everybody, the user owner, the group owner and others to run this. We must either provide the full path to the script and type this or we can type this, the dot represents our current directory. So when we type in dot slash script dot sh, the dot becomes slash home slash Aaron.","Since we're already inside this directory, the dot represents our current directory. So when we type in dot slash script dot sh, the dot becomes slash home slash Aaron, since we're already inside this directory slash script dot sh is added at the end. So it's the same as typing in slash home slash Aaron slash script dot sh. And let's see what our script did. We'll read the script dot log file pretty nice result.","Now, further down the line, we could set this up to automatically run each day and we'll have a log with all Linux kernel versions that we ever used. When something malfunctions, you could consult this log and say, hey, this problem started when we updated to the 5.15 dot X version. So that's the core part of writing a script. You must make a file executable and then you add in its contents with a shebang pointing to the path of the command interpreter, followed by some instructions for that interpreter. However, smarter scripts can be created with the help of what are called bash builtins.","And to get a quick list of the available builtins type in help, there are many available, but we'll explore two of these to get a glimpse of what they make possible. We'll look at if and test. Let's create a different script to archive the contents of the slash Etsy slash app directory. Now, we can run this command even if archive dash app dot sh doesn't exist, Vim will create it automatically once we save the content. Let's imagine we want this script to back up the contents of the slash Etsy slash app directory.","We'll use Tar to create an archive that will be saved at slash temp slash archive dot Tar dot GZ. Once the file is created, we'll make the script executable and run it. And we see that indeed, our simple script created in an archive with the content of the Etsy slash app directory. And we can look at the list of files inside using Tar TF. But now imagine this someone accidentally deletes some files in slash Etsy slash A.","Next our backup script runs then slash temp slash archive dot par GZ getss created overwriting the previous backup. Well, now we're left with a broken Etsy app directory and also a broken slash temp slash archive dot TGZ since it backed up the broken Etsy app directory, if only we still had the old backup around the one that contained the old content of slash Etsy slash app, which was still good. Well, we can make our script smarter, we can make it implement the following logic. We can check to see if slash temp slash archive dot TGZ exists using if and test two built in functions of bash. If it does, we can rename it to slash temp slash archive dot Tar dot GZ dot old and then create a new slash temp slash archive dot TGZ.","Now, we'll always have two backups, the new one and the previous older one. Now, if archive dot Tar dot GZ does not exist, it will simply create the first backup in the series. And as mentioned, we'll use if and test bash builtins to make this possible to get information about a builtin and its syntax use help followed by its name. For example, help if and help test. Let's create a new script called archive dash A dash two dot sh and add in this content that implements our desired logic.","Now let's analyze this line by line if test dash F archive dot Tar dot GZ then OK. So if starts a condition check, it verifies if the expression that comes after it is true or false. And inside our condition, we use the test built in. Now as help test command shows us this can perform various types of check. For example, for files, it can test if it's a certain type of file if you can execute it and so on.","In this case, the dash F option makes it check the file at slash temp slash archive dot TGZ check if it exists as a regular file, if it does the test built in returns true. If it doesn't, it returns false. Now our if builtin cannot know where the condition check ends, so we tell it where it ends by adding the semicolon at the end of the condition, we want to be verified. Finally, the word then signals what will happen if our test function returns true. So when this is true, the following two commands get executed.","And this simply renames the preexisting archive dot TGZ file to archive dot Tar dot GZ dot old and then creates a new archive named archive dot par dot GZ. Now note how we have four spaces before each command. This is just standard convention to make the script more easily readable because the lines are moved to the right. In this way, we can easily spot what will happen if our test function returns true. And the next line is this signals what will happen if our test doesn't return true and it returns false instead.","In this case, this is what will happen if fl temp slash archive dot TGZ does not exist if it returns false, a simple archive will be created and it won't rename the old archive this time because there isn't one available. Finally, on the last line, we have F I which is just if in reverse this signals that this is the end of our if block. Now let's save our script and make it executable and then we'll run it. And if we look in slash temp, we'll see this, which means our script is working as intended. The old archive was moved to a file with the dot old extension and a new archive was created.","So now we have both versions available. It's important to note that almost any command can be used in an if block. That's because most commands return a so called exit status code, they return zero. If the command executed successfully, they return a value larger than zero. If the command didn't find what it was looking for, or if it encountered an error, when it sees the command return zero, it considers this to be returning true when the command returns a nonzero value if considers that command to be returned false.","For example, consider the grep command. If it finds the text, you are looking for, it returns zero. If it doesn't, it returns one, so we can have a script like this. So in this first line, we're using grep to look at the Grub file and see if it has a timeout of five seconds, right? So grep looks for the number five in the Etsy default Grub file and Q stands for quiet.","This option tells grep not to display match lines because we don't need our script to output those lines. In this case, our script will output. Grub has a timeout of five seconds if the number five was found and it will output. Grub does not have a timeout of five seconds if the number five was not found. And now we make the script executable and run it and look at that.","Grub has a timeout of five seconds. These are the basics of scripting with bash builtins. If you want your future scripts to implement more advanced logic, you'll have to dive deeper into more builtins such as for and while loops functions, variables and so on. These allow you to construct scripts in a way that resembles programming logic. Thus, your scripts become more intelligent, so to speak, and they can do even more interesting things.","And if you're in need of a quick refresh of how scripting works, you could take a quick peek at files from the slash Etsy slash Chron dot daily, weekly and monthly directories. Usually all these files in here are scripts. This way you can get sort of a cheat sheet for common script syntax like the shebang on the first line and the syntax of F and so on to learn more about shell scripts, check out our shell scripts for beginners course on code cloud. It's a project driven course with many hands on labs that will help you learn with easy to understand theory and also practical exercises you can go through."],"Operations Deployment/4":["Let's examine how to manage startup processes and services in Linux. When we boot up Linux, certain important applications start up automatically, some of them even start up in a specific order. For example, if APP two depends on APP one, then app one will load before app two. All of this magic happens behind the scenes and in a rather intelligent way. Furthermore, if important applications crash, they will be restarted automatically.","This way the system can continue to run smoothly even if there's a small hiccup like this. But how does all this happen? Well, with the help of what is called the init system short for initialization system. So how does this init system know how to start up applications, what to do when a program crashes and so on? It needs specific instructions to know how to do its job.","And sure enough, it has all the instructions it needs in what are called systemd units. These are simply text files that describe the necessary logic and system D is the name of a collection of tools, components and applications that help start operate and manage most of the Linux based operating systems. System D is also the name of the program that starts up as the init system, which can be a bit confusing. Long story short system D is the name of a large collection of tools and components, but also the name of the application responsible for system initialization and monitoring the system as a whole to ensure smooth operation. Now, units can be of various types such as service socket device, timer and others.","For example timer units, let us tell the init system that it should launch a specific application once per week. Maybe to clean up some files or verify a database units can be of various types such as service, socket device, timer and others. For example, timer units, let us tell the init system that it should launch a specific application once per week, maybe to clean up some files or verify a database. But we'll be looking at service units in this lesson. Now service units have clear instructions about such things as what command to issue to start up a program.","What to do if a program crashes, what command to issue when a program is restarted. And many other things in a nutshell, a service unit tells the init system all it needs to know about how it should manage the entire life cycle of an application. We can see the multitude of instructions we can add in a service unit if we type in man systemd dot service. Now let's look at a real example, servers need to run the SSH daemon to let users connect to them from remote locations. And there's a service unit that instructs the init system about how to start this daemon and how to keep it running.","We can look at this service file with this command and we'll see lines like this in this file. Execstart tells the init system what command it should run when it wants to start the SSH daemon. Execreload tells it what command it should run to reload the configuration for the SSH daemon. We'll see an example of what reloading a service means later in this lesson. And then the restart line tells system D to automatically restart this process only when something fails.","For example, if the SSH daemon crashes, it's a good idea to have system D restart it. So we can still connect to our server. Now, if we want to edit this service file and modify these instructions, we can use a command like this. If we want to cancel our edits later and return the service file to its factory default settings, we can use this command and revert it back. So this systemd service is responsible for the life cycle of the SSH daemon to start up the SSH server application, restart it when needed and so on to see the status of the service, we can run this command system CTL status SSH service.","Now, this shows us a few interesting things. And here are the most important. We can see if this service is enabled, if it's enabled. That means that system D will automatically start up the SSH daemon. When the system boots disabled means it won't automatically start up, but it still can be manually started by the administrator.","And we'll see how later on next. We can see if the program managed by the service is running. If it's currently launched, loaded into memory and running, we'll see a status of active running, we could potentially encounter situations where the service is active, but the program managed by the service is not running because it was launched, did its job successfully and then exited. In such a case, we'd see a status of active but exited between parentheses. Now, if the SSH daemon is currently running, we'll also see its P ID or process identifier.","Every time we launch a program on Linux, a process will start up the process encapsulates the computer code and resources that are loaded into memory and using the CPU when it needs to execute instructions and every process has this unique number, identifying it. The pit can be used to interact with this process as we'll see in one of the next lessons. And we can also see the exact command that has been used to start up this process. And finally, system CTL status shows us a few log lines, status and error messages generated by this application. This can be useful to see what happened after the program started up.","If it encountered any errors and what settings are active and so on, we can press Q to exit from the status display. Now to stop a service, we can enter SYSTEMCTL stop and then the service, this will close the program that the service unit controls. We mentioned how some services do not automatically start up at boot time, but they can still be manually started up later. The command to manually start a service is system CTL start and then the service. Now sometimes we will change the settings of a program, for example, to modify how the SSH daemon works.","We could change its settings in the file at Etsy SSH, SSH D underscore config. Now, after we change a configuration file like this, the process currently running will still use the old settings until it's restarted. One way to force the process to restart and pick up the new settings is with this command system CTL restart SSH service. However, this restart command can sometimes be a bit disruptive. For example, if a few users are actively using that program, the restart may interrupt their work.","So there's a more gentle way to reload program settings without completely closing and reopening the application. And that's what system CTL reload. Now, if we would check out the status for the SSH service again, we would see that the first command restarted it and the second just gracefully reloaded its settings. It's worth noting that not all applications support being reloaded like this, but we have a command that can automatically try a graceful reload first and then a restart. If the reload is not supported by that app, we can do that by typing in system CTL reload or restart and the service.","Now, if we want to disable SSH logins completely, we could prevent the SSH daemon from automatically starting up a boot time by typing in system CTL disable in the service. And now if we check the status, it's disabled, but we can also check with system CTL is enabled now to reenable the SSH daemon and make it automatically start up at boot. We would type in system CTL enable in the name of the service. Quick note, if you went through these steps on your own virtual machine, if you disabled your SSH service, please reenable it now to ensure that you can log back in the next time you boot your Linux OS. Now, usually after we install a new application, especially if it's a daemon or a server of some kind, like a database or server application or a web server, we want to do two things.","We want to enable it to autostart every time the operating system boots up and we want to manually start it so we can begin to use it immediately. Operating systems from the Debian and Ubuntu family usually take these steps automatically after you install certain programs operating systems from the Debian and Ubuntu family usually take these steps automatically after you install certain programs. But other operating systems, for example, those belonging to the Redhat family don't. So it's useful to know how to perform these actions manually. One way to do this is with the two commands that we just showed.","First, we tell our init system to automatically start the service every time the operating system boots up. Then we tell our init system to also start the service immediately. Otherwise this service will only start the next time we boot up the operating system, but it would not run for our current session. But we also have an alternative command that does both things in one shot. The dash dash.","Now, option tells system CTL to both enable the service and autostart every time the system boots up but also start the service. Now, the disable command also supports the dash dash. Now option if you're practicing in a virtual machine, don't enter this next command as you won't be able to log in through SSH anymore. This would disable the SSH daemon from automatically starting up when the system boots up and also stop the service in the program it manages. Now with some services, we might notice that they're stubborn.","That is even if we disable them from autostarting at boot and we stop them, we might notice later on that they're still running somehow. They mysteriously start up on their own. And we'd be left wondering, well, how does this happen? Well, some services could automatically start up other services if they want to do so. So there can be a domino effect service.","One can start up service two, even if you disabled and stopped service two. But there's a brute force way to prevent this from happening. And it's called masking. Let's say we want to mask the service responsible for managing the at daemon. The at daemon is a program that lets us schedule tasks we want to run in the future and failed to start service.","So this basically ensures that the service cannot ever be started even if other programs users or services want to do. So finally, if we mask the service and we want to cancel that we can unmask it and return to normal with this command system, CTL unmask. But we can see that the system CTL commands are rather intuitive. What might not always be intuitive is how a service for a certain application might be named. For example, we can install a web server like Apache and expect the service for this application to be called Apache dot service.","But we may find out that it's called HTTP D dot service on some operating systems. So it can be useful to get a list of all service units available on the system no matter if they're currently enabled, disabled, started or stopped and we can do that with this command system CTL list units type service, all system CTL units would show all types of systemd units. And remember besides service units, other types exist like socket units, timer units, et cetera. With dash dash type service. We tell our command to only show us service units combined with dash dash all, we tell it to show us all service units no matter what their status is."],"Operations Deployment/5":["In this lesson, we'll show how to create a systemd service file and the best way to understand why we need such a service and how to define it is to go through a practical exercise. Let's assume we built some sort of application one that needs to run on our servers all the time. Now, the issue with programs is they can occasionally crash. So we want something to keep a watchful eye on our application and restart it if it ever fails. Also, we might want it to automatically start up when the system boots, systemd services can do both of these things and more.","Let's add these lines. Now, the first echo line sends a message to the system log stating that my app started system D dash C is the utility that receives the message and logs it dash T adds an identifier to the log so that we'll know that these messages belong to a program called my app. And dash P sets the priority of the message to info. In other words, it tells the system logger that this is a regular status message and the app is behaving normally. The next line makes our fake application, wait for five seconds.","We basically simulate that it's working correctly during this time. Finally, on the last line, the priority of the log message will be set to error. This tells the system logger that the app encountered some sort of problem. And the logger will highlight this with the red color, making the message stand out. So now we simulate that the app encountered an error after five seconds as it exits, it will print out that error message.","So let's save our file and continue. We'll make this script executable with the following command. So how do we create a systemd service? Well, we can get some help from the manual describing system D service files with the help of this command. We'll see two interesting sections in this manual.","First, the options section here, we will find configuration options for the service we'll define. Let's take a look at some of the options we'll use. For example, the restart option here with this, we can tell system D when it should restart our application note the values we can assign, we can pick node to tell system D to never restart it or on failure to only restart it when the app throws an error code. But our simple app is not complex enough to throw any error codes. So we'll just set this to always, to tell system D to always restart our app when it exits.","If we scroll back up we'll see this restart sec option which fits well with the previous one with it, we can tell system D to wait a while before attempting to restart an application that stopped running. In our case, our app will wait five seconds before it exits. So we wouldn't even need this delay. But a real world application could potentially fail and exit in just a few milliseconds. And if system D would restart it instantly, it could create an issue where the application would restart hundreds of times a minute.","This is called a restart loop. And it's something that can slow the system down by making system D wait a few seconds before restarting the application. We can avoid such issues. Finally, if we scroll down on this manual, we'll find the examples section here. In example two, we can see the simplest system D service we can create, which is very useful to get an idea about how this file should be structured.","We can see that we need three sections, unit service and install. We'll see what these do when we create our service. But for now, let's exit the manual. As beginners, it would be pretty hard to define a service starting from nothing. We don't know the exact text that we need.","But we can get some help with this trick in the directory, live system D system, we'll find already existing service, unit files so we can use something from that location to get a good starting point kind of a template on which we can build this from. Let's copy the service for the SSH daemon. We'll copy it to a file named myapp dot Service. The services that we create should be dropped into the Etsy system D system directory. So our copy command will look like this.","And now let's start editing our service file. Once again, we see the three sections we need to have in a service file, unit, service and install. So let's dive into the unit section first, we describe this service unit. So let's modify the description. Now, we don't have a manual for our application.","So let's just remove the second line. Now, the next line is probably the most important one in the unit section. This tells system D when it should run this service unit. And in this case, it's instructed to run it after both the network dot target and auditd service have started. Now, a target is basically a collection of services that should start together.","So on this line, we tell system D to start our service only when the networking related services have started. And the audit daemon service. Now this is vital because most applications depend on other applications or services. In this case, the SSH daemon shouldn't start before the network is configured. Otherwise it won't know what IP address and network interface to listen to for incoming connections.","So what this does is it basically gathers up some dependencies for your application and make sure that they're started and running before your application runs. But for our purposes, we can just leave this line as it is. Now on the next line, we can see an exclamation mark which is a symbol for negation. It's basically like not this. So this instructs system D to start the service only if this file does not exist.","And we don't need this line for our purposes. So we'll remove it. But nonetheless, it's interesting to see how detailed a service file can be in its instructions. Now, we get to the service section here, we specify how our application's life cycle should be controlled, how it should be started, restarted, reloaded and what should happen when it crashes. And so on, on the first line, we see an environment file specified this simply loads environment variables listed in that file like the path variable that we learned about in previous lessons.","We don't need this line. So we'll remove it next. With Execstart Pre, we can specify a program that should run before our application will be launched. This can prepare the terrain, so to speak. For example, we could run a program that scans the databases for errors before starting the database server application.","And just to test this out, let's modify it. We will see this message later in our logs informing us that the command at Execstart pre was launched before our application was started finally, we get to the part we're most interested in. The execstart specifies the command that should be used to start our application. So let's point this to the location of our application more specifically our shell script file. Now, execreload specifies what should be executed when an administrator runs the system CTL reload command.","So whenever someone wants to do a graceful reload to apply modified configuration settings, this is what will be executed, we can remove both exact reload lines here. Since our app doesn't support that, the kill mode line specifies how this application should be stopped. For example, when an administrator runs a system CTL stop command, in many cases, a service unit can start multiple processes or the application run by the service can spawn multiple processes. So system D has to know how it should stop processes that it started because of the service with kill mode equals process. We tell system D to only kill the main process and not any child processes that might be launched as well.","In our case, we can leave this as it is. In other scenarios, we might set this to kill mode equals control group which would kill all processes created by the service. The next line tells system D to only restart our application on failure. And this is not what we want with our specific app. When an application exits, it returns an exit code zero means it exited successfully.","Nonzero means it encountered an error. Now, our app is simple and it will always exit normally with a zero exit code. So system D would not restart it with this setting to restart our application every time it exits, we'll modify this line and we'll set it to restart. Always. Also, when we looked at the manual, we explored the restart sec option.","So let's add that this will add the restart delay that we discussed. When system D notices our app needs to be restarted, it will wait one full second before doing so the next instruction, restart, prevent exit status equals 255. Tell system D not to restart the service. If the application throws an exit code equal to 255 and we don't need this so we can remove it as well. The next line can be important depending on the type of application here, type equals notify, tells systemd that the application will notify it when it is finished starting up.","Now, our app does not have such capabilities. So we'll set this to type equals simple. Another commonly used option here can be type equals one shot for applications that don't need to run in the background. For example, a program that quickly opens up, deletes some files and then exits because it has nothing else to do. Another often used option is type equals forking for an application that spawns many child processes when it's executed.","Now, the next two lines specify the relative path to the directory that this application should run in and the permissions for that directory, this can be used to create temporary files or whatever the app might need. However, we'll remove these two lines and now we get to the install section to understand the next line. We need to know what systemd targets are. Normally a server boots up without a graphical user interface, but multiple users can still log in to a text only interface to provide this environment where many people can log in a collection of applications needs to start. This collection is grouped in what is called the multi user target.","If the server boots up to a graphical interface, this is called the graphical target. And there are many other targets. Each target is simply a collection of services that should start up to achieve a certain purpose. As we saw earlier, the network target groups, all services needed to have a working network. With this line, we tell system D that this service belongs to the target called multiuser.","Next, the alias line simply instructs system D to create links to our dot service file in other locations. The reason for this line here is that the service for managing the SSH daemon is called SSH D dot service on operating systems from the red hat family, but on operating systems from the Debian and Ubuntu family, it's called SSH dot service without the D at the end. So administrators who are used to typing commands like system CTL reload. SSH D dot Service would encounter an error on Ubuntu. But by linking the SSH dot service file here to another file called SSH D dot Service, both service names will work on Ubuntu when we want to interact with the SSH daemon.","Now, in our case, we don't need an alias. So we'll remove this line. Our final service unit file now looks like this. So let's save our file and see if our service works. But first when we add, remove or edit service files, we need to reload the system D daemon with this command.","Now let's take a look at the system log. We'll use the F option for follow mode so we can see log messages as they're generated and everything seems to be working perfectly. So every time our application crashes, we see the error messages highlighted in a red color and system D picks it up and restarts it automatically and let's press control C to exit from following the system log. Also note that our execstart pre instruction is executing correctly since we can see the message that we added to that option. So that wraps up the core concepts around creating a systemd service.","As you can see, it's not really that hard, just a bunch of instructions in a file. The hard part is finding the options you need. But as long as you know what you want system D to do for you. You'll find all the options you require by typing man, followed by system D with a dot At the end. No space and pressing tab twice.","Unfortunately, not all options will be found in the system D dot Service manual. You'll also find some of them in the system D dot unit manual. Specifically the stuff you add in the first unit section of a service file will be found there. Also some details related to how the application in the service should be executed will be found in the system D dot exec manual. And details related to how systemd should stop or kill processes started by services can be found in the system D dot Kill manual.","But as a beginner, you might learn faster if you start looking at already made service files. So start with LS live system D slash system and see how those service files were created to achieve the purposes of specific applications. And I hope this was a fun learning experience. I'll see you in the next lesson."],"Operations Deployment/6":["Whenever we launch a program, it lives as a so called process on the operating system that is until it finishes its job or it's closed. For example, when we run a command such as LS, a short lived process gets created. The process for LS displays the directory's content. And as soon as the job is finished, the process is closed. Other processes though have a long life.","The SSH daemon is a good example as it's usually running continuously on a Linux server. The commonly used command to inspect processes is PS and PS is a bit weird because it supports two kinds of syntaxes for its options, dash A in a command like PS dash A uses the Unix syntax in a command like PS space A. However, the BSD syntax is used and PS A has an entirely different effect than PS space A. The dash A and A options are not equivalent. If we use PS without any options, we'll see a result like this by default, PS will only show us processes launched in our current terminal window or session.","But we're interested in all processes on the system to make PS show them all, we need to combine three options A X and U, the A X combination makes PS display all processes, the U option lists processes in the so called user oriented format. This adds useful columns to the output or we can see things like memory and CPU usage. It also shows us which user each process belongs to. That's why the most often used command to see all processes on a Linux system is PS dash A UX. You can remember it if you think of the word auxiliary and if you forget it, you can get a quick refresh with man PS.","And if you scroll one or two pages down, you'll find the examples section showing you the most commonly used PS commands. Let's analyze this PS AUX output briefly percent CPU shows us how much percent of the CPU is currently in use by this process. 100% means the entire capacity of one CPU core is used. So we might see numbers like 150 here, meaning one full core is used but also 50% of the capacity of a second core percent memory shows us how much percent of the total memory this uses start, shows us the time at which the process was started or the date. If the process was not started today, time shows us how much CPU time is used during the process' lifetime.","So even if a process started six hours ago, it might have only used three seconds of CPU time during its lifetime. The rest of the time is spent in a sleeping state using 0% of the CPU to get one second of time here. The process needs to use 100% of one CPU core for an entire second. If it would use 50% of one CPU core for 10 seconds, it would get around five seconds of time recorded in this column. Finally, in the command column, we can see the exact command that was used including options to start this process.","Now, another interesting thing we see in this output is that some processes are wrapped between square brackets. These are kernel processes running inside a privileged area of the Linux kernel. We usually don't need to interact with these as the kernel manages them internally and will only deal with normal processes running outside this privileged area called the user space. These are the ones that are not wrapped between square brackets but PS only shows us the state of processes at the exact moment when we execute the command. If we want to continuously watch what's going on, we can use a different command in the name of that command is talk by default, top will constantly reorder processes in this list and show the most CPU intensive ones.","At the top. We can navigate through this output with the arrow keys and page up or page down to quit. We press Q but how about filtering results. We can also narrow down our list and only look for specific processes. If we know the P ID or process identifier number of a process, we can use it in a command like this.","In this case, one is the P I. And if we want to see the same kind of output as before with CPU and memory usage, we can use the U option again to display the user oriented format. If we want to see the processes that have been started by the user Jeremy, we pass the username after the dash uppercase U option. And again, if we want the user oriented format, we will add in the lower case U to this command. Now, sometimes we will want to explore processes that have a specific name to search for any process that contains the word this log in its name.","For instance, we can use P grap or process grap. The A option here which means all makes P grep also display the command in which the process was started. So we see a line like this. The first field is the P id or process id. In this case, it's 1084.","What follows it is the command that started this process. Now in Linux, there's this concept of process niceness that is how nice a process is to other processes. The niceness value can be a number between negative 2019. The lower the number, the less nice. It is.","In other words, a lower number means a higher priority. Consider process A with a nice value of negative 20 process B with a nice value of 19. If both of these are running at the same time, we need to use all CPU resources available. Process A will be very little available for process B A might use up CPU 95 to 99% of the time. While B will get very little CPU time only when there's something left to spare, we'll want important processes to have higher priority, which means we will assign them lower nice values.","There are two ways this can be done. First at launch time, we can launch a command with a preset. Nice value. The syntax is nice dash N followed by the nice value and then the command to assign a nice value of 11 to the bash command. We can run this nice dash N 11 bash.","Our previous PS A UX command doesn't show nice values, but we can use the L option that turns on BS D long format output which includes columns for nice values. We'll see these in the N I column. If we want to see nice values for all processes, we just add the A X options. Our nice dash N 11 bash command started a new bash shell and we can see that the Pslax command inherited the nice value of a Rash shell. That's because all processes inherit the niceness of their parent process.","The one that launched them to see which processes are parents for other processes, we can add the f option which stands for forest display because it shows us treelike branches for this child parent relationship. We can clearly see how our previous bash shell started a second bash shell. And this in turn started the PS FA X command. We can clearly see here how our previous bash shell started a second bash shell and this in turn started the PS FA X command. If we also want to see CPU usage, memory usage and the columns we're used to, we can throw the U option in here.","The order of the FA UX letters is not important. OK? Let's exit this second bash shell with exit. Now, a regular user can only assign nice values between zero and 19 to assign lower values for higher priority. We need to use root privileges.","So this command will not work. As you can see here. Permission is denied to set it to negative 12. But this will and we can confirm the value is set with PSL A and there's our bash shell with the minus 12 nice value. So let's exit our bash shell once again.","Now, the problem with the command pseudo nice dash N dash 12 bash is that it also started our bash shell as the root user. But what if we want our process to run under our regular user, we can do it this way. First, we launch the program as usual, we'll start yet another bash shell and imagine that this is some utility that we want to give very high CPU usage priority to. Now, in the PS forest view, we can identify the pid of this second bath shell right here. And we see that this has a pid of 12238, we can readjust the current nice value of an existing process to another value with re nice.","And we use this syntax Reni followed by the new nice value followed by the pid of the process. So to change it from its current default value of zero to something like seven, we can enter this and we'll get this confirmation message where it shows the old priority and the new priority. And if we take another look with Pslax, we see that this is indeed now seven. But what if we try to lower it to six? We find out that regular users cannot lower the nice value of a process that it currently has.","So we can't change anything below the current niceness of seven. We can only make it 89, 10 or larger but not 65 or lower. So this will work. But to lower it, we once again need root privileges since only root can lower process niceness. Awesome.","So let's exit our second best show. Linux has this concept of sending processes so-called signals. These are like high priority messages that tell the process, hey, stop what you're doing. We have the special request for you, but an application can only act on a specific signal if it was programmed to respond to that signal. The only exceptions are signals like sigstop and sigkill.","These cannot be ignored by the process. When they receive sigstop, their execution will be paused until sig or continue is received. So when they receive sigkill, they will be immediately terminated without even getting a chance to save their current work. A sigkill is usually only set as a last resort when the program is not responding and it can't be closed. In any other way, we can see a list of signals that we can send with the dash L or list option.","When we choose a signal to send, we can use a name from this list. So we could type Sigup, but we can also admit the SIG prefix if we want and just type in Hup. So Sigup and hup are equivalent. Let's find out the pid of our SSH daemon. Now we can press Q to quit this view and we have a pid of 1457 in this case.","Now let's send it the sighup signal to do that. We type in kill. Now let's send it the SIGUP signal to do that. We type in kill dash the H 1457. Now we get operation not permitted that's because the SSH D process is running under the root user as a regular user, we can only send signals to processes owned by our current user.","And of course, root is the Exception and it can send signals to any process, its own processes, but also processes owned by others. So we need to turn our command into this. The SSH daemon is programmed to respond to this in a certain way. We can see what it did. If we check out its service status again.","As we can see here, when it receives SIGUP, it's programmed to reload its configuration file and restart. Now in the table displayed here, we can see that each signal has a number before it. So to send the Sigkill command, we can use three different variants. We can do kill the kill, kill dash, kill without the sig or kill nine. It's also worth noting if we don't specify a signal name, the Sigterm signal will be sent by default.","So kill 1752 will send Sigterm to the process of P ID 1752. Sigterm tells the program to gracefully shut down. It has a chance to save its work and close down. Normally with kill, we send a signal to a process with a certain P ID number. But another command PKILL lets us send signals to processes with a certain name instead of API D.","For example, let's say we want to send sigkill to all processes that have a name containing the word bash. Before we send that signal, we can explore what processes would receive it. So we don't send it to the wrong ones. We can type in pgrep a bash. And if we're happy with the result, this abruptly ends our SSH session since the bash cell is forcefully closed.","So we'll have to log back in now backgrounding and foregrounding processes, pausing and exiting some commands can take a long time to finish their job. Let's simulate this with the sleep command. This will simply wait for 100 and 80 seconds until it exits way too long. Now, we can tell a program to quit whatever it's doing by pressing control C Now, let's see another scenario. Let's start editing some file.","But in the middle of this editing, maybe we want to do something else and leave our editor open. We can pause our current application and return to the terminal with control Z. Now we can do something else. Now, as you can see, we're back to our prompt. We're not in Vim anymore, but we can return to our PASD app with this command FG FG stands for foreground.","That is bring the app back to the foreground back to the focus in our terminal and we can type escape then quit to quit our Vim editor. It's worth noting that control C and Controlz won't work for any program, but they work for most of them. It's also worth noting when a program paused with control Z, it does not make any progress with its work. It's entirely frozen. So if we want a program to do its work in the background, we use the Ampersand symbol.","For example, this is called backgrounding a command. An example when this would be useful, imagine we want to back up 500 gig of data. And let's say this would take 30 minutes. We don't want to wait for 30 minutes until the backup command finishes. So we can background it, it will do its job for 30 minutes.","But in that time, we can continue to use the terminal and enter other commands. No need to wait for the entire backup to finish to check if we have any programs running in the background or paused, we can type in jobs. And as we can see, we have a job running here. If we want to return to our backgrounded command, we type in FG one because our backgrounded command has the idea of one. But now we brought our sleep 300 command back to the foreground and we're stuck.","Now, we can press control Z to pause it and get back to the command-line. But this is problematic. The app is now paused. If this would be the backup job, it wouldn't be making any progress when we could take a look at our active jobs again. And we can see the imaginary backup job is stopped to make it resume progress.","We can use the BG or background command and now jobs will show us that this is running and the backup is completing. Sometimes we want to see what files or directories a process is currently using. First. Let's find the P ID of our bash shell. Our P ID in this case is 13536.","And if you're following along, of course, it'll be different now to see what files or directories our bash process with P ID 13536 is using. We would type in this and this shows all the files and directories that this process is using. Now later on, we might want to explore other processes and we might get output that looks like this. We can also specify the path to a file or directory to see if any process is currently using it. And there it is.","This way we make sure we're getting the full picture and this concludes our demo on diagnosing and managing processes."],"Operations Deployment/7":["Now we'll look at how to locate and analyze system log files in Linux. Linux operating systems are largely server oriented and on a server you always want to know what happened, who did, what, what worked, what didn't work, what errors were encountered, who accessed the system and so on. Everything important that happens on a Linux system is saved as a text message somewhere which are called logs. The Linux kernel and most programs that run on the Linux operating system, generate status messages, error messages, warnings and so on. There can be tens of different programs generating these messages all the time.","So we need a way to collect all of these and organize them nicely into files. And this is the job of logging daemons. These are simply applications that collect, organize and store logs. The most popular one on Linux is rsyslog and its name comes from rocket fast system for log processing once logged in. Let's say we have no idea where SSH logs are stored and we want to see who logged in through SSH.","Now, we can search through all the files and look for anything that contains the word SSH D to find stuff belonging to the SSH daemon. And we'll notice a few lines like this and this tells us that some SSH logs are stored in the slash bar slash log slash A dot log file. We could then open up the file and start looking for SSH D log. And in Vim, we would do a front slash type in SSH, D and press N to find the next match and then Q to quit, we could then open up the file with less and start looking for SSH D logs, press the front slash type SSH D and press N to find the next match and then queue to quit in the slash bar slash log slash off dot log file. We'll find logs related to secure authentication or authorization.","We can see the date and time when this happened, the hostname of the computer where this happened, the application or source that generated this log message and the log message itself. Many log entries follow the same format, especially system logs that keep track of what happened on the operating system. And speaking of system logs, you'll find most of these in the slash bar slash log slash cis log file. Now, you might have noticed files with names like a dot log 0.1 or a dot log 0.2 dot gz. These are older logs and A dot log is the latest A dot log 0.1 is the one that was logged before the latest A dot log and A dot log 0.2 dot GZ is the one that was created before A dot log 0.1.","And it's also archived with the gzip utility to make the file smaller. Most of the time, you'll look at logs and see what happened in the past. But sometimes you'll also want to take a look at what's currently happening. Imagine you're debugging an application, you want to try different things and see what errors it generates while you push the buttons and you can get a live view of a log file and see log entries as soon as they appear with this command. Now we use tail dash F and then the file name, the dash F makes tail enter follow mode.","You can test this out by using that command and then switching to your virtual machine and logging in at the text console. And now we can easily tell journal CTL. Hey, only show me the logs generated by this command. Awesome, right. This output opens up in the less pager so we can use the navigation keys, we can search keys everything else we learn that we can do in the less utility and then press Q to quit.","In a previous lesson. We learned how the operating system is managing important system applications with the help of service units. For example, the SSH daemon that's constantly running in the background and such a service unit controls and monitors its activity. Now, if we know the name of the service, we can view logs for it with this command dash U tells journal CTL to display logs generated by the SSH dot service unit. If we simply type journal CTL, we'll see all logs collected by the journal daemon to go to the end of the output, we can press the greater than sign.","If we wanna open the journal and instantly go to the end of the output. We can use the dash E or end option just like we opened up a log file in follow mode journal CTL also has a follow mode. We can access that by passing the dash F option to our command. And now we have a live view of all the new logs that appear on the system and you press control C to exit this, most logs are just informative. They're telling us about normal stuff that's happening on the system, but some are warnings about things that are not quite going well.","Others are errors about things that failed. Others are huge alarm signals about things going critically wrong. And these are tagged as info warning err crit. Now, if we only want to look for moderately serious errors, we can use the dash P priority command-line option. And one of those flag, this shows us messages tagged with error or the error priority level.","The list of priorities we can use with the dash P option are alert crit debug emerge error info notice and warning. So if you forget this list, just type in journal CTL dash P, add one space at the end and press tab two times, you'll get a list of all the priority code names that you can use. Now, we can also use grep expressions to filter output with the help of the dash G command-line option. And of course, we can combine multiple command-line options. For example, we could filter out only logs with the info priority that have a message beginning with the letter B with dash G.","And then our regex Carat B, we pass the graph expression to only look for lines that begin with B. We can also tell journal CTL to only display logs recorded after a certain time with the dash S or sense option. This uses the 24 hour format. So 0 200 shows us logs that were generated since 2 a.m. We also have an until dash U option.","So if we combine both options, we could see logs generated since 1 a.m. and until 2 a.m. with a command like this, the dash S and dash U options also support dates. So we can use a command like this to see logs generated since 2024 March 23rd, 23 and 32nd note how we can fine tune this down to the second. It's important to wrap this date between the single quotes.","Normally journal CTL will show us all logs collected on the system starting with the first one available. But the first entries might be super old, for example, since two months ago. And in most cases, we don't need such old data in a nutshell. The journal Daemon also groups logs based on when the system booted. And often we'll get to see logs generated.","Since the last time the system booted to see logs generated for this current boot. We can use the dash bboot option. In our case, dash B zero picks our current boot, boot zero to see logs for the previous boot. We would type in this to see logs generated two boots ago, we would type in journal CTL dash B dash two on some operating systems. Though the journaling daemon is configured to only keep logs for the current boot and only in memory, not on disk.","So as soon as we reboot, the journal log is lost. It was a command like journal CTL dash B dash one might show us that no persistent journal was found. Although text logs are still preserved in the old fashioned logging format in the vlog directory, but usually the journaling daemon is configured to decide whether to save a persistent journal depending on the existence of the directory slash bar slash log slash journal. If it doesn't exist, it won't preserve journal logs on disk, but if it exists, it will start to preserve system D journal logs in that directory. So by simply creating this directory, we can instruct the journaling daemon to preserve these logs there.","The newest entries are at the top. So you might have to scroll up to see them. The lines beginning with reboot system boot. Also show you when the system was powered on. An alternative command is last log.","This shows you when each user on the system logged in the last time for remote logins. For example, through SSH, you can also see the IP address from which they logged in."],"Operations Deployment/8":["Now, we'll explore how to schedule tasks to be run at set times. And Linux on servers, we sometimes need to set up some tasks to run automatically. For example, we could set up a job to automatically back up the database every Sunday at 3 a.m. we have three main ways to set up such tasks with the cron utility with Akron or with a utility called at. Now, here's the main purpose of each tool.","The cron utility is well suited for repetitive jobs that execute once every few minutes or hours or on specific days or specific times. Anacron is also used to create repetitive jobs, but with a few differences, one is that it cannot repeat those jobs every few minutes or hours. The smallest unit it can work with is a day. So it can run a job every day or every three days or every week or even every month or year, but it cannot run a job multiple times per day just once per day. This utility has been created because cron can miss jobs if the computer is not powered on all the time.","For example, if a job is set to run daily at noon and the computer is powered on at 1201, that job won't run for that day. And Akron on the other hand, will check if that job for today ever got a chance to run. If it didn't, it will execute it no matter when the system was powered on. And now for the at utility, we saw that cron and Anacron are focused on repetitive and automated tasks. In contrast at is focused on tasks that should only run once.","Now, the syntax of a cron job might be hard to remember for the first few times you try to set one up, but you can get a quick reminder by taking a look at this file. This is Etsy Crontab. This is the default systemwide cron table and the commented lines tell you about the syntax you should use if you ever need to add a cronjob here, add a new line at the end of the file. For example, you could use a line like this. The first five values tell cron when this should run.","The sixth value is a username and this is followed by the command that should run. Cron will execute this as if the user specified in the username field launched that task. The thing about adding jobs to this default systemwide table is that in certain cases, the package manager might overwrite this file and we might lose the changes we've added. For example, this could happen when the package manager upgrades cron. So in our lesson, we'll learn how to add cron jobs by editing a user's personal cron table instead of the systemwide table.","Just keep in mind when you're managing servers to first look up cron jobs in the Etsy crontab systemwide table. And afterwards look at the cron table belonging to the root user. We'll soon learn how to do that. But first, let's dive into the first five values here that establish when the cron job should run in the first two fields, we pick the time minute between zero and 59 hour, between zero and 23. In the next two fields, we pick the day of the month, between one and 31 and the month between one and 12.","And in the last field, we picked the day of the week between zero and 60 being Sunday, one being Monday two being Tuesday and so on. This can be a bit confusing since we may feel it should naturally be a number between one and seven. So cron accepts the alternate notation too with one being Monday and seven being Sunday. Instead of fixed numbers in these fields, we can also use alternate characters, asterisk or star matches all values possible in that field. So a star for the hour field means run it every hour, 012, all the way to 23.","The comma can be used to specify or enumerate multiple values for that field. For example, if we want something to run at minute, 15 and minute 45 we could type in 15 comma 45 with no spaces in the first field. And the dash can be used to specify a range. For example, if we want to schedule a job to run at night at hours 23 and 4 a.m. we could type this in the second field.","2-4 front slash specifies a step. For example, if you have an asterisk in the hour field, cron will step through hours one by one. So by default, the step is one first, the job will run at zero AM, then 1 a.m. then 2 a.m. and so on.","But if we want it to run every four hours, we need to change the step to four to do this. We would type in asterisk front slash four. The job will now run at zero AM, then 4 a.m. then 8 a.m. 12 AM and so on.","We can also combine ranges with these steps. For example, let's say we want to run something at only these three hours, zero AM 4 a.m. and 8 a.m. We could type in 0-8 front slash four in the hour field 0-8 specifying the range from zero AM to 8 a.m. and slash four telling cron to step through this range in four hour increments.","With theory alone, this kind of stuff can look a bit abstract and hard to digest. So let's make it crystal clear by looking at some practical examples. We'll add a simple cron job that just creates a file. This way, we can see if the file is created at a certain time. And we'll know that our job executed correctly.","In cron jobs, we can execute either commands or shell scripts and we'll use the touch command here. The first thing that's required is to find the full path to this command. So again, we're doing this by typing in which touch. It's important to remember that in Cronjobs, we should always use the full path to a command. We mentioned that we'll edit the user's cron table instead of the system wide Chron table at Etsy Crontab to edit a user Chron table.","We can use the E edit option in the Chron tab command. We'll type in Chron tab. E this will edit the table of your current user. Whoever you're currently logged in as to run the touch command and create a file called test underscore past every day at 6:35 a.m. we would add this line.","You might notice that there's no field specifying a username. Now as we had in the systemwide cron table. This is not necessary here because this time cron sees who the table belongs to and knows to run commands as that user. Now, we can save our file and exit the text editor and that's all there is to adding a cronjob to give you ideas about alternate time settings for a cronjob. Here are some other lines that we could have used to run this every Sunday at 3 a.m.","or we could use this notation to run it at the middle of every month on the 15th at 3 a.m. We would use this to run it every day at 3 a.m. We use this and to run it once at every hour when the minute is 00, we'd use this. If you want to practice and get used to writing cron expressions, you can visit a website with the address www dot crontab dot guru. To see the crontab of the current user use the L list option with crontab L to remove your user's crontab entirely.","Use the R remove option to remove the cron table of a different user who would type in this an alternative way to set up cron jobs is through the use of these special directories daily, hourly, monthly and weekly. Whatever cron finds in here, it will run daily, hourly, monthly or weekly. Let's see how that works. Now, imagine we have a shell script in our local directory. Simply called shell script.","The shell script file should have no extension if we want to use it as a cron job. For example, don't use a name such as shell script dot sh. If you intend to use this with cron to make this run hourly, we would follow this procedure first copy the script to Etsy cron dot Hourly. We need root privileges since only the root user can write to this directory. Next, make sure the script is readable and executable.","So cron can run it and later on if you need to remove this job, simply remove the file. Now, let's move on to the next utility for scheduling jobs on some Ubuntu systems. Anacron might not be installed by default, but we can install it with the command pseudo apt install Anacron. When using Anacron, we don't really care what time the job will run. We just want it to run daily monthly or once every few days no matter what the time of day is.","So to schedule a job with Anacron, we would edit the Etsy Anacron tab file, there are a few helpful hints in this file. First is that we can consult the Anacron tab manual in section five to see more details about the syntax here. So we can get some help with a command like man five Anachro tab. Secondly, we already have three Aron entries at the end of this file showing us some live examples. And the syntax is quite simple.","We have four fields in an Aron tab file. So this line makes the job run once every three days with a delay of 10 minutes and the job identifier or its name would be test job and then the command and the command in this job will run as the root user. If we wanted to run it every week, we could write this. And if we wanted to run every month, we would write it this way. Now, after we save our file, we should verify, if we use the correct syntax, we can do that by passing the T or test option to the Anacron command to verify the syntax of our Anacron job is correct.","We use the T test option like this and we'll get a message if there are errors. But if it's correct, we'll get no message. As mentioned in the intro, cron and Anacron are meant to run jobs that repeat periodically. And with the command, it's much easier to schedule jobs. We just specify the time that we want the job to run.","For example, to have something run at 15 o'clock, we just type in at 15, then we type the commands that we want to run at that time. For example, here's our user bin touch file created by at and notice that we also use the full path to the command here. Although in the case of at this is not an absolute requirement, we could just type touch without proceeding that with the user bin path. After each command, we want this job to run, we press enter if we need to, we could add a second command and a third one and so on. After pressing enter on the last command on the next empty line, we would press control D to save this job.","We can also schedule this to run on a specific date if we want to run it on a specific date. But also at a specific time, we can do that too. And we can also use relative times and dates to run this 30 minutes later, we would use this and to run it three hours later, we could run this. And as you may have guessed, we could do the same thing with days, weeks or months. Note how we have to wrap our time and date specifications between single quotes.","Now to see what jobs are currently scheduled to run with at, we can use the A TQ command or ATQ. The first number in the output is a job id, which will be used in our next commands later on. We might forget what a job was supposed to do. So we can use the dash C or C option, which is like the cat command that prints the contents of a file to display what an Atob contains. Pretty cool.","Right. We'll see a lot of output. But the commands that we added to this job are included in the last lines and to remove a scheduled out job, we can use its job identifier at the end of the ATRM command. For example, ATRM one"],"Operations Deployment/9":["In this lesson, we'll learn how to install and manage software on Ubuntu. A Linux makes it easy to install, remove and upgrade software using a package management system. Let's explore how these systems work on Ubuntu through some practical examples to manage software on Ubuntu, we use the app command app stands for advanced package tool. Now, before installing or upgrading anything, we run pseudo app update and this refreshes the local database of available packages. This information is downloaded from repositories which are servers that store package information and the packages themselves.","So why is this needed? The package management system maintains a database of software packages that it knows about. So this information becomes outdated. For example, if we last ran the app update command one week ago, we only have information about the packages that were available one week ago. So with this command, we can update or refresh the information on our machine.","The command downloads the updated information from the repositories. These repositories are servers that store information about software packages and the packages themselves and these are maintained by canonical. Now, let's say we want to install a new application. For example, engine X, we'll just type in pseudo apt install and Genex. Now notice the output shows that installing engine X also installs additional dependencies packages that the software needs to run correctly such as libraries or functionality enhancements.","So what is a package? A package is an archive containing the files needed by a piece of software. It may include binaries, configuration files, documentation, et cetera and it's all bundled together for easy installation. Now, after installing a package, we can view the files in the package using the DPKG tool or D package. For example, we can type in DPKG dash dash list, files and Genex and it shows the files in the engine X package.","And here we can see the actual engine X program is slash user slash Espin engine X and we can list all of the files in the engine XCORE package and you can do this in reverse as well to find out which package the user Espin engine X binary is associated with. We can type in DPKG dash search and then the file and we can see that it was brought in by the engine X core package. But what about other dependencies that are pulled in by engine X? We saw one called live engine X dash mod dash stream that was pulled in as a dependency. Now, if you're curious about the purpose of a certain package, we can run the following command a show and then the name of the package and you can see a description, this tells us what the package includes and what it does.","Now, in some cases, we might not know what package includes the software we need. So we need to search for it. For example, to look up all the packages that mention the word engine X, we can search for a search engine. However, this can produce a lot of matches. That's because it looks for the text engine X in both the package names and their descriptions.","Now, in this case, it's a good idea to exclude descriptions and search for packages based only in the package name. And to do that, we can type in app, search dash dash names, dash only engine X and that will only search for packages with the name that has the word engine X in it. And we can also look for packages that match multiple search words. For example, to look for packages that match all of these words, we can do app search engine X module image. Now the search function must match all of these words with a command like this.","We can locate the engine X package that contains the module needed to process images to remove a package that's no longer needed. The command is quite intuitive. We just do pseudo app remove in the name of the package in this case engine and we'll look at an alternative way to remove packages. And now instead of running two separate commands to remove the main package and all the dependencies. We'll do this with a single command.","We'll type in pseudo app, Autoremove and Genex. So this will remove the engine X package and all its dependencies. So we've covered the basics of using the package manager in Ubuntu. Let's move on to the next lesson."],"Operations Deployment/10":["In most cases, the software we need will be found in the official repositories provided by Ubuntu. We can see the default repositories configured for this system by looking at this file. Now, usually that's true. But I'm using Ubuntu 24.04 which is the latest version of Ubuntu as of this recording. But in previous versions of Ubuntu, this is where this file is located.","But as you can see, we have a comment here that points to a new file name. So let's take a look at that one. Now, let's break this down line by line. The first line types specifies the type of repository being defined. Now in this case, Deb indicates that this is a Debian style repository which is the package format used by Ubuntu.","This line tells the package manager for instance app to expect Debian packages in this repository. These dot Deb files are the packages themselves. They are archives containing files such as the program files, configuration files, documentation, scripts to be run and so on the next line specifies the uniform resource identifier or URI of this repository. And in this case, it's an HTTPS URL pointing to the Ubuntu archive mirror located in the United States because as you can see it's us.archive.ubuntu.com. Now the slash Ubuntu path at the end of this URL indicates that this repository contains Ubuntu packages.","This line tells the package manager where to download packages from. Now. Here we have suites. This line specifies the suites or release components of this repository in Ubuntu. A suite is a set of packages that are associated with a specific release or version of the distribution.","Our current version is Noble. Now, here we have three suites defined. We have Noble and this is the main suite for the Ubuntu released code named Noble Numbat. This suite contains the core packages for the release and we have Noble updates. This suite contains updates to the packages in the Noble suite.","These packages are usually bug fixes, security patches or minor feature enhancements. And we also have Noble Backport. This suite contains backported packages from newer Ubuntu releases. So right now, there aren't any newer ones but in the future there will be and these backport are packages that have been rebuilt from newer releases to work on older releases. So this allows you to access newer software without upgrading your entire system.","And next we have components. This line specifies the components of the repository in Ubuntu. Components are categories of packages that are grouped together based on their licensing functionality or other criteria. And here we have four components to find, we have main. This component contains packages that are free and open source software and are officially supported by Ubuntu restricted.","This component contains packages that are free and open source software but they may have some restrictions on their use or redistribution. And we have universe. This component contains packages that are free and open source software but not officially supported by Ubuntu Multiverse. This component contains packages that are not free and open source software and they may have other licensing restrictions. Now, usually on a server, we only need Maine and Universe, preferably only Maine if we can get away with it.","If we need something extra, not found in Maine, we can enable Universe as well if it's not already enabled. But as we can see by default, we have all four of these. Now let's quit the editor and look at a different scenario regarding repositories. In some cases, the software we need won't be included in the official Ubuntu repositories at all. In other cases, the software will be included in ubuntu's Universe section, but it might be a very old version and we might want the newest version for both of these scenarios, we can enable third party repositories.","That means software repositories that aren't maintained by Ubuntu but by a different team or company. However, they're still compatible with this operating system. Here's an example, let's say we want the latest stable version of Docker. Now, the first thing we need to do is download a public key. When the Docker team uploads a new package, they sign it with their private key and with the public key, our package manager can verify the signature.","This makes sure that the package hasn't been modified by any malicious users. So to download this, we'll use the cURL command and we'll pass the dash O option to save the file in a file name called Docker dot key. So we'll type in cURL and then the URL to the key and then dash O Docker dot key. Great. Next, we use the GP G command to transform this key from text format to a binary format that our package manager can understand.","This action is called de armoring in GP G terminology. This D armored key will be found in the Docker dot key dot GP G file. So GP G simply saved the D armored content in a file using the same name as our original file, but added the dot GP G extension to the end of it. Now, we'll have to move this into a directory belonging to our apt package manager, namely the Etsy app keyrings directory where it keeps thirdparty public keys to verify package signatures. Finally, we should add the Docker repository to App's configuration, but let's look at App's configuration directory first, instead of adding the new repository to the Ubuntu dot sources file that we looked at earlier, we can create a new config file in the sources dot list dot D directory.","That way we keep things separate and organized. Ubuntu dot Sources will contain the default entries and sources dot List dot D directory will contain one file for each new repository we add to the system. This keeps things really organized and neat. And when we don't need the third party repository anymore, we can just delete that config file from the directory. Now, older methods kept everything in one file which can make it enormous and huge and hard to go through.","So this is a more organized way of doing things. Let's create a new file in that folder called Docker dot list. And we'll add in this configuration line. Now we can see that this follows a different format from what we saw in the Ubuntu sources file. This follows the older convention of the sources dot list file, the Deb keyword followed by the repository address, the code name for the distribution and the components that we want to cherry pick from for that repository.","And in this case, we use the stable component to access the stable versions of Docker software. But one more thing is required here, namely the path to the public key used to verify signatures for packages downloaded from Docker. So we'll add this part in the following spot right after the Deb keyword. Now the instructions for all of these steps are usually mentioned on the website that provides access to the third party repository. So we'll save the file and write it.","Now, after we save the file, the last thing we want to do is update our package manager database again. This way, app downloads new information from the Docker repository and gets up to date with the latest packages available there. Now to confirm everything, we can look for a line that mentions the address for the repository we just added and we can see the Docker repository here in this output. So everything works smoothly. If you get an error mentioning that signatures can't be verified, then the D armored public key hasn't been configured correctly.","Now, another type of repository, Ubuntu lets you add are called PPAS short for personal package archive. These simplify the whole process of adding a third party repository and they also allow you to create your own third party repository. For example, you can create a PP A and pick John as your username, then name your repository, my latest app. After you upload your packages there, other people can add this PP A with the ad app repository command. The next argument in this command starts with PP A colon to tell this utility that this is a personal package archive.","Then followed by the user named John and the repository, my latest app. But let's modify this to add a real PP A and that's all the command takes care of all the other details like modifying Ubuntu dot sources files, adding the public key used for verifying package signatures, updating the package manager's database and so on to list any PPAS we might have enabled, we can use this command and pass the dash dash list option, add apt repository, dash dash list. And to remove a PP, we simply pass the remove option followed by the same PP specification that we used before. So in here, we'd use the same command but we type in dash dash remove and then press enter to confirm that covers configuring the repositories of the package manager. Now let's move on to our next lesson."],"Operations Deployment/11":["We saw how to install software from ubuntu's repositories, but also from third party repositories. Now, let's take a look at a third method of installing software to get the absolute latest version of some application. In some cases, we'll simply have to download the code from a place like github and compile it ourselves. We'll have to convert it from human readable text code to executable code that our computers can run efficiently. So let's install git first.","Now you might notice that it's already installed. In fact, in most versions of Ubuntu and Linux, it's already there. Well, we're going to download the git repository for an application called HTOP. And so we'll type in git clone and then the github URL. Now let's switch to the project's directory and we'll take a look at the files in here and will notice a readme file.","Every project has different compilation requirements, different libraries, it needs different commands to run to get some clues about what we need to do to get this to work. We can look at the instructions in this file. Now, I already read the instructions here. So here's what we need to do first we install a library and some compilation tools. Now, the library is included in the live ncurses W five dash DEV package.","Now, since the name starts with LIB and it ends with dash DEV, it suggests that this is a package containing a library used for development purposes. Hops code relies on this library to draw the application interface. So that's why we need it here. And the build essential package is almost always needed as it pulls in most of the compilation tools that we require. And if we take a look at the directory again, we'll notice an executable file marked with a green color Autogen dot sh.","Now in the Readme file, we've been instructed to run this to execute this shell script. We type a period sign to tell our bash shell. We want to run a file from within this current directory. So type in a period and in a slash with the path to the file, this will generate a configuration script. And if we type we can see it here.","So this file is simply called configure and it helps us configure how we want to build this application. For example, what features of the app we want to enable or disable plus other things. And we can type in not configure help. And this will give us a lot of the configuration options that we need. If you want to configure it with the default options, you simply run the script with no arguments.","Now, besides the configuration tasks, this script also verifies if some compilation requirements are met by this system, everything is in order. So we can continue to our next step, we can use the make utility to compile and build our final application. Now make also accepts additional arguments. But what arguments it supports depends on the project we're compiling and to see what arguments we can pass to make, we can use the familiar trick we type in make and then space and press tab two times 127 possibilities here. And these are called Make targets.","Now, not everything we see here is a valid target though, but for the ones that are valid, not only do they work here, they'll be available in most of the other projects as well. For example, the targets clean, this clean install and uninstall clean can be useful when things are not going according to plan. A scenario is when the compilation fails midway through and we get some inconsistent results. It's basically like a half built project to clean everything up and start again. We'll enter a command like make clean and then fix the problem and recompile our project and we'll soon see what make install is used for as well.","Generally, if all we want to do is compile and build this app, we just run make without any arguments. So let's do that. Now, if we take a look at this directory. Again, we'll see. The executable hop has been compiled into a binary called HTOP.","It's right here and we can run it by typing dot slash HTOP. Great. Now, let's press Q to quit the app. Now, the disadvantage here is that we need to type the relative or full path to this utility every time we need to use it. For example, if I was in another directory, I'd have to run home Jeremy H top htop to run this application and that's inconvenient.","So we can instruct the make command to install this application in a dedicated directory. Now, we'll see that the binary HTOP file was moved to the user local bin directory. So now every time we want to run this app, no matter where we are, we would type in htop. Let's test it out. I'll go to my home directory and type in HTOP.","That's because by default, every user shell on an Ubuntu system is configured to also look for applications in the slash user slash local slash bin directory. So we don't need to type in the full path to the utility anymore. And this wraps up our current lesson. I'll see you in the next one."],"Operations Deployment/12":["In this lesson, we'll take a look at how to verify the integrity and availability of important resources and processes. As time goes by a server will usually use more and more resources. Storage space is a good example. As the database grows, as users store more and more files, more and more gigabytes of storage space are used in the cloud. It's relatively easy to add more disks or ssds to a server.","But how do we know when we're running out of storage space? Well, we can use the DF Diskfree utility but this output is a bit hard to read. For example, we can see how much space is used on a disk. But we see this in one kilobyte blocks, we can tell DF to make this output a bit more human readable with the dash H option. Now, sizes are displayed in megabytes, gigabytes and terabytes.","Instead where we see the letters MG or T, we can ignore the file systems that contain the word T MP FS. Those are virtual file systems that only exist in the computer's memory, not on storage devices. In our case, we can see that we have two real file systems. The one mounted on front slash is the root file system where our Linux operating system is installed, the one mounted on slash boot is a small file system where boot files are installed. Now, we'll learn what slash DEV slash mapper and slash DEV slash BD A two mean in one of our later lessons where we discuss how to manage storage devices, we can see that 3.9 gig are used on our root file system and we have 5.4 gig free.","Also, since 43% of our file system is used, that means we still have plenty to spare around 57% of our free space to see how much disk space a specific directory is using. We can use the DU disk usage. Utility dash S is the summarize option. This makes du display disk space used by the entire slash user directory alone. Without the dash S option.","DU would show us disk space used by that directory and also every subdirectory it contains the H makes a display sizes in human readable format, megabytes, gigabytes and so on. Now let's move on to the server's ram or random access memory to see how much memory is utilized we can use the free command. And once again, we use the dash H option to make the command display sizes and megabytes gigabytes and so on. Technically speaking, these are actually mebibytes and gigabytes. That's why we see the letters M I and G I here, one mebibyte is actually equal to two times 20 or 1 million, 48,576 bytes and one megabyte is equal to 1 million bytes.","In this output, we see that we have around 3.6 gigabytes usable memory and 1.0 gigabytes are currently used. But how much memory is free and ready to be used by programs? That's the amount we see in the column titled available. Not the one named free, which can be a bit confusing. But the technical explanation is that some memory is used, hence not actually free.","And the operating system can make it available at any time. For example, if we read a one gigabyte file, the operating system will temporarily store it in memory for faster access the next time we need it. But if a program needs that one gigabyte of memory, the operating system will free it up immediately. So even though that one gig was used and not free, it's still available any time it's needed. Now, let's look at how the processor is used with the uptime command.","We can see how the CPU cores were used by programs running on our server. The important information here is the three numbers that we see after load average. The first number tells us that the load average for the last one minute. The second one tells us the load average for the last five minutes. And finally, the third number refers to last 15 minutes.","So if we see a load average of 1.0 is our first number. It means that one CPU core has been used at full capacity in the last one minute. On average, otherwise said, 100% of that CPU core was used intensely in the last minute. Since it's an average, this could mean 100% utilization for an entire minute. But it could also mean that for 30 seconds, 0% was used.","And for the other 30 seconds, 200% was used. That is two cores that would still lead to an average of 100% utilization displayed as 1.0 in this field, if we see 2.0 as our last number, it means that on average two CPU cores were fully utilized at 100% in the last 15 minutes. Now, let's say we have eight CPU cores on the system and we see this load average, that would mean that six CPU cores were used intensely in the last minute. But in the last five and 15 minutes, the CPU cores were barely used. So it's something that happened recently.","Some programs worked hard to do something for a very brief time. But overall the system is not pushing the CPU too much. So we shouldn't be too concerned. But if we see averages like this, then this would tell us that some programs use our CPU CORS heavily almost all the time, it's time to upgrade to a more powerful server or optimize our setup so that it requires fewer CPU resources. If we need to see some details about the CPU used on this system, we can run this command LSCPU.","And if we need to see some details about other hardware on the system, we can type LS PCI. Now let's jump into the integrity of file systems to check a file system for errors. We first must unmount it. In case it's mounted, we'll learn more about file systems, partitions, mounting and unmounting in the storage section of this course, operating systems from the red hat family tend to use XFS file systems by default. And operating systems from the Ubuntu family use the EXT four file system.","But this can change in the future for both of them to verify an XFS file system. We'd use a command like this. Now dash B turns on verbose, output verbose means that more details will be displayed about what the command is doing. Dash B is not required, but it's a useful option to throw in there to see what's going on slash DEV slash B db. One points to the partition where this file system is stored on.","In this case, it points to the first partition on the second virtual storage device. In a real scenario. This could be something entirely different such as slash DEV slash VD A two or slash DEV slash S DC three depending on where the file system you want to scan is located and we'll learn how to decode those device names later on to check and repair an ext four file system. We can use a command like this. Now, this is fsckext four and dash V turns on verbose output dash F forces a check even if the file system reports that it's healthy dash P turns on the so-called pre mode.","This lets the utility fix some simple problems automatically without asking us any questions. But if serious errors are encountered, yes, no questions will still be asked. Dash V and dash F are optional. If you can't remember all these options, at least remember to use the dash P option. That one is more important.","If you have a file system with many errors, it can help you avoid typing. Yes to dozens of questions and instead they'll be fixed automatically. You could think about ext four file system problems to remember the dash P option. Finally, how do we make sure that key processes important programs are working correctly on our system? First, we can use this command system CTL list dash dependencies.","This will show us a treelike structure of important system D units that are active and inactive. A transparent or white circle next to the unit name means that this particular unit is currently inactive. Usually that's no issue. Some units or services run once at boot and then exit. Others are not meant to automatically start at boot time just when they're needed.","But there are some exceptions. Some service units are very important and should actually run at all times. An example is the SSH service unit that controls the life cycle of the SSH Daemon. If this service shows up as inactive, it would mean that SSH daemon is also inactive, which would mean that administrators wouldn't be able to connect to this operating system remotely if they need to manage it. A green circle next to a unit name means it's currently active.","So we generally want to see this green circle next to all important services meant to run all the time. For example, besides the SSH dot service, we also want the cron dot service and the A TD dot service to be active, otherwise scheduled cron jobs and A T jobs wouldn't run automatically. Now, let's purposely terminate our at daemon to simulate an issue with a service that should generally be active at all times. Now, in this command systemctl list dependencies, we see a white light next to the A TD service which signals a problem for such an important service scheduled A T jobs won't be executed to start investigating what went wrong. We can begin with this comment system CTL status A TD service.","This will confirm that the service is currently inactive. The progress that should run is dead or nonexistent and the last log line will show us when the A TD service was deactivated. So no serious errors that we need to fix. And also we can see the service is enabled to automatically start each time the system boots, which means that our job is rather easy. All we need to do is start the service again.","In a real scenario, things might be slightly more complex. But the log lines in the system CTL status command usually point to what went wrong and why the service cannot start? Which means that first, the problem pointed in the logs should be fixed and only afterwards, can we try to start the service again? If the logs in the system CTL command don't show the real cause of the problem. Remember from our previous lesson, we can dive deeper and see all logs generated by this service with a simple journal CTL command.","For example, to see logs for the A TD dot service unit, we just run this command."],"Operations Deployment/14":["In this lesson, we'll explore Se Linux context applied to files and processes. We saw that Linux supports some rudimentary forms of access control files and directories have permissions that establish who can read, write or execute content. Also, regular users are not allowed to do certain things that the root user can do. But these forms of security are not enough for today's world of sophisticated cyber attacks. They're just too generic, not finetuned enough and a simple read or write permission gives a user or process too much power over a particular area of the system.","Fortunately, the Linux kernel can be easily extended with so-called modules and there's a security module called Se Linux that adds a very advanced capabilities of restricting access. Now, Se Linux is preconfigured and enabled by default on operating systems from the red hat family, but it's not enabled by default on a boon to and the initial configuration on a boon two can be a bit tricky, but we'll take a deeper look at Se Linux and Ubuntu in one of our next sessions for. Now, let's see how this Linux security module works at a surface level. Se Linux allows for very fine grained control of what actions should be allowed or denied. Now, how does it make such decisions?","We saw how command like LS dash L shows us what permissions are enabled or disabled for a file or directory in output like this, we can tell from the string RW dash, rw dash R dash dash, how permissions are set for this file? Se Linux employs a similar mechanism just like we can see permissions for files and directories. With the dash L option, we can use a different option to see Se Linux file directory labels. And we do that by passing the dash Z option to our LS command and we'll see output like this. Now, this part is called the Se Linux context and it's also sometimes just called a label because at a file system level, this is simply a label applied to the file, just some additional metadata glued on top of it.","And by looking at the label, Se Linux can decide what the security context is for this file and what kind of actions it should allow. Although these vaguely resemble file directory permissions, they're much more complex. So let's take a quick look at what this context tells us. First, these context components are always displayed in this order, user raw Tate and level. So in our case, the unconfined underscore U is the user object underscore R is the role user underscore home underscore T is the type and zero is the level to allow or deny some action.","Se Linux roughly follows this logic first, it looks at the user part of the label. This is not the same as the username you log in with. It's an Se Linux user. Every user who logs into a Linux system is mapped to an Se Linux user as a part of the Se Linux policy configuration. After the user is identified, a decision is made to see if it can assume the role, each user can only assume a predefined set of roles.","For example, we can imagine how a developer underscore U user should only be able to enter roles like developer underscore R or Docker underscore R that allow them to read and write application code and launch containers and so on. But they should not be able to enter roles like sysadmin underscore R that let them change system settings if the role can be entered. Next, the check is performed to see if this role can transition to the type. The name of this field comes from type enforcement. This type is like a protective software jail.","Once something enters the type enforcement defined here, it can only do certain things and nothing else. The fourth part, the level is almost never actively used on regular systems. That's a better fit for complex organizations that require multiple levels of security. An example of this imagine Jane is a high ranking military officer, she has a high clearance level and will be able to read documents classified from level zero to level four with level four being top secret files. On the other hand, a user with level one access can only access stuff on level one and level zero, but access to anything above level one is restricted.","So we notice how we can basically go from user to roll to type to enter into some kind of restricted secure box going through this three step path. Se Linux achieves three important things. It makes sure that only certain users can enter certain roles and certain types. So it keeps unauthorized users and processes from entering types or domains that are not for them. It lets authorized users and processes do their job by granting the permissions they need.","However, at the same time, even authorized users and processes are allowed to take only a limited set of actions. Everything else is denied. Why is this important? Here's one example, it can protect against the hijacked program. Now imagine a hacker takes control of the Apache program that serves up web pages to our website visitors.","Even though the malicious user now controls the program, he or she is still restricted by Se Linux. The hijacked Apache application is confined to a certain security domain applied to that process. So it can only take actions allowed for that security domain. Hence, the hacker isn't able to step out of those boundaries. The attacker is effectively in a virtual jail.","And the damage that can be done is greatly reduced. And usually the most important part of Se Linux context is the type slash domain. The third part in our label here. And since we mentioned processes also get context like files, how do we explore what se Linux contexts are applied to them with the same slash Z option? To see all processes, we use the PSAX command and to add security labels to the output.","We add the Z option like this and we can see our SSH daemon running here and spot that it's restricted to the SSH D underscore T type enforcement domain. This domain contains a strict set of policies that define what this process is allowed to do. Also important to note not anything can enter the SSH underscore T domain in the default systemwide Selinux policy only files that have the SSH D underscore exec underscore Tse Linux type in their label can enter this domain. And sure enough, if we check the file containing the SSH D program, we'll see that it's indeed labeled with this type. Now to recap only a file marked with a certain type can enter a process that can shift into a certain domain.","This type establishes permitted actions for a file. The domain establishes permitted actions for a process. Now back to the output of our Psaxz command. Anything labeled with unconfirmed underscore T is running unrestricted. Se Linux lets those processes do almost anything they want back to the output of our Psaxz command.","Anything labeled with unconfined underscore T is running unrestricted. Se Linux lets those processes do almost anything they want. Here are some other useful commands we can use. Se Linux puts a security bubble around everything, even a user, not just a file or a process to see the security context assigned to our current user. What got applied to our user.","When we logged in, we can run ID dash Z. When we log in the user, we log in as is automatically mapped to an Se Linux user. To see how this mapping is done, we can use this command output. Like this means that when the root user logs in, they'll be mapped to the Se Linux user called unconfined underscore U. If any other user logs in that's not defined in this list, they'll be mapped to whatever we see on the underscore underscore default underscore underscore line.","In this case, also unconfined underscore U we can see that by default. Se Linux doesn't initially restrict the user in any way. Since they transition to this unconfined user on most systems, it's usually the role and type components of a context that start to restrict access. But for advanced use cases, things can be set up so that restrictions begin at the user level only if we see enforcing here. It means that it's doing its job and denying unauthorized actions.","Permissive means it's allowing everything. It's just logging actions that should have been restricted, but it's not actively denying anything. Finally, if we see disabled here, it means that Se Linux is not doing anything. It isn't enforcing security and it isn't even logging."],"Operations Deployment/15":["In this lesson, we'll explore how we can enforce mandatory access control with the help of the Se Linux security module. Now, Red Hat and Sento operating systems have Se Linux enabled by default. But on Ubuntu, a different security module called app Armor is used by default whenever we end up on a system that was set up by other people. If we need Se Linux, we should first check if it's already configured before we attempt to install anything. We'll explore how to check the security module status later in our lesson.","But since we know our system doesn't have Selinux, let's see how we can get it started from scratch on Ubuntu. Since app Armor is enabled by default, first, we'll have to disable that using two security modules at the same time might lead to conflicts or unexpected results. So first we need to stop the App Armor service, then we disable App Armor from automatically starting up when the system boots next to get started with Se Linux, we install these two packages. Se Linux basics and auditd. The Se Linux package will pull in all the Se Linux utilities we'll need and some default security policies.","The audit D package contains the audit daemon. This is the utility that will log certain system events and help us build additional Se Linux security policies, customized for our system to check if Se Linux is enabled on our system, we can run se status and as expected, it's disabled in our case to enable Se Linux on a system. A couple of things need to be set up first. The bootloader needs to be configured to tell the Linux kernel to load the security module. Also, every file on the system needs to be properly labeled because Se Linux uses labels for everything to decide what a certain object is allowed to do.","If we take a look at our system. Now we'll see that Se Linux labels are currently missing question marks appear instead of proper security labels. The good thing is that Ubuntu has a utility that can go through all the steps necessary to enable Se Linux and re label the file system. So the command that we need to run is pseudo E Linux activate. And we can see it modified the grub bootloader to instruct the Linux kernel to load the Se Linux module at boot time.","Let's look at some of the settings passed to the bootloader. This line instructs the bootloader to pass an argument to the Linux kernel, which in turn will load Se Linux. And if we take a look at the root file system telling Ls to show us the hidden dot files, we'll see a special file called auto re label that instructs Se Linux to re label every file with the property security labels when we reboot. So let's do that. The relabeling process takes a while.","The more files there are on a file system, the longer it's going to take. And after the reboot, the bootloader is configured to wait for 30 seconds. This is a measure to help us step in and fix the boot process in case something went wrong. So for the first boot, after enabling Se Linux is successful, this delay for the bootloader is removed. So Linux will boot normally the next time and now we can SSH back in and things should be different.","First of all, the files should have proper security labels. And if we check the status of Se Linux, again, we will see different output this time. Notice that the Se Linux status mentions that our security model is enabled. But under current mode, we can see that it's in a permissive state. That's because Se Linux can run in two modes, permissive or enforcing.","We'll see what each one does. Now, another way to check the CF Selinux is running in permissive or enforcing mode is to use the get enforce command when Se Linux is enabled. But in permissive mode, it's not enforcing security rules. It's just observing every action on our system passively. We can think of this as sort of a learning mode.","It's not disallowing any action, but it logs what its current security policy would normally deny and it logs these potential security violations in the audit log. That's why we installed the audit daemon earlier. Now, in a real scenario, we'd have to run se Linux in permissive mode for a while. We can think of this as training it. We let it observe all of the typical actions that happen on this system every day.","Once we think it observed everything, we can go to the next step, creating a security policy, custom tailored for our system. At this point. If we were to switch Se Linux to enforcing mode, it wouldn't even allow us to log in through SSH. But because it's in learning mode, the audit log recorded the actions it observed when we logged in, we can take a look at what Se Linux recorded in the audit log with this command. The dash dash all option tells the audit two Y command to show all logged events, we'll pipe the output to less so that we can scroll through it and search for text.","Let's search for parts that contain the text. SSH. We'll see many matches here like this one note that the part mentioning AB C colon denied AB C is short for access vector cache. This simply shows that the action that Se Linux observed here would normally be denied by the current security policy. Also notice the SSH D underscore T label, basically a process with this label tried to do something that is not permitted.","But since se Linux is in permissive mode, the action was logged but not disallowed. Also notice that something called a type enforcement rule is disallowing this for the SSH D underscore T context. And a tool called audit two allow is recommended to fix this and we'll get to that soon. Let's exit from this log. And let's see what processes are labeled with SSH D underscore T, we can make use of the PS command and pass it the dash E parameter to show all processes and the dash Z parameter to show their Se Linux context.","Basically how they're labeled by Se Linux to remember these parameters for PS. Just think of the word easy since the letters E and Z are pronounced easy. Let's also pass the output to grep to filter out only things labeled with SSH D underscore T we can see that the SSH D process is labeled with this SSH D underscore Tse Linux type. Now, if we take a look at how the file for the SSH daemon is labeled, we'll notice this. The file is labeled with the SSH D underscore exec underscore T type.","And when Se Linux runs something labeled with the SSH D underscore exec underscore T type, the process will be transitioned into a domain labeled SSH underscore T A domain is a sort of security bubble that confines this process and it allows it to do only what is defined under the SSH D underscore T type enforcement rules. Basically because the file is labeled a certain way. Se Linux knows what restrictions it should apply to the process that starts up currently, processes restricted in the SSH D underscore T domain can't do enough. But if we set Se Linux to enforcing mode, we wouldn't even be able to log in. But since it observed some actions, when we logged in earlier, we can use another command to tell Selinux to enable everything it witnessed up to this point.","And my module is simply a name we choose for this security package file. We could call it anything we like. Just use a good name to remember the purpose of this module in the future in the output, we even get a suggestion on how to load these rules into Se Linux. So let's run this command. The I option here is used to tell the semodule command to install this module package into Selinux.","We may notice this message depending on the Se Linux version used. This seems to be a bug in the current version. The default policy comes with a predefined Se Linux user named SDDM, but we can ignore this message since we're not using SD dbm, which is simple desktop display manager. Anyway. Now remember that in the current scenario, Se Linux is still running in permissive mode to make it actually enforce the security policies.","We can run this command and then we can check if it's actually enforcing the rules with Getenforce. And it shows enforcing mode looks like it's doing its job in a real scenario. At this point, we would test everything on our server to see if things still work as expected since Se Linux denies everything by default. If the current security policy doesn't have all the necessary allow rules, some programs will be prevented from doing their job. That's why it's a good idea to put Se Linux into enforcing mode only when we're sure the policies include everything that's required.","It's useful to know that using the get Andfor command puts Se Linux into enforcing mode only temporarily. Once we reboot, it will go back into permissive mode for the first test run. It's a good idea to enable it temporarily this way, that way if anything goes wrong, we can reboot our server and start over from permissive mode. When we're certain our policies are complete, we can permanently put Se Linux into enforcing mode to do that. We'll edit this file and we just change this line from permissive to enforcing.","Now, when we reboot, se Linux will start in enforcing mode note that if the Se Linux security policies are not inclusive enough, we might not be able to log back in or use the system normally. So we should only enable this when we're sure that everything is set up correctly. Now, let's get back to the module we generated with the audit to allow command. We can see that two files were actually created. We can explore the mymodule dot PP file since it's in a binary format that makes it easy to plug into Se Linux PP is short for policy package.","However, a second file is generated where we can see the rules in a text format. The dot te extension is short for type enforcement. If we explore the mymodule dot te file, we can get an idea about the Se Linux security policy generated by audit two. Allow. Now in a real scenario, we'd have to scan this file thoroughly and explore everything this would allow in the system before actually deploying these rules to Se Linux.","It is a tedious process and customization might be necessary along with having Se Linux documentation at hand. It's also a good idea to create a separate policy package file for each object we want to secure. Since this file we got from audit to allow groups rather random things together. Let's look at our SSHD underscore T section here. At a first glance, this content can seem pretty intimidating, but let's try to simplify and understand at least one of these lines.","For example, this one we mentioned that Se Linux confines processes into security bubbles called domains and a domain is labeled with a certain type, the type applied to the process decides what rules to enforce. And that's why it's called type enforcement. In this case, SSH D underscore T is the name of this type. So in this policy, we tell Se Linux that processes in the SSH D underscore T domain type should be allowed to do certain things. So on this line, we simply tell it that processes under this domain should be able to take certain actions on files labeled with the bar underscore log underscore T type.","In this case, they should be allowed to create open and edit such files. Now, let's exit and see why we have this line. If we take another look at the Selinux labels or context for our SSH daemon process, we'll see that it's confined in the SSH D underscore T domain type and the SSH daemon has to log its activity in the slash bar slash log slash A dot log file. If we look at Se Linux labels for that file, we'll notice that the bar underscore log underscore T type label is applied to it. Now, let's connect the dots.","Remember the Se Linux rule we explored earlier. Let's refresh our minds. So in the policy generated by audit to allow with this line, it told Se Linux to allow processes under the SSH D underscore T domain to create and write files labeled with the bar underscore log underscore T type. We know that in Linux, we can set permissions on files and control what users can read, write or execute them. Well, with Se Linux, we get even more fine grain control.","We can limit what files a specific process should be able to access this way. Even if someone takes control of say an engine X web server process, they won't be able to read and modify system files. They will be able to only touch files that have specific labels targeted for an HTTP daemon. Now let's clear our screen and make this easier to read. And let's look at the label of this file.","Again, the user label is system underscore you. The role label is object underscore R and the type label is bar underscore log underscore T note how the last letter denotes. If it's a user role or type, these three parts can find every file and every process into what Se Linux calls a security context. That's why the command used to change. This context is called ch con.","It's simply an abbreviation for change context. Now to change the user label, we pass the U option and the label we want to use. For example, unconfined U, we type in pseudo chcon U, unconfined underscore U and then the file to change the role, we pass the R option and to change the type, we pass the dash T option. Usually we'll only need to work with the type. Se.","Linux can also enforce certain limitations based on the user trying to perform an action and checking if that user can transition to certain roles. But this is not enforced by default. Since when we log in, we're associated with an Se Linux user called unconfined underscore U which can access almost everything the user role and type that we set with CH con needs to be valid Se Linux labels. So we can use only the things that are predefined in our security policies. But what's a valid label?","Well, we could find out if we go through a huge part of the documentation but a shortcut is to use commands like this. The se info, you will show the valid Se Linux user labels. R will show roles and dash T will show types. But to understand what actions are allowed by each type, we have to go through a large part of the documentation. It's just easier to look at files with similar purposes and figure out what type we might need for a certain scenario.","In fact, we can explore this option because at this point, the label for our all thought log file is messed up since we changed it with the CHK command and used random values that don't make sense for a log file with these incorrect labels. The SSH daemon won't be able to write to the file anymore. So how do we fix this? Well, we have two ways. First, we can look at the labels of other files in the varlog directory.","If we're lucky, we'll see other off dot log files such as a thought log 0.1 which have correct labels or we can notice a similar files like CIS log. And we can guess that a thought log would need to be labeled just like the CIS log files since they have a similar purpose. But manually setting each label part for the user role and type would be tedious. So we can set all three in one shot. We can tell the chcon command to use another file as a reference and copy all the label parts from the good files to the bad file.","And for this, we'll use a dack log. We can tell CHCON command to use another file as a reference and copy all of the label parts from the good file to the bad file. So I'm going to say that the DMES file is the good file that should be used as a reference. So here we're using that as a reference and applying it to the A dot log file. If we take another look at the labels, we can see this is now fixed.","But how about new files and directories? Let's say we create a website and store its files in the default path for this purpose in the slash bar slash www directory. So let's create the directory and then let's create 10 new files in here with Se Linux enabled the Apache or engine X web server won't be able to work with these files, if they don't have the correct labels. So what labels do they have now? Now this doesn't look right.","Usually, Se Linux type labels are quite suggestive indicating a specific purpose in the name they use bar underscore T looks pretty generic. So in this case, it will be hard to guess what the label should be here, but there's a command that can help us out. Se Linux has a sort of database that it tells it how to label files in certain directories. In case the labels are incorrect, we can fix it with the restorecon command short for restore context. We'll use the R parameter to take this action recursively on the entire directory and any subdirectories or files that may contain.","Se Linux has a sort of database that tells it how to label files in certain directories. In case the labels are incorrect, we can fix it with the restorecon command short for restore context. We'll use the dash R parameter to take this action recursively on the entire directory and any subdirectories or files that may contain and let's check if this worked. Uh huh Well, HTTP D underscore sis underscore content underscore T seems like a much more suggestive and targeted label this time referencing that this is for system content for the HTTP daemon. But something that might not be immediately obvious is that restorecon by default, only restores the correct label for the type.","For example, let's say we change the user label for one of these files and set it to staff underscore U if we run restorecon again, recursively, we'll see that the user label wasn't restored file, one still references the wrong user because restorecon does not touch user and role labels. Just the last part, the type to force restorecon to restore all labels for the user role and type, we can pass the F force option. Now, if we check our files again, we'll see that the parts of our labels have been changed to Se Linux system defaults for this directory. Now, here's a note changing labels with CH Con has a possible drawback if the file system is ever relabeled again. Like it was when we installed Se Linux, the custom label we set on a file can be lost.","So to futureproof our change, we can do it in a different way. Let's say we want file 10 to always have a different type label instead of Ch Con. We can run this command and there's that message again, but that's ok. So we use the Se Linux manage command and inform it about a new default file context. We dash dash, add an entry into its database mentioning that the dash dash type label should be var underscore log underscore T for the VWWW 10 file.","This just tells it about the default label but it doesn't actually change it. So we have to follow up with this to restore the default label and we can confirm the change here for setting default labels on entire directories and the contents within the syntax is a bit trickier, but we can get a bit of help by listing Se Linux's entire database of default file context slash labels for various files and directories. And we can see this line which basically shows us how to use the regular expression with the parentheses to set a default label for a directory and all the subdirectories and files that may contain and the command we'll use is this note that we have to wrap the directory location between quotes to prevent bash from interpreting the special symbols. The asterisk is just a regular expression that matches any kind of file or subdirectory path we may have under that directory, we would then have to run restorecon to restore the default context we created for the location. But since the directory doesn't exist, let's first create it.","And now lets restore context and will see that the proper context has been set. Se Linux uses these labels to make decisions about what it should allow to happen on the system for the most part at least. But we do have some exceptions or rather shortcuts we can use, for example, instead of defining complex type enforcement policies, we can use what are called booleans. It's like flipping a switch to tell Se Linux to allow or disallow a set of actions. Here's an example, we can take a look at supported se Linux booleans with this command.","So on a line like this, we can see that the Vert underscore use underscore NFS boolean is currently disabled. The first off between parentheses shows us that this is currently set to false or zero. The second off signals, the default state of this boolean that's normally set to off. And we can see in the description what each boolean does to enable the Vert underscore use underscore NFS boolean. We can run this command to check the value of a certain boolean.","We can run this command. Another thing that Se Linux enforces is what ports, certain daemons can bind to, to listen to for incoming connections to list port bindings that are allowed for each type. We can run this command pseudo se manage port dash dash list. Now lets filter this to show port bindings only for SSH. So only port 22 is allowed if we want to allow an additional port like 22222, we can run something like this and to disallow this port or delete it from our rule set.","We just changed the dash dash ad to delete. This covers the surface level concepts about enforcing se Linux rules. I'd say it takes around 10 to 20 hours of study and practice to really understand Se Linux on a deeper level. It's a complex tool that can seem pretty overwhelming at first, but it's certainly worth learning more about in your spare time. Many attacks where millions of users accounts were compromised could have been prevented by a carefully designed Se Linux policy.","So if you're passionate about cybersecurity, it's something you'll want to look into. Good luck on your learning path and I'll see you in the next lesson."],"Operations Deployment/16":["Docker containers are very popular nowadays. But why to get a glimpse of their usefulness? Just think about a database server that we can create with maria DB. The classic way we install it with a pseudo app, install maria dash DD server command. Then we configure its settings by modifying files in the Etsy, mysql directory.","We create a few databases, we configure database users assign privileges and so on. Now let's say we're happy with our setup and we want to move it to a more powerful computer somewhere in the cloud. The problem is that all its components are scattered all over the place. The settings for Maria DB are in one directory. The databases are in some other directory logs in another one gathering and migrating all of these things can be a hassle.","But what if our maria DB setup would have been created in a Docker container instead. Now everything would exist inside this container, the daemon, the configuration files, the logs and the databases. All we would need to do is copy this container to some other computer and everything will work exactly the same as it did before containers, encapsulate applications. This makes them portable and easy to move around as easy as copying and pasting a file from one location to another. Once we're happy with a containerized application, we can deploy it to tens of different servers at scale.","The same thing that Kubernetes does. And part of the reason why it's so popular to understand how to work with Docker. Let's jump right into some practical examples. All right. Now, let's dive into our practical exercises.","Let's say we're Docker beginners and we have no idea what commands we have available. We can quickly get an overview of how to use the Docker utility with this command Docker dash dash help. And we notice the search subcommand and this can help us find the engine X image we need if we type clear and we'll type in Docker search engine and we've got a ton of these. Now, an image provides everything that is needed to create and start a container. In this case, it includes the engine X daemon, the software libraries that requires the configuration files, scripts and so on this first image seems to be the best match.","In our case, many people have added it to their favorites list. So it has a lot of stars. Furthermore, it's also marked as the official image. We'll need to download this image and pull it to our computer. We can use the name in our results list under the name column.","So the command will use this Docker Pole and Janette's in the previous screen. I did see an image from Ubuntu farther down the list. So we'll download that as well. Type Docker Pole, the Boon two Engine X. Now you'll notice in both of these where it says Engine X colon.","Latest. The last part latest is called the image tag. We can think of it as a label for a Tshirt in the store. We can buy the same Tshirt in different sizes like small, medium and large. So the labels on clothes can be SM or L and it's the same thing for container images.","The engine X image has many different variations. Now it's still engine X but in many different shapes and sizes. For example, there are multiple versions for the engine X build a tag like latest points to the latest versions available and a tag like 1.24 0.0 would point to the 1.24 0.0 version by providing the correct tag, we can pick the exact engine X build that we want. So how do we use this tag in a command? Well, let's pull an older version of engine X to see an example to get version 1.22 0.1 we'll use a command like this.","Now we're saying Dockerpool engine X colon 1.22 0.1. Now if we don't specify a tag like we did earlier, then Docker will assume that we want the latest version to be more accurate the image that is tagged as latest. And here we go. Now we've got an image for engine X 1.22 0.1. At this point, we have some images that we don't really need.","So how do we delete them? Well, first, let's see what images we have. OK. So as we're looking at this, we don't need this image and we don't need this one to delete the Ubuntu engine X image. We can use the RMI or remove image command.","There we go. Now we'll do Docker images. When we got two to delete the last image, we can't just provide its name alone. That's because both have the same name, Ingen X and Docker wouldn't know which one we want to remove. So we'll need to specify the tag as well and we can delete the old version of Ingen X with this command colon 1.22 0.1.","We set that tag and there we go. Now let's move on to the next part. How do we actually create and run a container quite easily with the Docker, run command followed by the image name. And the tag is optional. In our case, we'll type in Docker, run int this command creates the container and also runs the engine X daemon inside it.","But notice the problem. We're now stuck inside this container. We can see status messages generated in the container, but we can't type other commands and this is actually a common problem. Docker beginners run into before they get used to the workflow. Let's fix this problem first, we'll press control C to detach from this container's environment.","And unfortunately, this keyboard combination will also stop the container. OK? But let's create and run another one and we'll see how to do it correctly this time for this purpose, we'll use a few options. We'll start by typing Docker run, let me clear my screen here and then we'll add the command options that we need. And the first one is dash dash detach which will make Docker detach us from the container, so we don't get stuck inside it anymore.","The next one is dash dash publish 8080 colon 80 I'll type this in, ok. And this option will make our engine X daemon running in this container accessible to the outside world. Now, the option parameter 8080 colon 80 might look a little confusing, but this tells Docker to redirect network traffic. So anything that comes to port 8080 on our computer will be redirected to port 80 inside the container. So an easy way to think about this is just left to right.","We're going from the computer to the container and the final option we'll use is dash dash name and pick a name for our container. Otherwise, Docker will automatically generate names for us like relaxed leader beg and this would make it harder to identify which containers which when we have many of them running on the system. And then finally, at the end of our command, we'll specify the image we want to use, which in this case is engine X. When we run this Docker will generate a unique identifier for this container. And that's what we see in the output here.","Now, how can we see what containers are currently running? Well, let's get some help again. We'll type Docker help and then we'll scroll up here. Oh, look, here's the command to list containers. PS, so let's run Docker PS, everything seems to look ok here.","But remember the first container we created the one that we force exited with control C why is that not listed here? Well, because Docker PS only lists running containers but not the ones that are stopped. So how can we list all containers even those that aren't running? Well, let's get some help with Docker PS dash dash help. Ah dash A or dash all here is the option we're looking for.","So if we run Docker PS dash dash all. Ok. Now we've got a few of them in here. So now we could see our running container and then a couple of containers that just didn't work out. The status column shows that one is up, so it's running and the other ones have a status saying exited.","So that's stopped. Now to start our older container we can use a Docker start command. This can be followed by either the container name that we see in the last column here or the container ID that we see from the first column. So let's use a container ID, we'll say Docker start. All right.","Now if we run Docker ps ah we can see that both of them are running now under the ports column. We have one here that says 80 slash TCP. Let's stop this container that says 80 slash TCP under the ports column. We didn't publish ports for the first one we created. So the engine X web server is not easily accessible and we can use the Docker stop command followed by the name of the container.","And speaking of published ports, we can actually test if port 8080 is redirected to port 80 on our container. And for this purpose, we'll use the Netcat command. We type in NC localhost 8080 and this connects us to port 8080 on our local machine. And Docker should now redirect us to port 80 on the container. So we're practically connected to the engine X running in there.","And if we type in get front slash, we can send the get command to the web server which should return the index page in text format. In this case, it does this output confirms that our containerized engine X works and the port is indeed published to our host. We'll press control D to exit the NETCAT session. Now, let's make a note here when we first learn Docker, it can be a bit confusing about what's the difference between a Docker run command and a Docker start command. Both seem to simply start containers but Docker run is effectively building a container from an image and configuring it in a certain way.","And only afterwards it starts it Docker start doesn't build anything. It just starts a container that was previously created by the initial Docker run command. To remember this difference. Think about it this way if the container does not exist, I need the Docker run command to initialize one. If the container exists, I need the Docker start command.","Let's continue with our lesson. Now, how do we remove containers once again, let's list all of our containers. Even the ones that are stopped with Docker PS dash dash all to remove the stopped container. The one with the exited status. We can use the Docker RM command.","Let's remove determined Pearlman and we can do Docker RM. Lucid Feynman. An RM is short for remove and it's not to be confused with our previous Docker RM I command which removes images instead of containers. But speaking of images, let's explore an EdgeCase we might encounter in the real world say we want to remove the engine X image. Now, if we type in Docker RM I in X, we'll notice an error.","We can't remove this image because a container is still using it. We first have to remove the container or the containers depending on it. In this case, we should remove the container called my web server that we created earlier. So we'll say Docker RM my web server and we get another error, we can't remove containers that are still running. So now we have the full picture to remove the image, we have to go through all of these steps.","First stop the container that depends on the engine X image. So we'll say Docker stop my web server. OK. Now it's stopped. Now let's remove the container.","Docker RM, my web server and finally remove the engine X image. Docker RM I it's going to have an eye this time in JTE. All right. So we type in Docker images and we have nothing. So we can confirm that that image has been removed.","Now, let's explore another interesting thing about Docker. In our lesson, we first pulled the image we needed afterwards, we ran a container based on that image but Docker can automatically figure things out. For example, if we type in this command, Docker will automatically pull in the required image to start this container. There's no need for us to manually pull the image beforehand. Now let's jump to a different topic.","Can you spot the problem with our engine X container? If we want it to work as a web server, we need to make sure it's always available in the current scenario, if the container experiences an error, it will enter a stop state and even worse. If we reboot our server, we'll have to log in and manually start our container every time. Isn't there a way to tell Docker to always keep this running? Well, there is, let's find it.","So we'll type in man Docker run. Now, this is a big manual for doc run command. So let's type in front slash to begin a search and then type in restart policy. And we're gonna look for this text and we find this interesting command option always. So to create an engine X container that will always restart when it experiences problems and will also start every time the system boots up, we can adapt our previous command.","Lets press Q to quit the manual and we'll return to our Docker run command. OK? Now we can insert the additional command option. We need restart always. And there we go.","Since we didn't have engine X colon latest locally downloaded. Notice that it automatically goes out and downloads it up to this point. We worked with publicly available container, images built by other people. But in the real world, these generic images won't be enough. We'll either have to modify them to adjust to our specific needs or we'll need to build our own images.","Let's see how we can do that. Now, images can be built from zero but to make life easier, we'll use the engine X image and build on top of it. The first thing we need to do is create a directory for our little project and let's call it my image. OK. Then we'll cd into the directory here.","We can add all the files that we want to add to our final image. Only one file is needed for our purposes. Now, let's create it with Vim index dot HTML and we'll add a simple line of text to it. OK? It's not valid HTML, but it suits for demo purposes.","Let's save index dot HTML and continue. Since we have all the files, we need. The next step is to tell Docker how we want to build this image. And the instructions should be included with a file with a very specific name, Docker file with a capital D. So let's create that with Vim Docker file and we're going to add in the following text.","Now, the first line instructs Docker to use engine X as the base image. So that will be the starting point. And on top of the engine X image, we'll add our desired changes. The next line tells Docker to copy one file from one location to another. Specifically index dot HTML.","From our current myimage directory will be copied to user sharex H TM L index HTML inside the container. This will replace the preexisting file in that container with a custom one that we just created. So we'll see the effect of this later on and we can save our Docker file at this point. So we type escape colon WQ. Now obviously, this is a very simplistic file with very simple instructions.","Other popular instructions are run to tell Docker what commands to run to prepare this image. For example, we can instruct Docker to install a few additional utilities to the image. Other important instructions are C MD entry point. This tells Docker what command to run inside this container when it starts up along with the command parameters that might be required. Now, the difference between them is that the startup command under C MD is optional.","It can be ignored under certain circumstances. For example, if the user provides an alternate way to start the container in a Docker run command, but the command provided an entry point is not optional. It will be executed when initializing the container. Now let's go back to our home directory. We'll type CD with nothing else.","And let's see how we can build our image. The usual command is Docker build dash dash tag, followed by the repository image name tag. And finally the path to the directory that contains our Docker file. So in this case, we'll use this command doctor bill, dash dash tag. And we're gonna call this Jeremy slash custom engine X one point.","Oh my image. Now, Jeremy is the repository name. If we'd want to publish this on Docker hub and our username. There would be Jeremy. All of our projects would be stored under this repository called Jeremy.","We can think of it as our own personal folder on Dockerhub custom engine X is the name that we choose for our image and 1.0 is the tag for our 1.0 version of this image. Finally, my image is a directory where we included our project files and our Docker file. Now you can always use man Docker build and scroll to the examples section. If you need a quick refresh on how to use this command. It's worth mentioning that the repository name can be omitted if we don't need it.","We can also run a man such as this. We can also run Docker build dash dash tag, custom engine, X colon 1.0 my image. Now let's move on to our next lesson."],"Operations Deployment/17":["In software, we can create a virtual computer on our real computer. These simulated computers are called virtual machines and they can be very useful on servers. Here's an example, imagine we have a powerful server with 64 CPU cores and 1024 gig of RA M. If we create 32 virtual machines, each one would have two virtual CP US which are also called VCP US and 32 gigs of RA M now instead of renting one big physical server to just one client, we can rent our smaller virtual servers to 32 clients. That's what cloud compute companies like DigitalOcean Amazon web services and Google Cloud use to sell some of their services to thousands of clients.","There are many software solutions out there to create this kind of setup. But in Linux, the most popular at the moment is key emu dash KVM. These are actually two pieces of software that interconnect. The name key U comes from quick emulator. This emulates or simulates a virtual computer KVM comes from kernel based virtual machine.","This is code included in the Linux kernel that uses hardware technology meant to speed up virtual machines. Essen will focus on a tool called Vers or V IRS H. This is a program that lets us manage virtual machines from the command-line. Now, if you've ever used VirtualBox, you use the graphical interface, clicking buttons to create a virtual machine, um configuring it, starting it and so on. VH just does all of these things through commands rather than a graphical interface to quickly get started.","We can install the Vert manager software package with this command pseudo apt install Vert dash manager. Normally Vert manager is meant for Linux machines that include a graphical user interface, but we can also install it on a Linux machine that only has a text user interface. We won't need the Vert manager application itself. However, by installing it, the package manager will pull in a lot of other dependency packages which include utilities that help us in the lessons that we have about virtual machines. Now, let's continue with our demo.","We'll create this configuration file type in Vim test machine dot XML and we're going to add in this content. This will help us create a virtual machine called test machine and it's virtualized through KMU with one gig of ram one virtual CPU and a 64 bed architecture. Now H VM stands for hardware virtual machine. Now, of course, this is a hacky example. So we can just kind of speed through this process.","A real virtual machine also needs storage, network cards and operating system and so on. Now, to create this virtual machine, we'll pass in the definition file to verse and we'll type in verse, define test machine dot XML. Now we can dig deeper into this command. First of all, verse has a very nice help page. As you can see by typing in VERS help.","It's quite a lot of content, but commands are nicely grouped together. And if we scroll up, we'll see domain management. In this category, we have most of the commands we're interested in a domain basically refers to a virtual machine. So the commands in here can be used to interact with them. But first, we'll need to see the domains that we have.","Hmm no sign of what we defined earlier. That's because only active domains are shown. The one we defined is not active yet to see all domains, even in active ones, we can use this command and we'll add dash dash all to the end of it. We can see our domain virtual machine called test machine and we can see that it's currently shut off. We can start it with this command verse start test machine.","Now, if the name of the machine contains spaces, wrap it in double quotes. And now we can see it in verse list and to reboot it type in verse reboot test machine. This sends a reboot signal to the operating system running the virtual machine. So the reboot should be graceful processes should be closed. Normally, applications should have a chance to save their data, things like that.","It's just like restarting a real computer. But if a machine is not responding and it's frozen up for some reason, we can force reset it. Now, this is just like pressing the physical reset button or unplugging the power cord and plugging it back in now to shut down a virtual machine. Normally we type in verse shutdown test machine. This sends a shutdown signal to the operating system inside the virtual machine.","So just like rebooting, this should do a graceful shutdown and give applications enough time to close normally and save their data. Now remember that our simple virtual machine here has no operating system. So this won't work as expected but it will work normally in a real setup with an operating system. Now, if a virtual machine is stuck, we can force a hard power off with verse destroy test machine. Most verse commands are pretty intuitive, but this one's an Exception.","It sounds like we're actually destroying or deleting the machine. But that's not the case. This command is the equivalent of unplugging the power from your home computer and not powering it back on afterwards. And we can confirm this with verse list dash dash all to actually delete a domain or virtual machine. We have to power it off and then use a command like this BSH undefined test machine.","But let's not use this yet. The command above deletes the virtual computer we defined but not the data stored. For example, it's virtual disks and we usually want to keep the data. But let's say that this time we want to remove that too. Now, we can explore advanced use cases for each command with verse help and then the name of the command.","In our case, we'll type in verse help undefined. And we see that this would delete all data associated with the domain. So let's run this. And now we can confirm that everything is now gone. Now let's recreate our machine and explore some other things.","When we start up our real server, the virtual machines on it have to be manually started. If we want a virtual machine to start up automatically every time the real machine the server boots, then we could type in verse autostart test machine. If we want our virtual machine to not autostart, when the server boots, then we would type in verse autostart dash dash disable test machine to explore the virtual resources assigned to a virtual machine. We use Dominfo which is short for domain info, just one CPU core. Let's say we want to bump this up and make it a little more powerful.","We want to assign two CPU cores. Instead we'll need some help on this one. So we'll start by typing in verse set. And let's imagine we forgot the correct command. We can press tab a couple of times to get some suggestions.","And now we remember the correct command verse set VCP us. Now remember this is set VCP us with an S at the end, not set VCPU. But how do we actually use it? We'll type in verse help set VCP us. And we can see the syntax here.","Set BP us, domain count, followed by a dash dash long option. And we'll use dash dash config option that sets the number of VCP us to two. The next time the machine will boot. So let's run this command verse set VCP us test machine two dash dash config and it seems that the maximum is set to one. So let's change that with this command ver set BC P US test machine two config dash dash maximum.","Now this sets the maximum CP US allowed for this virtual machine to two cores to actually assign two virtual cores. We'll run this again. Now remember this does not change VCP us while the virtual machine is running. So we have to power it off now to force the power off. In our case, since we have no operating system in there, we'll just type in verse destroy test machine and then we'll start it again for the changes to become active.","And if we check our domain info, we see that it worked. Now to change the memory size, we first changed the maximum to 2048 megabytes. Then we set the memory allocation to 2048 megabytes. And then we shut down the machine gracefully or force power off. If we need to.","In our case, then we'll start it back up again. And domain info will show our changes took effect. Awesome. Right. And that concludes this lesson on creating and managing virtual machines."],"Operations Deployment/18":["In our previous lesson, we created a very minimal virtual machine. It didn't have any virtual disk attached so it couldn't host an operating system. Now, it served well for our demonstrating purposes as we learned how to manage it with the BSH command. But now let's do something more interesting to see some real world scenarios as well. We'll look at how to create a more complete virtual machine with an operating system running inside.","This will give us a chance to see the real thing in action and learn about some additional commands. Now, the first thing we need to do is download a disk image now on Ubuntu site, they have a listing here of an Ubuntu operating system preconfigured to be used in a cloud environment. These are the cloud images, for example, Google cloud uses these similar images when we spin up a virtual private server on their platform. So we're going to download this minimal cloud image with WGET OK. And our image is finished downloading.","Now, what we want to do is we want to check to make sure that this image wasn't corrupted during the download process or modified to do that we have what's called a checksum process on the Ubuntu page. We have a couple of files here. SHA 256 sums and ss A 256 sums dot GP G. Now the first file here contains check sums for all of the images. And we use this GPG file to check all of the images that are in the folder of these two files.","And I'll show you how to do that. We'll copy the link here and we'll wget this file. So now that we have these two files here, we can run this simple command to check that image file that's in this folder. What this will do is look at every image in the folder and you want to see this OK message. If anything else pops up, you need to redownload that image because there was some kind of problem with it.","But here we can see the images exactly as it needs to be. So we're ready to go. Now, we can also inspect this image with the KU utility. For example, if we run KU dash image info, we can see the information for this image. What's interesting to note here is the virtual size of 3.5 gigabytes.","When we attach this image to a virtual machine, it will serve as the virtual disk and virtual size tells us that this disk is configured to have a total size of 3.5 gigabytes. In most cases that won't be enough. We'll want to install additional software on our virtual machine. So we'll need a larger disk to expand the size of this virtual disk to approximately 10 gigabytes. We can run this command and it shows that the image is resized if we check the info again.","Now we can see that we have a 10 gig drive. Perfect. Now, our virtualization tools will need to access what's called a storage pool in this pool, it will store disk images, snapshots and other data. Now by default, it will use the slash bar slash live slash live Burt directory. For this additional storage pools can be created if you desire.","Let's follow a simple path and just use the default location. Now, if we take a look at VRLI live bet we can see this images subdirectory here. So let's copy our disc image file to that subdirectory. And now we get to a pretty complex task. What command should we use to create a virtual machine and tell it to use this image file as a virtual disk.","Well, for this purpose, we can use the Burt dash install utility. However, this utility supports hundreds of combinations of command-line options. So it can be quite confusing to figure out the options that we need for each purpose. Let's see how we can kind of orient ourselves as total beginners. Well, you can start by just typing in the command.","Now, even though it'll throw an error, it will help us discover the first options that we need. The output tells us that the vert install needs to know what kind of operating system we intend to use in our virtual machine. It even then gives us an example of a command that we can use to list what operating systems are supported. So let's run that command, scroll up here and we can see that the one that we want to install Ubuntu 24.4 Ubuntu Noble is available here. We'll need to use Ubuntu 24.04 as a parameter in our future Vert install command.","So let's get some extra help by looking at the manual. We'll type in man Vert dash install. We'll notice that this example here seems to be the most relevant for our scenario. It shows us how to create a virtual machine based on an existing disk image. Now, unfortunately, it's a very minimal example.","We'll need more command-line options to get to our desired result. However, note that the dash dash import option is what we'll need to use. And that's quite important for our purpose here, but it can be easily missed if we're not aware about what it does. Why do we need this dash dash import option? Well, let's see what the manual says.","We can hit the question mark and type dash dash import and press enter and we press n a few times until we find the information that we need. Here we go with dash dash import, we skip the OS installation process and build a guest around an existing disk image. The device used for booting is the first device specified via dash dash disk or dash dash file system. We can see that this option tells our utility to skip the OS installation process. That's because normally Vert install will try to install an operating system on top of a disk image.","That's why it's called Burt dash install. So it's default mode of operation is to do that with the dash dash import option, we can tell it to skip that step. Our disk image already has the Ubuntu operating system preinstalled. Also, we can see that this option tells bird install to set our image as the device used for booting in this virtual machine. Now let's exit the manual and see another trick we can use to find the options that we need.","We can type in Vert dash install, dash dash help. Now we can start to cherry pick the options we need. Now, if we scroll up, we can see the first three that interest us name memory and BC P us with these, we can set the name of our virtual machine so we can easily identify it later. Then we can set the memory to be allocated in this machine in megabytes. 1024 megabytes is equivalent to one gigabyte.","Finally with dash dash VCP us, we pick how many virtual CPU CORS we want to allocate to the virtual machine. Now let's scroll down. The next option we care about is dash dash disk to provide the path to our virtual disk. In this case, the path to our image file note that we can provide multiple parameters for this option. In the example, we can see dash dash disk, the location of the disk comma cache equals none.","This line specifies the path to the disk but also disables cache. For our scenario, we'll have to provide the path to the disk. The next option is dash dash graphics with dash dash graphics. None. We'll tell our virtual machine that we don't have a graphical user interface available on our system.","Now let me make a quick note for the next commands we'll use in this demonstration. First, we'll type invert dash install then OS info and we're going to use Ubuntu 24.04 that we found in that previous help file for the name. We're going to call this Ubuntu one for memory. We're going to use a gig vcpus, we're going to use one and then were going to import disk and we're going to put the full path to our image file that we used earlier. And then we're going to say graphics none.","Now with OS info, we'll set the operating system type to Aunon 2 22.04 and this is equivalent to the OS variant option. But O SS info is easier to remember and type. And then with the name, we set it to we assigned 1024 megabytes of memory. Then we assign one virtual CPU. After that, we'll tell our utility that we're importing the disk image.","So it should skip all installation steps. Everything is already preinstalled on the image. Then we provide the path to the disk image it should use. And finally, we specify that the host system does not have a graphical interface available. Now, let's run it and see what happens.","And we're automatically connected to the text console of the virtual machine. It's as if we plugged in a monitor and keyboard to it. So we can see the output that it displays and we can start interacting with it if we wouldn't have specified the option dash dash graphical. None. This wouldn't have happened.","That's why this option is so important in a text only environment. Now, we can type in a username and interact with the virtual machine we can't log in since we don't know the password. However, the machine is fully booted up. Now, the problem is how do we exit from this virtual machine? Well, if we scroll all the way back up, we can see this line to exit from the console of a virtual machine, we have to press control plus the closing square bracket and that combination will be hard to remember.","So after you run a Vert install command, look for this output before the virtual machine starts up and fills your screen with output, it will serve as a quick reminder. So let's press control, closing bracket and exit the message domain creation completed, tells us that everything went fine, at least with the virtual machine creation part and booting up, but we don't know the root password. So let's fix this now, we can list all of our virtual machines that are available. And we can see here that we have Ubuntu one running. Let's shut it down and we'll give it a few seconds and we'll see if it's successfully powered off and it did if for some reason, it would refuse to shut down.","We can also force a power off with BSH destroy Ubuntu one as we learned in our previous lesson. Now, in our case, it's in a shutoff state so we can remove it from our system. So now we've completely deleted the virtual machine and we removed all storage, which means that we removed that image file that we copied earlier. So let's go ahead and copy that back. Now, let's recreate this virtual machine.","But this time with a twist, since this Ubuntu disk image is created for the cloud, it has some automation scripts. These are included in a collection of tools called cloud dash and kit. And some of these scripts can help us set a root password if we pass the required data to them. So let's do that. We'll reuse our old command, but we'll add in a new option at the end.","Now here type in cloud init root dash password generate on by writing in cloud init root password generate equals on here. We pass instructions to cloud init to generate a random password for the root user. Now, before we press enter a quick note, the random root password will be displayed for 10 seconds. We should be ready to select and copy that password text from our screen. Before the virtual machine starts outputting its boot data, we can copy it afterwards as well, but you have to scroll up a lot.","So now it's present and we will copy this root password and as we can see the installation is starting. Ok. Now we're at the login prompt and we don't have any errors with anything. So we're ready to go. So we'll type in root and then we'll paste in that password note that nothing will be displayed at the password prompt when we paste it in.","Now, we're forced to reenter our password to change it. So we'll paste in the previous one again. And then at the new password prompt, we'll pick a new one that we want and you'll have to confirm it. All right. We're in after that, we've successfully logged into the AUD two operating system running in our virtual machine.","Now, let's look at the sizes of our virtual disks with DF dash H. Notice how our virtual disk comes preallocated with around 10 gigabytes. The size we picked when we resize it with the key U dash image command. And if we type in apt update, we'll see that we even have internet connectivity on this virtual machine. Even though we didn't specify any networking parameters.","Vert install will pick many reasonable defaults for us. Now, we can log out with this command and disconnect from the virtual machine console with control and closing bracket. In a running virtual machine, we can always reattach to the console with the BSH console command followed by the name of the machine, but this won't show us previous output. So we won't see the login prompt to force the virtual machine to output that login prompt again. A trick we can use is simply press enter here and we'll get the login prompt.","Now, let's disconnect from the console with control closing bracket and we'll wrap up our lesson with some alternate commands. Now, another important thing to note is that in some cases, you can't be sure about the operating system type included in a virtual disk image. In other cases, the operating system can be newer than what Vert install supports. For example, Vert install won't know about Ubuntu 26.04. If this operating system was launched yesterday, it will take a while for the tool to be updated with new information.","And in such cases, you have a few options available. We can type in Vert dash install to get a quick reminder of these alternate options. Now, if we're not sure about the operating system instead of that dash dash OS info, Ubuntu 24.4 parameter, we can type in OS info detect equals on here and Vert install will try to auto detect it based on what it can find in the image. Alternatively, if we have a fair idea that this is a Linux distribution that appeared after the year 2022 we can type in OS info, Linux 2022. And of course, we should remove the cloud and knit option if we don't need it, we only use that with images that were built specifically for the cloud that include those cloud and knit utilities and that wraps up our lesson.","Let's jump ahead to the next one."],"Operations Deployment/19":["Now, in our previous example, we learned how to work with cloud images that have preinstalled operating systems on them. So we don't need to install one, a different scenario we can encounter is if we actually want to install an operating system, that is we don't want to use a preconfigured disk image. We want to start fresh and create a clean install. Now, in this case, we can create a virtual machine attach a virtual CD ROM or DVD ROM to it and create an empty disk image. Then Vert install can boot from the CD ROM or DVD ROM and install the operating system on top of that disk image.","Here's an example of a command that we could run for such a purpose. There are a couple of important differences here. First of all, we no longer use the dash dash import option. We have no prebuilt disk image, no preinstalled operating system. So there's nothing to import.","Then we don't point the dash dash disk option to the path of an image file since none is available. Now it's kind of broken with the line return here. But with size equal 10, we tell the bird install to create a new image with 10 gigabytes available on it. Finally, with the dash dash location option, we point to an ISO file which is basically an image of a CD or DVD ROM installation disk. And again, we do dash dash graphics.","None. At the end of our command, we'll need an extra option with dash dash extra args console equals TT Ys zero. We tell the Linux kernel booting in the virtual machine to expose what is called a serial console represented by TT Ys zero. Here. In essence, it gives us a way to connect to that virtual machine and interact with it in a text only environment.","Without this option, it would boot in such a way that we'd have no way to connect to the console without a graphical user interface available on the system. Now, we won't run this command since we don't have a de and 12 installation iso available and we won't waste time downloading one because we have an even more efficient way to get this done. We can tell Vert dash install to get its required files directly from the internet and it can download only what it needs. And that reduces the time necessary for the operation. All we have to do is edit our location parameter instead of using a parameter to point it to an iso file.","We use a parameter pointing to a web address, the web address we type here needs to offer access to a certain directory and file structure that bird install can understand and use. You can visit the address in your web browser if you want to see the file and directory structure. And this is the URL that we're going to use. So instead of this iso location, we will put in this location and I'll put in for secure and we will press enter. And as we can see, it's gonna start pulling down the necessary files to do this installation.","There we go. And after we see a bit of output from the Linux kernel, we're greeted by the installer. Now, sometimes depending on your terminal, this can be a little messed up like this. Now, we've booted a very minimal installation environment. So extra languages aren't available.","We can just press enter and pick the defaults for the first two steps to see how this actually works as intended. But in our demo, let's stop here since the next steps start downloading stuff and we'd have to wait around. But now let's press control and closing bracket to close out our terminal. Now, as a note, our output might be a bit messed up and clear, doesn't always fix it. And this is how we can create a virtual machine and install an operating system on it.","Now, most of the time you'll want to use the cloud images that we used in the previous lesson, but it's helpful to know this method as Well, and now on to the next lesson."],"Users and Groups/3":["Now we'll look at how to manage systemwide environmental profiles. And Linux first, what is the so called environment? We can see our current user's environment with a command like this print E and V or E and V. What we see here are environment variables. And let's take this example from the output his size 1000.","This means that currently the variable called his size is set to 1000 and changing this to another value is as easy as writing his size equals 2000 at the command-line. So what does this do? In this case, the environment variable is used by bash our current login shell each time bash runs, it checks this value to know the maximum size of the command history. It should save, we can check out the history of every command we ever typed in this session and previous sessions with this command. So with the size of 1000 bash will never save more than 1000 commands in the history.","In this case, an environment variable was used as some sort of program setting other times applications look at these variables to get an idea about the sort of environment they're running in. For example, we'll see something like this variable. This tells applications where our home directory is. If they want to save a file, they can make use of this variable to save it at the right location. To get an idea of how this works, we can run this command, adding this dollar sign in front of the variable, drops the content of that variable in the same spot on the command-line.","So writing Echo home is the same as writing echo slash home slash int. This can be useful in scripts. Imagine we have a script and we want to save a file in the user's home directory. We could write this. Now when the user Aaron runs it, the variable will fill in slash home slash Aaron in that spot.","So this command will run if Jane runs it, this command will run. Now, this is useful because any user can run the script, but the command will intelligently fill in a different home directory for each user. So it dynamically adjusts for every user's environment. If a user wants to add their own environment variables, they can edit the dot bashrc file. We'll see something like this if we explore it.","But in this case, we want to make sure that we set some variable to a certain value for every user that logs into the system. Not just one to do this, we make changes to this file Etsy environment. Now, imagine we type this in and save the file. This variable will now be set every time a user logs in to test this out in a virtual machine. After editing the file, you can log out with this command and then log back in.","Now if we type this, we'll see this output which confirms the variable has been set accordingly. But that file is only for setting environment variables. What if we want to do more complex stuff like run a command every time a user logs in, we can use the special directory slash Etsy slash profile dot d here, we can create a file that ends with a dot sh extension. Let's add a file called lastlogin dot sh. And we will type in this and then save the file and exit.","This is a simple script that uses the theory we learned about in previous lessons. It basically logs a date and time when the user logged in in a file called last login under their home directory. Now, once again, let's log out and then log back in. And if we type LS, we'll see a new file called last login. And then if we type can last login, we'll see that our script did its job perfectly.","And we even made use of the environment variable dollar sign home to showcase how we can actually use it in a script. While these are bash scripts note that the dot sh files we add to Etsy slash profile dot D. We don't need a shebang like slash bin slash bash. The system already knows that anything found in Etsy profile dot D should be processed under our current shell, the bash command interpreter."],"Users and Groups/4":["Now let's look at managing the template user environment in Linux. When we created new user accounts, we mentioned one action that happens by default, all files from the Etsy skill directory are copied to the new users' home directory. This can be very helpful when we want to create a template for the user's environment. Now, practical examples are easier to understand. So let's jump right in and explain as we go along.","Imagine that we want to inform all new users about some default policy. Our old users already know about it. But every time we add a new user, it's likely that they don't know yet. What we can do is add a custom file to Etsy skill. Let's do that.","So we'll edit this readme file in Etsy skill and we'll add this text. Please don't run CPU intensive processes between 8 a.m. and 10 p.m. and then save our file. Now we'll add a new user to test this out and let's explore our home directory.","The dash A option makes LS list all files including some that are hidden from the default output of the command files that have a name beginning with a dot like dot Bashrc are not shown if we don't add the dash a option. And here we can see all files were copied from the Etsy scale directory, including our Readme file. Trinity will notice this when exploring your home directory and with a command like cat Reedy, she'll find the information that we intended to reach all of our new users. So think of the Etsy scale directory as just a place where we go and put in files that will be used for every new user we create after that."],"Users and Groups/5":["Now let's look at managing user resource limits. And Linux, when we have a lot of users logging into the system, we may want to impose limits on what resources they can use. That way. We can ensure for example, that a user does not use 80% of the CPU leaving very little despair for others. Now to set such a limit, we can edit this file and it's located at Etsy security limits.com.","We'll see a lot of comments in that file explaining how we should use it, the syntax required and so on. Now, if we move further down in the file, we'll see the syntax for setting a limit and we can observe that the syntax is domain type item and then value, let's break this down into easy to understand parts first, the domain. Now what can we specify here? Usually one of these three things, a user name. In this case, we simply type the name of the user such as Trinity or we can use a group name to set a limit for everyone in the developers group.","We just add an at sign in front of its name. So we'd write at developers to set such a limit for the developers group. And the asterisk will match all setting a limit for asterisk basically says set this limit for every user on the system. So it's a way to set a default limit note. However, that this limit will only apply to every user that is not mentioned in this list.","A user specific limit overrides the global asterisk limit. For example, one asterisk limit can specify that everyone can only launch 10 processes. But then another limit for the user. Trinity says that she can launch 20 processes. In this case, the limit for everyone will be 10, the default one.","But for Trinity, it will be set to 20. Next is the type field which can take three different values hard, soft and then a dash, a hard limit cannot be overridden by a regular user. If a hard limit says they can only run 30 processes, they can go above that. It's basically the top the max value of a resource that someone can use. And here's an example for Trinity, a soft limit.","On the other hand is different instead of an absolute maximum value. This is more like the startup limit. The initial value for the limit when the user logs in if a user has a soft limit of 10 max processes and a hard limit of 20. The following happens when they log in the limit will be set to 10 processes. But if the user has some temporary need to increase this.","They can raise it to 1112, 15 or 20 processes. This way, they can get a slight increase when it's absolutely required. So they can manually raise it to anything they need, but never above the hard limit. It basically gives them a little bit of extra breathing room when they need it. And last, we have the dash sign, this specifies that this is both a hard and a soft limit with this.","We're saying Trinity should be able to run 20 processes at most. When she logs in, she should be able to use up her entire allocation without needing to manually raise her limit. Next up the item value. This decides what this limit is for. We can have things such as N proc N proc sets the maximum number of processes that can be opened in a user session size, sets the maximum file size that can be created in this user session.","The size is in kilobytes. So 1024 here means the maximum file size is 1024 kilobytes, which is exactly one megabyte. CPU sets a limit for the CPU time. And this is specified in minutes when a process uses 100% of AC pu core for one second, it will use up one second of its allocated time. If it uses 50% of one core for one second, it will use up 0.5 seconds of its allocation.","Even if a process was opened three hours ago, it might have only used two seconds of CPU time because that's the time it actively utilized the CPU, the rest of the time it was sitting idle, basically sleeping and not using the CPU. If you want to see more stuff that can be limited, just consult the user manual for this limits.com file with Man limits.com. Now let's test our knowledge and add a limit for our user called Trinity to ensure that she can open up a maximum number of three processes. So under this line, we will add this, make sure there's no pound sign at the beginning of this line that would make it a comment and the Vim editor might automatically add it when you press enter to add a new line here, make sure to delete the preceding pound sign. Otherwise the line will be commented and have no effect.","Let's save our file and exit. And at this moment, only one process is permanently running in recession, the bash shell. So we should be able to run two or more processes. Let's launch PS and pipe the output to the less pager. We can see that it works and it got us to running three processes, which is the max limit.","Now, what would happen if we tried to launch the fourth? Let's press Q to quit the less pager and then try the following command. Now, this would try to launch three new processes, LS grep and less plus bash already running would total four processes and we'll see this failing as expected, we cannot run more than three processes. So the three process limit is enforced correctly. Now, if you're following along, let's type logout to exit from trinity session.","If we want to see the limits for our current session, we can type in this UIM dash A, we have small hints between the parentheses. For example, we can see dash U displayed for mac user processes. That means we could type U limit dash U 5000 to lower our limit to 5000 processes. By default. A user can only lower their limits, not raise them.","The Exception is when there are hard and soft limits. In that case, the user can raise his or her limit all the way up to the hard value. But only once after that limit is raised with the UIM command, the next command can only lower it. It cannot be raised the second time even if the hard limit would allow it."],"Users and Groups/6":["Now let's examine how to manage user privileges. And every time we had to make some important changes to the system, we used sudo in our commands. That's because only the root user also called super user can make changes to important areas of the operating system. However, when we put Sudo in front of a command that command runs as if the root user executed it. So how come our user is allowed to use Sudo?","If we type in this command, we'll see that our user is a part of the sudo group. Whoever is a part of this group is automatically allowed to use Sudo. That means the easiest way to give another user SUDO privileges is to add them to the sudo group, to add our user trinity key to the sudo group. We would type in this and that's it. Now, this user can get administrator privileges whenever they want.","But this gives them power to do anything they want on the system. What if we want more fine tuned control, then we could take a different approach. Now there is a special file and I'll spell it out its in the slash etc folder. And its SUD O s like SUDOERS and it defines who can use sudo and under what conditions, what commands they can run and so on. But we should not edit this file directly.","We use the utility called by sudo or VBA sudo is like a combination of the two. This utility can check to see if our edits are correct to help us avoid mistakes in this file. First, let's remove trinity from the sudo group to make sure she can't use Sudo anymore. And instead to find a different sudo policy for her later to start editing the Etsy Sudoers file. We're just run Sudo by sudo and it opens up in the vim editor.","So this file is thoroughly commented, but we're not interested in the first few parts. So let's navigate to the end. You will notice on this line that allows people in group sudo to run all commands. Now, we see why any user added to the sudo group can run any command with Sudo. Let's break down this line into the five different parts and analyze what they do.","Number one, this is the users group here, we define who this policy is for. Number two is the host here, we can specify that these rules only apply if our server's hostname or IP address has a specific value not really useful for our purposes. So we'll just type in all for this field. And the next is the run as user field. Now, here we can type in a list of usernames.","Normally, sudo, LS will run the LS command as root because that's what sudo does. It runs the command as a different user and by default, it runs it as the root user. But sudo can also be used so that Aaron can run commands for some other regular user like Jane and we'll see more about this later. So if we list Aaron Jane, etcetera in this run as field, then sudo can only be used to run commands as user, Aaron or Jane but not root in these fields. We want to specify multiple values.","We just list them one by one separated by commas. This is the run as group field and it's the same as before. If we type all here, then users that belong to the sudo group can use Sudo to run commands as any group. If we type a group name, then they can only run commands under the group that's listed here. And five, this is a list of commands that can be executed with sudo.","So we could say that the syntax for a policy defined in the Sudoers file is this now let's go through some examples to define a policy for our Trinity key user and let her run any sudo command. We can type in something like this. And we can see in this case between the parentheses, we just specified Trinity can run all commands as all users. But we did not specify any groups. This will mean implicitly that she can run commands as any group to even if we didn't add the second all field here to specify a policy for all users in the developer group, we just add the percent sign in front of the group name and this will include all of the people in the developers group.","We mentioned that sudo lets us run command as root but also as nonroot regular users. For example, to run LS home Trinity command as the user called Trinity, we could write something like this. After the dash you, we specify the username, we want to run this command as if this third field is all, then this policy allows someone to run SUDO commands as any user. But if we want Trinity to only be able to run sudo commands as the users, Aaron or John, we would write this. And also this is wrapped in parentheses which hints that the field is optional.","So a line like this is also valid. We mentioned that in the fourth field, we can specify a list of commands with our previous entries. The user or group granted sudo privileges can execute any command but we can live with them like this. Now, Trinity can run commands such as LS or Stack and only the LS or Stack commands will work. If Trinity key tries a different commands such as this, she will get this error.","And since we specified the third field is optional, this line could also be written like this. We know that the first time we run a SUDO command in a session, it asks for our current user's password in our sudoers file. We see a hint about how we could get rid of this kind of requirement. So we could use this example in the comments and figure out how to apply this for our user trinity. If we want to be able to run SUDO commands without providing a password, we can write in this line into the sudoers file."],"Users and Groups/7":["Now let's examine how to manage access to the root account. In Linux, we already saw one method to temporarily become root whenever needed when we run a command such as this. It's basically the same as the root user saying, execute LS root pseudo dash I. And that's it. We're logged in as root to exit from the roots session.","We'll type su dash if root had a password set in the past. But then the account was locked for some reason, we can unlock it with this command after one of these steps, then we can run su and type the password for root to log in. Of course, we would also find ourselves in the reverse scenario. Imagine this, let's say that currently people can log in as root, but we decide that this is a bit insecure for our purposes. We can lock password based logins to the root account with this or pseudo password dash L root.","Other logins might still be possible if they were previously set up. For example, if an administrator has set up logins with an SSH private key, they'll still be able to log in. Even if the root account is locked since that method does not use a password but a cryptographic key instead."],"Users and Groups/8":["By default, Linux keeps information about user accounts and groups stored locally. For example, user accounts are defined in the Etsy password file. The name can be a bit misleading because it seems like it stores password data, but it's just data about user accounts. So if we run cat Etsy password, we can spot a line like this, which shows us information about the username, the user id, which is 1000, the user's home directory and the user's preferred shell, which is bin bash. The problem is in today's world, we usually have to manage hundreds of servers and keeping track of what user accounts and groups exist on each system can be a hassle even with automation tools like chef Ansible or Puppet, especially if we constantly need to create new user accounts, remove unused users to find new groups and so on.","So there's an easier way to do this. For example, let's say a new member joins our team and we need to create a dedicated user account for this member on 200 servers. Instead of updating user accounts on hundreds of servers, we can just configure them differently from the get go all we need to do is add an LDAP server into the mix. LDAP is short for lightweight directory access protocol. We can configure our Linux systems to use an LDAP server as an additional source of information for user and group account.","So whenever we need to add, remove or modify user accounts and groups, we avoid having to make changes on hundreds of individual servers. All we need to do is update data about these accounts on a single central location on the LDAP server. And our Linux servers will get the updated data about the user and group accounts automatically whenever they need it. It's easier to understand this if we see it in action. So let's dive right into a practical exercise.","First, let's start off by checking for two user identities. If we type in ID John, no such user and ID Jane, no such user, we'll see that these user accounts don't exist on the system. Now, later in our lesson, we'll get back to these users and notice some differences. Now, let's get an LDAP server running in the real world. It might not be our responsibility to manage that LDAP server as it might be running on Microsoft Azure or on a Windows server.","Our job is to manage Linux servers. Instead. In that case, we'll just need to set up the clients to use LDAP but not the LDAP server itself, which means we'll need to instruct all Linux servers to ask for user and group identities from a central LDAP server. Now to avoid having to set up an LDAP server, which is a complicated process. Let's just get our hands on a preconfigured one.","And for this purpose, we'll use LXC, which is short for Linux containers. You can think of LXC as a Docker for an entire operating system instead of a single application. Now, first, we'll have to initialize LXD, which is the hypervisor that will monitor LXC containers. Now, we can press enter here for everything displayed to pick the default values shown between the square brackets. Now, if you want to test this out yourself, just make sure that the size and gigabytes of the new loop device is set to at least five gigabytes.","That's the default value in our case. So we can press enter on that option as well. OK? For our convenience, we've created a very simple LDAP server code cloud and we have it in an archive. Now for our convenience, we've created a very simple LDAP server here at code cloud and stored it in an archive here.","And if we take a look at our files, we'll see a tar archive which contains our Linux container image. So let's restore the image to our system with this command. Now, after this import process hits 100% we still have to wait a while until it finishes setting up the Linux container. We will know when it's done because the command-line prompt will be displayed again. Now let's list our Linux containers.","We will see our restored containers called LDAP dash server and it's currently stopped. So let's start it up and that's it. Now, we have an LDAP server available. If you want to experiment with it for simplicity, every password on it is set to the word password, not super secure but convenient for demonstration purposes. So if you want to add data to the LDAP server, the password for the admin is password and the password for the two Linux accounts we defined on it.","John and Jane is also password. Now, let's list our Linux containers again. Now we have to copy this IPv4 address because we'll need it in the next step. Now, finally, we get to the real focus of our lesson. How do we configure our Linux system to use group and user accounts that are defined on this LDAP server?","Well, there are many ways to do this, but let's check out what's probably the easiest way we can install a package called LIB nss dash LDAP D. Notice that it's LDAP D with ad at the end, there's also a package called LIB NSS dash LDAP without that D letter at the end. But this package that ends with ad has some improvements over the other one, NSS is short for name service switch. And it's just a convenient way for Linux to get data about the various things from various sources. So whenever a Linux system needs to get information about things like user accounts, groups, passwords, host names and other stuff, it can consult the name service switch to find out where to get this data from.","Now after we run our command here. Now, after we run our command, we get a guided install wizard where it's very easy to pass our configuration options. Now note the highlighted red text at the top. In this first step, we're configuring settings for something called NSL CD, the name service local daemon, a component responsible for retrieving data from the LDAP server. That's why here we need to type in the IP address of our LDAP server.","First, we'll replace the 127.0 0.0 0.1 IP shown as an example with the actual IP address of our LXC container, the one that we copied earlier. Now after our IP address, we also add a forward slash just like we had in the example shown before. After we press enter here, we get to the next configuration step. We need to pick a distinguished name of the LDAP search base. LDAP can store information for a lot of departments in our organization.","So things are separated in a tree like structure in our case. This is the part where we tell our name service local daemon, which segment of this tree is storing information about our Linux users and groups. And we have some helpful text that shows us an example of what we could enter here. Now, the LDAP server from our Linux container is configured to keep user and group information in a tree stored under the code cloud.com domain name. So the components of this domain are code cloud and com, which means we will have two domain components here abbreviated as DC.","Now, in some cases, the LDAP server might store information for a subdomain as well. For example, users dot code cloud.com. And if that were the case, we'd need to add an additional component at the beginning containing the name of that subdomain. So we'll say DC equals users and this would equal users dot code cloud.com. But let's delete this subdomain component since we don't need it and press enter to continue to the next step.","And here's the most interesting part. Since at this point, we finally tell our Linux system where it can find additional data for user and group accounts. Passw D refers to user data group to data about groups and shadow is concerned with passwords. So we'll take these first three boxes here, simply put we infirm. OK?","So we're informing our system that user group and password data is not only stored locally but on the LDAP server as well. Note that this list goes down quite a bit and there are more elements than we might currently see on the screen. We don't need to enable LDAP lookups for the other entries here since we're only concerned with user and group accounts in this lesson. Now, let's press enter to wrap up our configuration. OK.","So what does this configuration wizard do? Well, we can already see from the output that it modified a file called Etsy and a switch.com short for name service switch. If we take a look at this file, we'll see this. The word LDAP was added to three lines in this file equivalent to the three boxes that we ticked earlier in our configuration wizard. So if we take a look at the first line, this simply tells the name service switch that when it's looking for password data, that is data about users, it can find this information in three places in the default files like the Etsy password file.","Then some user data might be provided by systemd and finally valid user data is also present on the LDAP server. But this simply informs the name service switch where it can pull data from. However, it is not able by itself to pull data from LDAP. So that's where the other component we looked at NSL CD comes in the name service local daemon is able to get that data from the LDAP server. And its main configuration file can be found at Etsy NS L cd.com.","So these are the files that we can edit if we want to change something or fine tune settings. Now, let's see if all of this actually works. Remember at the beginning of our lesson when we checked for two user accounts, John and Jane, they weren't present on the system. However, on our preconfigured LDAP server, we added definitions for both of those accounts. So let's test it out.","Can our Linux system find these? Now, now when we run the same two commands, we can clearly see in the output that our system now knows about these additional accounts on our LDAP server. We defined that both users should belong to a group named LDAP users. Now, this was done for convenience to make it easier to notice when users were defined locally and which were defined on the LDAP server. But we could have made these users part of any other group to see all users, both those defined locally and those defined on the LDAP server.","We can use a command like this. Get ENT is short for get entries and this command asks the name service switch to show entries for the past WD section to get this data from all of the sources. It has available. All the users seen here are defined locally except the last two John and Jane, which were defined on the LDAP server. But we might want to filter output to only see entries from the LDAP server.","To do that. We can pass the dash dash service option and tell our command that we want entries only from the LDAP service. Now to get group entries defined on the LDAP server, we can enter this. Now notice an interesting thing about our user entries above on our LDAP server. We fine tune these accounts a little bit and we provided data about the full name of these users home directories and user shells.","But there's a slight problem. We can see that John should have his personal directory at slash home slash John. But if we take a look at our system, this location does not exist and this can be an issue. We can't manually create home directories for everyone. Since we'd get back to the same problem described at the beginning of our lesson with updating a whole bunch of servers.","Every time a new person comes in, we'd have to reconfigure hundreds of servers and create specific home directories. But this has an easy solution as well. We can use pluggable authentication modules also known as PM. Every time a user logs in these modules are automatically triggered, all we need to do is pass an additional instruction to them to create a home directory. When someone logs in in case that home directory doesn't exist.","Now, for this purpose, we will run this command pseudo pam dash a dash update and we will tick the box next to create home directory on login. Then we'll press enter to save this change. Now, let's test this out. We can enter the command to log in as John and we can confirm that So that's it. Everything is working as expected.","Now, at this point, if we had 100 Linux servers configured the same way, updating user and group accounts for all of these systems would be easy. We just log to the LDAP server, update data about our accounts and all 100 Linux servers would instantly have it available one central location where we can create, remove or modify our Linux users and passwords plus our Linux groups pretty convenient and I hope you've enjoyed this lesson and I'll see you in the next one."],"Networking/1":["In this lesson, we'll learn how to configure network settings on Ubuntu. Let's start with the basic concepts. First to be able to communicate across networks. A device needs an IP address IP stands for internet protocol and there are two kinds of IPs IPv4 and IPv6. The V here stands for version and in version four of the internet protocol addresses look like this.","Basically four numbers that can take values from 0 to 255. Why these specific values? Because internally computers use eight bits for each number here and eight bits can represent a number from 0 to 255. For example, 000000000 is binary zero in decimal notation. And you can see here 1234 and so on all the way up to all the way to solid ones, which is 255.","So an IP like 1921681101 and decimal is actually this in binary. And this binary representation is important for the next thing we'll discuss. Sometimes we will see IP S displayed in this format. We have the IP address with a slash 24. At the end, this is called Cidr notation or CI Dr which is an abbreviation for classless inter domain routing.","We can see an extra slash 24 at the end next to the IP address. This tells us that the 1st 24 bits of this address are the prefix of this network. If we take another look at the binary notation, this will become easier to understand. So 1921681101 in binary is this and we can see that the 1st 24 bits align with the first three numbers we have in this IP address, which means that 1921681 is the network prefix. Basically the address of the network itself.","And the last part refers to a specific device on that network. It's somewhat similar to phone numbers. For example, if you call someone from Italy, you need to use the plus 3 9 prefix when you call plus 3 +9123456789. The first part plus 3 9 is the country prefix and a +123456789 is the personal number of the person you're calling. So a network prefix is analogous to a country prefix in phone numbers.","So a network prefix is just like a country prefix and phone numbers. If a device knows that the Cidr notation of a network IP is 24. Then it understands that 1921681 refers to the generic network prefix and 101 to a specific host on this network. This also means that any IP from 1921681.0 all the way to 1921681.2 55 is part of the same network, but an IP like 1921682101 is part of a different network because the prefix does not have the same initial three numbers. Or if we think in binary, it does not have the same 1st 24 bits as the slash 24 Cidr notation indicates that it should have.","Now, if the Cidr notation would be different, for example, 1921681101 slash 16, we get something along these lines. The 1st 16 bits would be the network prefix and the second would be the host. So in this case, just the 1st 16 bits of the network prefix which coincide with the first two numbers, 192168. That means the last two numbers. Now 1.101 refer to a host or device connected to this network.","In other words, for our current Cidr notation, any IP in this network must begin with a fixed 192168. While the last two numbers can be anything from 0 to 255. So we can have IP S from 1921680.0 all the way to 1921682 55.2 55. Now let's look at IPv6. Now these addresses have 100 and 28 bits instead of 32 bits like IPv4, these addresses have 100 and 28 bits instead of 32 bits.","As this case for IPv4. Here's an example of an IPv6 address. Now we notice that this is a bit different in three ways. For one, we have eight groups of numbers here instead of four like we had with IP V four two, these numbers are not in decimal format instead they're in hexadecimal format. There are 16 hexadecimal digits and AB CD ef.","In this case, the letter A is equivalent to the number 10 in our decimal format that we use every day. B is 11 C is 12 all the way up to F which is 15. So a number like FFFF is equivalent to 65535 and decimal notation and all ones in binary. And the third difference is that each number here is separated by a semicolon character. Since these IPv6 addresses are quite long, we'll often see them shortened.","So an IP like this will usually be shortened to something like this. And this is done by removing all of the leading zeros. First. For example, we can see that zero db eight became db eight and the consecutive group of zeros and semicolons was abbreviated to two consecutive semicolon characters IPv6 addresses also support the Cider notation. So an address like this with the slash 64 signals that the 1st 64 bits represent the network prefix and each group of two hexadecimal digits is eight bits.","So 64 bits would mean eight groups. Now, in this case, we've highlighted in purple, the network prefix of this IP for a slash 64 cidr notation. And of course, if the IP is shortened, you'll usually see it noted like this as we can see for prefixes that are multiples of the number. Eight things are straightforward enough. Unfortunately, some Cidr notations can be a bit harder to understand if the prefix is 20 bits.","For example, this doesn't coincide with the first three numbers of an IPv4 address anymore. But there are online tools to help you decipher or calculate the Cider notations you need to use, just look up Cider calculator or Subnet calculator on the internet."],"Networking/2":["Now lets see how to configure network settings on an Ubuntu machine. The IP command can help us discover almost everything we need to know about our network configuration. We can start out by entering IP link. This shows us our networking devices also called network interfaces. In Linux terminology, some are real devices like Ethernet cards, wireless cards, et cetera.","But other devices shown here can be virtual. Now, the lo here at the top is the loopback interface which is virtual. The loopback interface is used by the system when it needs to connect to itself. For example, when a program wants to connect to a database application running on the same system, it goes to 127.0 0.0 0.1 through this loop deck device ENP 0 S3 is an interface representing a real device. In this case, an Ethernet card.","Now to display the IP addresses configured for these interfaces, we can run IP address or even shorter command IP AD Dr or IP A and this output is a bit messy and hard to read to make the important bits stand out, we can pass the dash the color option, it looks much better, right? And the output is much easier to go through, make sure to add the dash the parameter in the middle, adding it at the end won't work. So in this output, we can see the things that we're interested in, namely the IPv4 and IPv6 addresses configured for each network interface. But notice the output for our third interface. We can see it's marked as being down.","That means it's not currently active. We can also see it doesn't have any IP address configured to make the interface active or bring it up. We can type in something like this. We can see there's a nice flow for this command. First, we specify we want to work with a linked device, then we want to set a property.","Next, we specify the name of the device or DEV that we want to modify settings for and finally up states our intention to activate the device. Now, if we check the IP address settings again, we'll see that it's now up and even autoconfigured with an IPv6 for itself. If we want to manually add an IPV four address for it, we can do that. Once again, the logic of the command follows a similar straightforward pattern. We specify that we want to work with an address and add an IP, we assign the IP address to our DEV slash device and we specify a slash 24 side range for it.","Finally, we type the name of the device, we want to set this property for. Now, if we look at the IP settings again, we can see the IP address has been configured to add an IPV six address. Things are roughly the same. We'll use the shortened format for this address. Just the IP format differs.","Ok. And if we explore the IP settings again, we see the address has been configured. Note that a network interface can have multiple IPs to remove an IP address, we can type in this. But now let's bring this network device back down. The trouble with setting IP S with the IP command is that these changes are temporary.","As soon as we reboot, the server, our changes are lost. So the IP command is mostly used to test new IP settings temporarily and of course to explore the current network settings. So how do we make our changes permanent? Well, it depends on the operating system. Each one has its own set of tools.","And this can sometimes change from one major version to the next default networking tools have been changed a lot in the past. What Ubuntu currently uses is called Netplan. This is a set of utilities that reads some configuration files, then sends instructions to other networking tools, telling them to configure network settings accordingly. On the Ubuntu server edition Netplan sends instructions to systemd's network daemon called systemd dash networkd. So let's see how we can instruct Netplan to assign some IP S for our devices to see the current Netplan configuration files we can type in pseudo Netplan.","Get we noticed that this specifies some settings for the Ethernet device called ENP 0 S3. By setting DHCP four to true, it makes this device ask for network settings from a DHCP four server. The four in here refers to IPv4 settings. A DHCP server is often used to auto configure IP S for the devices on a network. Now, chances are you use one at home?","For example, when you connect your phone to the Wi Fi router, you're also using a DHCP server. The phone asks the DHCP daemon running on the router to tell it what IP settings. It should configure for itself, but we want to manually configure these settings for now. So how can we do that? Well, Netplan configuration files are stored in the Etsy Netplan directory.","We can take a look at it now and you'll see in this case, we have a single file in here to explore the contents of this file. We can run cat Etsy Netplan. Ah We see permission denied. Now note that it contains the same stuff we saw displayed in our Netplan get command earlier. If we want to change the settings for the device mentioned here, we just have to edit the file and make our desired modifications.","But let's actually create a new file dedicated to a different Ethernet device on this system. First, we'll copy this text and use it as a template in our new file. Now, let's remember the name of the device that we want to configure, which in our case is ENP zero S eight. And let's create a new Netplan YAML file. Now let's paste in the content that we copied earlier.","And the first thing we need to modify here is the interface name and for mine, it's ENP zero S eight. Next, we should change the DHCP four line. As we don't want this device to auto configure with the help of a DHCP server. We'll change the property here to false and we'll also add an extra line and set DH TP six auto configuration to false as well. Now, we should be careful to preserve formatting here, namely the preceding space characters we add before every line we can see that the settings are aligned with a certain number of spaces to signal what the parent is for each object and what the child objects are.","For example, Ethernet is apparent for the objects below. Then ENP zero S eight is apparent for the DH TP settings. This way when Netplan processes the file, it will know what property applies to what object. And this type of formatting is specific to YAML files to specify the IP addresses that we want to assign to this device. We simply type in this.","And on the next line, we type 10.0 0.0 0.9 slash 24. Now note when we press enter, how the Vim editor intelligently adds two leading spaces for us to help with formatting. In this case, we need this alignment to add some properties that belong to this address' object. But in other cases, it can format things wrong. So do watch out for that when editing YAML files to avoid any mistakes.","Now here the minus sign signals that this is an element belonging to a list. Once again, this is specifically how YAML files should be defined. But why can't we just list elements without a dash sign because each IP here would have additional properties. And if we add two or three properties below it, Netplan wouldn't know how to differentiate between lines, what are the elements and what are the properties for each element? Since they would all be aligned the same by adding a dash sign.","Here, we signal where the elements are. And if we want to add two or three properties to an element, we simply type them below, but don't add the dash sign before them. This way, Netplan understands where the elements are specified and what properties apply to each one on the line below. We'll also add an IPv6 address. Let's use something that will stand out.","So we can check out later. If this worked, we'll add an AB CD in our address to recognize it easily. It's going to be right here. Now, we're ready to save our file and we can notice this countdown. So if we don't press enter to accept changes, network changes will be reverted to whatever they were before.","In case, we lost network connection to a server because of wrong settings, we can try to log back in in two minutes when settings will automatically be reverted. In our case though, let's press enter to accept the changes to specify a smaller timeout window like 30 seconds, we can pass the dash dash timeout option and to cancel the operation, we can press control C CH mod and then the file name and then it will make that warning go away. Now, if we check the settings for our devices, it looks like everything worked perfectly. Our device is now using the new IPv4 and IPv6 addresses. Notice the AB CD part that we used in our IPv6 address and the interface is also in an upstate.","So Netplan helped bring it online as well. Now, if we reboot our server, the settings would be reapplied automatically. So these settings are permanent until they're modified again. So if we run that plan, get once more, we'll see the entire configuration is displayed both for the old YAML file we already had and also the settings we defined in our new file. So everything is available at a glance.","Now let's go back to editing our file to showcase some additional network settings that might be useful. Let's add a few lines and make sure to align this text two spaces to the right of the ENP line above to signal that these are settings for that parent element. This sets two name servers, one with the IPv4 address of 8844 and one with 8888. These are domain name servers belonging to Google and they're used to solve domain names to IP addresses. For example, when we type in a name like code cloud.com in our browser, the computer can't know on its own what IP address is assigned to this domain.","So it asks a name server for this data. With the lines. Here, we configured two name servers also called DNS servers for our EP zero S eight interface. We'll also add a few extra lines and I'll explain them in a few moments. Note how this time the minus sign makes sense.","We differentiate between elements and properties. So when we type in via here without a preceding minus sign, Netplan will know that this via property applies to the element above. So what we did here was add two network routes. The first one tells our machine that when it wants to reach any IP. In the 192168.0 0.0 network, it can contact a server at 10.0 0.0 0.100.","The server acts as a middleman passing data from one network to the other. The other route specifies that if the computer can't figure out what route to use from everything it has available, it can fall back on this default route. For example, with current settings, it wouldn't know what route to use to send data to an IP like 20.20 0.20 0.20. No route is configured for this destination. So we'll fall back on the default route and tell the server at 10.0 0.0 0.1 to pass this data along the server is often called a gateway.","Since there's a door to an external world from this network to any other network. For example, when we use the internet at home, our router is usually the gateway because it helps us send data from our home network out to any other network in the world. Now, let's save our settings and we'll apply our settings and it looks like I have a YAML problem. So let's dig in and take a look at it. And as we can see here, I forgot to put a colon after routes and now it should work properly.","All right, it's accepted. But were our settings applied? We can check our network routes with this command IP route. We can see the routes were indeed applied. We noticed the default route via the 10.0 0.0 0.1 gateway for the ENP zero S eight device and the route here via the 10.0 0.0 0.100 gateway.","What about our DNS resolvers? Well, we can take a look at configured resolvers with this command resolve CTL status. Here we go. So the resolvers were successfully configured as well. But note that the 8.8 0.8 0.8 resolver only applies to the ENP zero S eight device.","Sometimes we will want resolvers to be applied globally to all network devices we might have for this purpose. We can configure resolvers in a different way. We can edit the Etsy Systemd resolved.com file. All right, we'll open this up. We'll go down here to DNS and uncomment that line by removing half time.","And we'll put in 11.1 0.1 space 9.99 0.9. And we'll add these two DNS servers and after we save the file, we should restart systemd's resolver daemon. Now, if we run the resolve CTL command again, we'll see that our resolvers have been applied globally as an added tip, we can also use this command for cleaner but less detailed output about DNS settings type in resolve CTL DNS. Now, these DNS resolvers work for domains such as code cloud.com and other similar entries. But what about simple host names for machines in our internal network?","For example, we might have a database server around, but we can't remember its IP address every time we want to use it. However, it would be easier to remember a code name for it like db server. Now to implement this idea, we'll edit this file Etsy hosts. Now, as you can see, we have some addresses in here and we're going to add a line 127.0 0.123 0.123 space DV server. This tells our machine to associate this hostname DB server with this IP.","And now we'll try to ping DB server and we can see our system now knows the IP address of this specific hostname. We can also add more complex host names in that file entries like this 1.2 0.3 0.4 example.com effectively tricking our system to believe that example.com has the 1.2 0.3 0.4 IP address. This is a common way to test out websites on a local machine before we actually post them online. And our machine thinks that example.com is 1.2 0.3 0.4. Now to wrap up this lesson, here's a useful tip.","You might have noticed that Netplan YAML files are easy to read, but a bit harder to write. It can be tricky to figure out what exact words we need to use to configure various network settings. But we can get some help from this manual by typing in man space Netplan. Now, here we'll find quite a few examples about various settings we can configure. Here's one interesting page.","If we press front slash to begin a search, then type in address label we get to this part which shows us a similar format that we used in our demo to configure an IP address or we type front slash, then default routes, then press N to look for the next occurrence. Here, we get to this part also similar to what we used for our purpose, except it has a few extra settings in the middle, like metric settings and so on. So these examples are very helpful to figure out how to define that YAML file. But if all of this seems a bit too complicated, here's another trick we can use. In fact, it might come in handy with many other tools that require us to edit configuration files.","We'll find a special directory on almost any Linux installation called slash user slash share slash doc. This includes additional documentation for various software packages. And in the case of Netplan, we can get access to quite a few example configuration files, we just have to list the contents of the directory. So we'll look at user share doc, netplan examples and pick the example that we want to take a look at. Now in our case, DH DP, YAML and static YAML are pretty interesting.","This shows us how to configure a simple Ethernet interface that gets its IP settings through DHCP. And the static YAML file shows us how to configure an Ethernet interface with static network settings. Hopefully this will make it easier to create Netplan YAML configuration files. OK. This is how to configure IPv4 and IPv6 networking and hostname resolution.","This wraps up our current lesson. Now, let's jump ahead to the next one."],"Networking/4":["In this lesson, we will learn about two concepts, how to bridge network devices and how to bond network devices. Now, what does it mean to bridge or bond? It means that we can take two or more network devices and glue them together under the operating system. And this creates a virtual network device. In one case, it's called a bridge and the other case, it's called a bond.","So if bridging and bonding glue together multiple network devices into a single virtual one, how are these methods? Different? The clues are in the words themselves, bridge and bond. Now when we bridge two network devices, we're actually doing exactly what the word implies that is we build a bridge between network one and network two. Such a bridge allows computers on two separate networks to talk to each other as if they are part of the same network.","Now, here we have an imaginary server at the top. And this server has two network cards where we insert our networking cables, any network device is called a network interface in Linux. So network interface one and network interface two are simply our network cards. And we can add these two network interfaces to a bridge. And now all of the servers below can talk to each other even though they're on separate networks.","For example, server, one would be able to contact server eight through our bridge, just like bridges in real life, connect two pieces of land separated by water. A bridge in Linux connects two networks. In fact, even more than two networks can be bridged if needed. In Linux networking terms, the bridge is also called the controller and the network devices that are part of the bridge are called ports. Now, let's discuss our next topic.","Bonding. We can take two or more network devices and bond them together depending on the bond type. This can help in different ways. Here are three examples. One, it can make the connection to a network more resilient and able to keep working.","Even if a network card goes down or gets disconnected two, it can increase network throughput. For example, if one network card supports a 1000 meg connection, two cards can be combined to reach much higher speeds, it wouldn't get to exactly 2000 megs, but it'll be much higher than a single card sports. And three, it can make a connection more reliable. For example, a single network device might experience a few slowdowns. But if we bond three network devices, we might be able to eliminate those slowdowns and increase stability.","Let's see how bonding works. First imagine this setup, we have a server with two network cards called interfaces. And Linux, there's a wire connecting the first network card to some network in the office and we have a wire connecting the second network card to the same network in the office. The problem with this setup is we'd normally define two separate connections to this network under Linux, even though both connections go to the same place. And this wouldn't provide any of the benefits mentioned earlier.","No speed increase and no resistance to failures. A program could either use the first connection or the second connection to communicate with the network on the bottom. And it would have to pick just one network path that it wants to use. Let's say it picks the first network card and goes through the connection on the left. If the wire on the left is cut, then the program suddenly gets disconnected and it fails the data transfer.","Now, let's see how bonding can prevent this. If we bond these network interfaces. From a hardware perspective, we still have the same setup. Two network cards are connected to some network with the use of two separate cables. But from a software perspective, things now look like this to any program that wants to reach the network below.","It looks like there's a single network interface, the bond we just created. And as far as the program is concerned, there's also a single wire connecting it to the network, it wants to reach. And now the negative scenario described earlier is eliminated. Let's say if some application is exchanging data with the network below, suddenly the wire from network interface one is cut. Well, now there's no issue.","Linux can simply switch over to using network interface two which still has a functional wire. As far as the application is concerned, the connection from the bond it's using to the network below is still working. In other words, the operating system pretends there's a single wire between our server and the network below. And that simplifies things for us. We don't have to configure applications to use various network cards and various connections.","And IP addresses all to reach the same destination through different paths. We just tell our applications use the bond interface. Let's talk about bonding modes. Linux can handle network traffic through these bonds in many ways to decide how it should handle it. We should choose what's called a bonding mode.","Currently, most Linux operating systems support seven bonding modes. Their codes range from mode zero to mode six. If no mode is specified, some operating systems will pick mode zero by default. Also called round robin. Not all modes, increase network throughput.","Above what a single card can achieve one mode that can increase network throughput. Above what a single card can achieve is mode four also called I triple E 802.3 ad here is the list of modes and what each number corresponds to each has its own set of advantages, disadvantages and limitations making the choice is a complex task that requires thorough knowledge of the entire network in your organization. Diving into the details here would take hours to explain. But here's a short summary about the modes, mode zero. Round robin uses network interfaces in sequential order for a certain amount of data network interface.","1 may be used then for another amount of data network interface two and so on. Mode one or active backup uses a single interface and it keeps all others inactive as backups. If the active interface goes down, then a backup becomes the new active one mode two X or pick the interface to use based on the source and destination of the data packet. This ensures that if device one wants to reach device two through this bond, it will always go through the same interface. And if device one wants to reach device three, it might go through a different interface but it will be the same one for every transfer mode three.","Broadcast means all data is sent through all interfaces at once. Mode four I triple E 802.3 ad can help increase network transfer rates above what a single interface supports mode five adaptive transmit load balancing load balances outgoing traffic from the bond. That is it will try to send data from the interface. That's the least busy mode six adaptive load balancing tries to load balance, both outgoing and incoming traffic. In the context of a bond, all network interfaces that are added to it are called ports.","If we oversimplify, we can now differentiate between bridging and bonding. A bridge glues together two or more networks, helping the devices on them, talk to each other as if they're on a single network. A bond glues together two or more connections to a network that is two or more roads to a network that are presented as a single road. These are the typical use cases, at least talented network administrators can find many more varied and creative uses."],"Networking/5":["In this lesson, we'll learn how to bridge and bond network interfaces. So let's see how we can do that with Netplan and Ubuntu to get started. We can take a look at some example files. First, we'll see that there's an example YAML configuration file for just about any network setup we want. But in this case, bridge dot YAMLS seems to be what we're looking for.","Let's display its contents here. We can see it's a rather straightforward configuration. I'll explain the important lines once we edit and adapt this file for our own needs. Before that let's copy this file to the Netplan configuration directory, we'll basically use the file as a template that we can build upon to make our life easier and to avoid error messages, we'll change the permissions of this file. So it's a bit more secure.","Before we edit this file, we need to know the name of the network interfaces we'll use in our config. So let's run IP C link to list our interfaces. Now ENP 0 S3 here is our main interface. We won't touch that since changing network settings on it might break our current SSH connection. But we can freely use the other two interfaces and they are down.","So they're currently not used for anything. But let's make a mental note that the names of the interfaces will bridge together are ENP zero S eight and ENP zero S nine. Now let's edit our Netplan YAML file. We can see that this definition of a network bridge lists a single interface, but we'll want to bridge two of them ENP zero S eight and ENP zero S nine that we have on our system. Now, the Ethernet section lists Ethernet devices.","It's how we inform Netplan that ENP three S zero is actually an Ethernet device. Otherwise it would not know its type and it would not know how to deal with it. So let's change ENP three S zero to the actual name of our first interface which is ENP zero S eight. Then we'll add the other interface in this list under the Ethernet's definitions. Remember we have to respect proper indentation, but we can just model it after the two lines above.","So we'll add ENP zero S nine and DH DP four equals no to include our ENP zero S nine device. Here. In this case, we disable DHCP auto configuration for the individual Ethernet devices, but we enabled the HCP auto configuration for the bridge itself. So ENP zero S eight and ENP zero S nine won't get an IP address but the bridge that glues them together will, it's not mandatory to do it this way though, in the real world, there can be many variations to the setup. The bridges line begins the definition for our network bridges.","Next BR Z zero is simply the name we pick for our bridge. We can freely choose any name we want. If we replace this text. On the next lines, we follow up with the parameters of this bridge. First, we say that we do want this bridge to auto configure IP D four settings for itself by asking for this data from a DHCP server.","Next, we follow up with the interfaces that we want to add to this bridge. So we'll modify ENP three S zero with the name of the first interface. We want to use ENP zero S eight and we'll add our second interface to this bridge E and P zero S nine. This config is now ready to be deployed. So let's save our file and try it out and will press enter.","If we inspect our network interfaces again with IP dash C link, we'll notice a couple of differences E and P zero S eight and ENP zero S nine are now in an upstate. So they're active and processing network connections. Also notice this text, both interfaces mention master BR zero, which immediately tells us that these interfaces belong to the BR zero bridge and are slaves to it. Since BR zero is the master. Also note that BR zero now appears listed as an extra interface in this list.","So it looks like our Netplan config did its job. However, we also told Netplan to use DHCP auto configuration for this bridge. So let's check our IP settings and we can now see that our bridge does in fact have an IPV four address. So DHCP auto configuration worked out too. In fact, many other IPv4 settings were configured as well.","Now, if we take a look at network routes, we'll see that even routes were configured for this bridge. Now let's free up our two network interfaces so that we can also test how we can add them to a network bond. First, we remove the bridge definition from Netplan by deleting the AML file and then we can simply delete the bridge interface to free up our slave devices. In a real scenario, it would be better to just reboot the server to make sure that all previous network settings are reset. But for our experimental purposes, this will be enough.","Now, let's reapply the same procedure. When bonding our network interfaces first, we'll copy the example config file for a network bond definition and then we'll change the permissions and then let's edit this file, Etsy Netplan 99 dash bond dot YAML. And we need to change our Ethernet. Unfortunately, this file is missing a few important lines. If we tried to apply this config with Netplan, it would throw some errors saying that some network interfaces are not defined more specifically what we have under the interfaces line, which makes sense.","It does not know what type of interfaces these are to fix this, we can add these lines under the renderer. So we simply tell that plan that the interfaces we'll mention later in the config E and P zero S eight and ENP zero S nine are Ethernet devices. Basically wired network cards. It's worth mentioning that in a real scenario, these interfaces might be defined as Ethernet type devices in an entirely different YAML file. There might be a dedicated file defining Ethernet devices and a different file defining the network bond.","So this Ethernet's list might simply be included somewhere else. Keep that in mind when exploring and editing Netplan configuration files. Now let's continue the bonds line instructs Netplan that will define bonds in here. Next, we pick bond zero as the name for this network bonding interface. Again, we can choose any name we want.","Then we follow up with enabling DHCP four auto configuration and we list the network interfaces we want to add to this bond. So here we have it defined as ENP zero S eight and E MP zero S nine. Finally, we define parameters for this network bond and probably the most important parameter is the bond mode. In our previous theoretical lesson about bridges and bonds, we learned about various modes that bonds support the values we can use here are balancer R to use round robin or mode zero, active backup to use the active slash backup mode, one balance X or to use the X or mode two broadcast for mode 3802.3 ad for the I triple E 802.3 ad mode four balance tlb for the adaptive transmit load balancing mode or mode five or balance A lb for the adaptive load balancing mode six, I'll show you how to find these possible values in the manual. But in our case, let's stick with the previously defined active dash backup mode here because unlike some of the other modes, we can see this mode requires an extra parameter, namely what the primary or active interface should be by default.","So let's modify this line to make ENP zero S eight our primary interface in this bond. And now let's save the file to get a refresh on the values. Netplan accepts as valid bonding modes. We can open up the manual by typing in man Netplan in search for the text bonding. OK.","And if we skip to the third result, we'll see this which shows us the possible values that I mentioned before. Also quite useful, we can see other parameters to use for a bonding interface. But for now let's apply our Netplan config and we'll notice a difference here. Netplan tells us that we can't use the try command because reverting or undoing changes is not supported in case something goes wrong. So we simply need to make sure our config is correct and hope we don't break our connection on a real setup.","You should make sure you have a way to fix this if SSH won't work anymore. All right. So we can only use the apply command to activate or define network bond. So let's do that. Now, if we check the IP settings again, we'll see that the bond was created and an IP address was autoconfigured.","Also, we can spot the text master Bond zero next to our two interfaces which just like we saw with bridges signals that these two interfaces belong to the bonding interface called bond zero. And we also have an option to see additional details about a bond. We can just explore the contents in the special directory slash proc slash net slash bonding followed by the name of the bond in this case bond zero. Now, if we list network interfaces again, we see this. Now here's the interesting thing Bond zero is listed just like any other network interface along our Ethernet devices above.","So far as Linux is concerned, even if a bridge or bond is a bit more special than a regular Ethernet device, it's still treated the same way in some regards, which means we can use commands like this or commands like this, the same commands we can use on regular Ethernet interfaces, we can also use on bridge or bond interfaces. So we can add, remove change IP settings on the fly. The same way we did in our lesson about configuring IPV four and IPV six network settings. Just remember that changes made with the IP command are temporary and they'll be lost after a reboot. So thank you for watching this lesson and I'll see you in the next one."],"Networking/6":["All right. In this demo, we're gonna take a look at squid. We're gonna install it, set it up. And if you're unfamiliar with what squid is, we'll do a quick intro and talk about it. So why would you use squid?","Well, part of it is the slash Bandwidth costs, speed up web requests and block unwanted content. This is what is commonly used for. It's an open source caching proxy server. It supports HTTP HTPS and FTP. It was originally developed by the National Laboratory for Applied network research.","Now, how does squid work? Well, it caches content to save Bandwidth. It controls access to web content through AC LS or access control lists and it logs web usage for audits or analytics and speeds up repeated requests. Now, it's often used as a shared office, internet proxy or for school internet content filtering, enterprise gateway traffic logging and reverse proxy for web acceleration. It uses caching to really speed things up now that you know what squid is and how it's used.","Let's check out a demo, let's install it, set it up and see how it works. OK. I'm here on my Ubuntu server and we're going to set up and install squid actually very easy. Now, the way this works is every time a user requests a website squid can cash that response. So the next user gets it instantly from your local server instead of pulling it over the WAN.","Again, this is one of the biggest advantages of squid is it can really speed things up. So here's how we'll install it and that's it. Now, it's actually up and running pretty simple, right? But you never want to run Squid in its default form. Um especially on a public facing Squid server.","So there are hackers Attackers, whatever you wanna call them that will scan entire IP blocks looking for Squid servers because they use them to do all kinds of NPAR stuff. So we want to lock this down the way we do that is by editing the Squid con. And here we see a gigantic file with a whole bunch of values in it. I'll show you the ones that we're really concerned with. For instance, HTTP port, you can find it here and it says Squid normally listens to port 3128.","You can change this to whatever you want. And in fact, I think you should, especially like I said with a public facing squid server. Um It's a little bit of security by obscurity to do that, but it just makes it. So it's not instantly available on port 3128 telling Attackers that it's a squid server immediately. All right.","But we're going to leave it alone here. Now, the important part here that we need to look for is AC L lists. We'll say AC L. Now in here, we'll see. The AC L section says, defining an access list.","Every access list definition must begin with an AC L name AC L type followed by either type, specific arguments or a quoted file name that they are read from. So this file tells you exactly how to set this up. Now, what I'm going to do is I'm gonna lock this down a little bit, right? We'll go through all of this stuff. There is just a kind of AC L example you can use super helpful.","Now, we have a set of AC LS here. So I'm gonna add some, I must say AC L allowed I TS source and we're going to say 10.0 at zero slash. So this is a good way to allow the ips that we want to allow. Now, there's some other stuff we could do, we could really set this up uh And rather than go through it and type it all step by step, I will paste it in. Here's a really good AC L list that you can use and we're going to include this.","So this is the bare minimum right here is just allowing an IP Subnet. We're allowing an internal Subnet use this rather than just saying anybody can connect, you have to be on this Subnet and that, but let's dump in this one and I delete that because I have it down below. Now here you can say allow connect for HTTPS ports. That's 443 will allow port 80 which is HTTP uh not commonly used much anymore. And actually it looks like the safe port here.","So we have our SSL port. We're gonna add that as a safe port as well as port 80 AC L connect method connect here. We have AC L allowed IP S source and then the network that we did earlier, we're going to allow connect from allowed IP S right. And that's the AC L up here and we'll say HDP access allow allowed IP S so we're gonna allow connect, we're gonna allow pass through. And then down here you can see we have deny everything else, everything else will be denied.","And this gives us a good security setup. So really the only thing that can get through is somebody on our network that wants to get out into the internet system, CTL enable squid and then we will restart it. Awesome. Now, I'm gonna hop on to another machine and connect to the internet through squid. Ok.","Now, I'm on another machine that's 10.0 0.0 0.66. Our squid machine is 10.0 0.0 0.2. So I should be able to connect here. Now, I'm gonna do a cURL dash L Apollo redirects, HTTPS www cloud.com, we load this up, we can see the HTML from code cloud.com and we could even do a dash B make it verbose. And then up here it will show all the details.","So when you're debugging your Squid server, you might want to use that dash V so that you can scroll all the way up here to the top and you will see more verbose messages. As we know, we use a lot of sbgs and things like that. So it's going to go, but let's use this Squid server with our cURL command. And one way that we can do this is we can say export HT TB proxy and then have that be the HD proxy. Now, you may not want to do that for whatever reason.","So another way you could do this, I will type in the following. We're gonna do cURL dash X then HTTP 10.0 0.0 0.2 port 3128. And that's the squid server that we just set up and installed then dash L. So we follow redirects HTTPS www dot code cloud.com. And now if we run it, we can once again see all the script.","So we know that it worked. But how do we really know it's going through the Squid server, right? It might be kind of hard to tell, but we can kind of go up here and look at the top, you can see that we definitely specified it etc, but here's how we can find out how to see if we've actually gone through it. So how do we know for sure that we're really going through that proxy? Well, let's jump back over onto our proxy server.","Now, here I'm on 10.0 0.0 0.2. This is our squid proxy server. Now, as long as this is running, we should see the accesses that we make. So I'm gonna jump over to 10.0 0.0 0.66 other server and start throwing some requests. Ok.","And once again, I'm doing cURL dash X HTTP 10.0 0.0 0.2 colon 3128 dash L. So we're following redirects code cloud.com and there we can see the code cloud website. Let's try some other sites. We'll do www google.com. We'll try jmorgan.com, use.ycombinator.com, one of my favorite sites, right?","We'll hit that one a few times. Ok. So now we've run this a few times. We've directed it to go through our squid server. But how do we know it really works?","Well, let's jump back and check. Ok. Now we go back to our squid server where we were looking at our access log and you can see here we have the access to the code cloud.com, google.com, Jeremy, morgan.com, news.ycombinator.com, all of it is here. So this is how we can prove that the squid access is working. This is one of the things I recommend while you're setting it up is to make sure that requests are actually going through Squid and they're actually being processed.","You can do the same thing in a browser. You could put in that 10.0 0.0 0.2 in a browser and open up a website and see if it works and check the log there. So this is the basic things that we covered. Now, here's a little bonus thing that I'm gonna throw in here. This likely won't be on the test, but I just kind of wanna show it because it's a cool usage of squid.","OK? So we're not actually going to run this, but I'm going to put it in here into our squid comp. Now, squid can also sit in front of your web server. So the way that we've configured this so far is we're going to route all of our internet traffic on our internal network through the squid server as a proxy to the outside world, right? So a lot of administrators will lock down each individual machine on the network.","So it cannot connect to the internet without putting in the address to Squid into their browsers or into their networking configuration. Um They won't be able to access the network without going through Squid. So that's from the internal, going through Squid to the outside internet. You can also do that in reverse it can sit in front of your web servers and it can cache static content like images, HTML CSS javascript, stuff like that for faster load times. And this is really cool.","So I'm going to copy in what you would put in your Squid pump. So I would put in H TV B port 443 or SSL port Excel default site code cloud.com, right? So this is gonna listen in on code cloud.com and it's going to route it to appear. So in here, I'll put cash peer 127001. That's our localhost parent 8080.","That's the port that our web server is listening on. So say we have something like engine X setup. We would set up engine X on port 8080 and then here no query origin server, et cetera. So this way Squid actually works in reverse. So as people come in squid is listening on port 443 and then it routes it to an engine X or Apache instance or something that serves our website and it works in the reverse direction where it caches everything.","Like I said, images, HTML CSS whatever for faster load time. So let's wrap this up with some takeaways here. Why do we use Squid while squid saves Bandwidth, it improves performance. And as we saw when we were able to look at our log files, it gives you control and visibility over outbound traffic. So you can see where people are going, what they're doing, et cetera and it's easy to deploy and flexible, right?","It only took us just a couple of minutes to install this configure it and get it set up. So I wanted to show this um just in case there are some things about Squid on the LF CS exam. I've been told that there are. Um So this will give you a good idea of how to set up squid, how to configure it, how to test it and make sure it works. But more importantly, um this shows you a good way of optimizing network traffic.","So if you're a Linux administrator, this is useful stuff to know, even if it's not on the LF CS exam, it's useful to know how to save Bandwidth and improve performance with your internal web browsing or your external website as we showed before. So, thank you for watching."],"Networking/9":["In this lesson, we'll look at how to configure reverse proxies and load balancers for web servers. Whenever we access websites like code cloud.com or youtube.com, the content that's displayed in our browsers comes from a web server. The most simplistic scenario looks something like this. The user requests, the web page, the browser sends that request to the web server and the web server returns some content. But nowadays the websites that see heavy traffic are not accessed this way, not directly.","A lot of infrastructure sits in the middle between the user and the web server. Let's see what changes when a reverse proxy is added into the mix. If we add a reverse proxy in front of the web server, things now look like this. The user request now goes to the reverse proxy first, then the proxy sends it to the web server. Next, the web server returns the page to the reverse proxy and finally the reverse proxy hands it out to the user.","So why would we need to add this reverse proxy in the middle? Well, it brings a couple of advantages. Here's just one example, let's say we build a new and improved web server, more ram more CPU power and it's just working faster. And as soon as we finish building it, we can tell the reverse proxy to switch some traffic to it. We basically flip a switch and traffic stops going to the old web server and goes to the new one instead.","And this transition happens instantly. But if our set up would be the old model we looked at with user requests going directly to the web server, then the transition wouldn't be instant. We'd have to update our code cloud.com domain name to point to the different IP address of the new web server. And until all users get redirected to this new IP address, it can take minutes or hours. This is called DNS propagation.","DNS is short for domain name system. A reverse proxy can bring many other advantages such as filtering web traffic, caching pages to return results faster to users and so on. But it's actually the way that it can instantly redirect traffic to a different web server that created another interesting use case load balancing. We'd have to update our code cloud.com domain name to point to the different IP address of the new web server. And until all users get redirected to this new IP address, it can take minutes or hours even days.","And this is called DNS propagation. DNS is short for domain name system. A reverse proxy can bring many other advantages such as filtering web traffic caching pages to return results faster to users and so on. But it's actually the way it can instantly redirect traffic to a different web server that created another interesting use case load balancing. A load balancer is in a way similar to a reverse proxy.","But the difference is that it redirects traffic to multiple web servers, not just one. And it can dynamically pick which server is the best one to use. For example, it can redirect a request to the server that's the least busy this way, no server is overloaded and they're all used evenly. This is the core idea of load balancing to balance the load that is to split up the work that needs to be done so that each server gets a fair share. This is the core idea of load balancing to balance the load that is to split up the work that needs to be done so that each server gets a fair share that it can more easily deal with.","Now think about sites like youtube.com, there's no way a single web server can respond to the requests of millions of users at the same time. But load balancing, distributes requests to thousands and thousands of servers. And this makes it possible to serve all of those users without overusing any one server. And now that we know how reverse proxies and load balancers work, let's see how to create them. An application often used to create reverse proxies is Engen X.","Although alternative solutions exist such as H A proxy, Apache, et cetera, you might already be familiar with Engen X since it's one of the most used web servers in the world. But even though it's a web server at heart, it can be configured to work as a reverse proxy as well. And also as a load balancer. As we'll see later on the steps to go through to create a reverse proxy are quite simple. First, we install engine X then in the Etsy engine X sites available directory, we'll create a configuration file called proxy con.","We can pick any name we want for it. It's not mandatory to use this exact name as far as engine X is concerned. These are simply settings for a website that it will host. Since technically, it still functions as a web server at its core. When users connect to it, they can get to website content.","But the difference is when a user requests a webpage instead of generating it internally, we tell engine X to fetch the content from an external web server, which is what makes it a reverse proxy. Now, as far as engine X is concerned, these are simply settings for a website that it will host. Since technically, it still functions as a web server at its core. When users connect to the engine X reverse proxy, they can get website content. But the difference is is that when a user requests a web page instead of generating it internally.","We tell ingen X to fetch the content from another web server, which is what makes it a reverse proxy. We'll add the following content in our configuration file in the server block. We tell engine X to listen for incoming connections on port 80. This line might be a bit harder to understand, but in essence, it tells engine X to apply the proxy pass directive below only for requests that match this location description. For example, let's look at these URL S.","What do they have in common? Well, after the name of our domain, example.com, all of the URL S continue with a forward slash since there's no URL that doesn't add a slash. After example.com domain name, it means that this location directive will match all requests for any web address that the user might want to visit. But in some cases, we might want to proxy requests only for a portion of our website. So we could change our location directive to location slash images.","Now, only URL S like this under the slash images will be proxied but URL S like example.com and example.com/admin won't be. So you can look at this location directive as a filter we tell engine X to proxy requests only if the web address visited contains this part specified under location directly. After our example.com domain name. Finally this line, this is almost self explanatory the request matched in the location directive will be proxied to the web server specified here, we can use an IP address but also a hostname or domain name if we want to. So a line like this with web server, one.example.com is also valid.","If the web server that we are proxying requests to does not listen on port 80 we'll have to specify the port ourselves. For example, if the server listens for incoming connections on port 8081 we can modify our line this way. Now think of the web server from its point of view, it looks like the website visitor is our proxy. Since it's the proxy that connects to it depending on our setup, we might want to avoid this illusion. For example, imagine we want to collect statistics and see what users visit our website.","So how do we tell our server that we're actually making this request on behalf of a user? And this user has a certain IP address that should be logged. Well, we can add this line include proxy underscore params. This simply includes instructions specified in another file called proxy underscore Params. And we can find this file at Etsy engine X proxy underscore params.","And if we use the cat command, we would see this content, we can see that all this does is set some headers for the requests that will be redirected to the web server. This way, the server gets some additional data and the requests it receives data like the real IP address of the user that requested the page, what protocol they used and so on. After we save our file, we need to tell engine X to use settings for this website as we defined or more accurately, the website definition will tell it to work as a reverse proxy. Remember we added this config file to the Etsy engine X sites available directory. But as the name suggests, this just lists what website configurations are available.","Gen X has an additional directory called sites dash enabled and it only applies definitions for files it can find in that directory. Now, the standard practice is to simply softlink files. When we want to enable a website config from the sites available directory, we soft link it to the sites enabled directory. In our case, we would use this command also, we should disable the default website configuration that's currently active to disable it. We simply remove the file from the directory and the file is actually called default.","Now this file is also a soft link so we won't lose any content since a real file can be found in the Etsy engine X sites available directory. And at this point, it's a good idea to check our config files for errors with pseudo engine XT dash T is a parameter that tells engine X to test configuration files and we should see this output otherwise errors will inform us about the lines that we have to correct. Even though this mentions Etsy Engine xex.com, this configuration file will load other files including the proxy.com file we created. So that will be tested as well. Even if it doesn't show up in this output and to apply our new settings, we simply reload engine X and that's it.","Engine X now works as a reverse proxy, redirecting all requests to the web server at 1.1 0.1 0.1. Now let's see how to reconfigure this as a load balancer. First, we should remove the link to our old configuration. Next, a new configuration file should be created in the Etsy engine X sites available directory. Then the content we add could look something like this to understand it.","Let's analyze from the last lines upward because we're already familiar with how this block at the bottom works. It's almost the same as what we saw in our reverse proxy section of this lesson. The only difference is that we tell engine X to proxy request to myweb servers instead of the IP address or hostname of a web server. Basically my web servers is something that doesn't exist out there in the real world. But we defined what it means internally in the first block above.","Now the text at the top makes sense with the upstream directive. We defined a collection of servers and we called this collection my web servers, but we can choose any name we want here. Then we simply listed the IP addresses of various web servers with the current contents in this file. The load balancing method would be what's called round robin. This means that any time a user requests something from our load balancer, this request would be redirected to a random server.","Sometimes it would be 1234 or other times 5678. This could work fine for a website that's not seeing very heavy traffic but with heavy traffic, the random redirection might not be ideal. It could happen that the server at 1234 gets more than 50% of the request and it might get overloaded with more than it can handle to make engine X distribute requests to the least busy server. We can add this line, the least underscore con keyword makes it pick the server with the least active connections from this list. Pretty neat.","Right. Another scenario we can encounter is that we might have web servers that are more powerful than others. So they could process more requests than our weaker servers. In this case, we can assign what are called weights. If we add this keyword here, engine X will send many more requests to the server with the IP address 1234.","And even if we don't specify it, the default weight is equal to one. So server 5678 has a default weight of one assigned to it with these settings every four requests. Roughly three will be sent to 1234 and one will be sent to 5678. If we need to do some maintenance work on a server, we can mark it as being unavailable by adding the down keyword note how we can add multiple settings to a single server specified here, we assigned it a weight equal to three but also marked it as down now engine X will not redirect requests to the server anymore and we can do maintenance work, restart it and finally remove that keyword when the server is ready to accept requests. Again, here's a different scenario we can encounter maybe two servers are enough to handle web traffic.","But if one server breaks, things will be slow with just one server left around. So we want to prevent this. We can add the following line here. We're adding 1020 3040 as a backup. What this does is adds 10.20 0.30 0.40 to our collection of web servers, but it marks it as a backup, which means traffic will not be load balanced to it yet.","Instead if 1234 or 5678 breaks down, then 10.20 0.30 0.40 becomes active and traffic begins to be redirected to it as well. So it sits on the sidelines and it's only used if necessary. It's also worth mentioning that if these web servers are not listening for incoming connections. On the standard port 80 we can specify a custom port like this. In this case, we specified that server 1234 is listening for incoming connections on port 8081.","After a config file for the load balancer has been created. The rest of the steps are identical to what we did for the reverse proxy. Meaning first we should link this file to the sites enabled directory. Then the configuration should be tested. And finally, engine X settings should be reloaded with this command.","And that's all for this lesson. See you in the next one."],"Networking/10":["Hardware clocks and computers are not perfect. They slowly drift away from the real time drifting means that after a few days, the real time might be 12 o'clock, five seconds, but our server might show 12 o'clock and six seconds. In this case, it drifted one second ahead. Now, servers need an accurate clock. But you might have noticed that nowadays, we almost never have to manually set our clocks.","Our phones, laptops and servers somehow magically know what time it is. That's because they periodically get the exact time from the internet and they get it from what are called time servers. More specifically NTP servers. NTP is an abbreviation for network time protocol. Now, most modern operating systems include time synchronization software.","By default in Ubuntu, the default one is included in the suite of systemd utilities and it's called system D dash time sync D. But before learning how to work with that utility, we need to cover another important thing in the real world. We have a lot of time zones when it's 147 in Germany, it's 747 in Singapore. And if we launch a server in Germany, it might be preconfigured to use that zone. Now imagine we have servers in five different countries looking at logs can get confusing.","We'll have to constantly add or subtract a few hours to figure out when a certain logged event took place relative to our own country's time. So the first thing we might want to do after launching a server is to set a specific time zone for it, preferably the time zone where we live in or where our company's main office is a time related operations can be performed with a time date CTL utility to see the available time zones. We can type in time date, CTL list dash time zones. And there's a bunch of them and we can navigate this list with the page up and page down keys or with the arrow keys. And we can quit by pressing Q, we can see that these zones are specified by typing the name of the continent first followed by a slash character, then the name of a city and usually it's a capital city of a country or state.","Now, not when the city contains two words, we separate them with an underscore character, not a space character. Now, we can check if our change took effect simply by typing time date, CTL and I can already have it installed. As you see, then we can tell timedate CTL to turn on synchronization with NTP servers. Finally, we can use the timedate CTL command with no arguments to see if the NTP service is now active. Now, we can also check the status of systemd's time sync daemon with this command, it should show us that the service is active and enabled to autostart at boot.","We'll also see a few log lines showing us the NTP server. It's using to change the settings of systemd's time synchronization daemon. We can edit this file, Etsy, Systemd timesyncd.com. Now, if we want to specify what NTP servers it should use, we can uncomment this line by removing the preceding hashtag sign and we can list multiple servers on the same line. For example, we can type this out note the format for the host names of these NTP servers.","They're often laid out in a similar way. If the main NTP website is hosted at ntp.org, then the NTP servers might be referenced by a subdomain such as us.pool.ntp.org. In this case, us is the country code for the United States. And each collection of NTP servers is assigned to a further subdomain represented by a number from zero to N. So the first collection will be assigned to the zero.us dot pool.ntp.org subdomain and the second collection will be one.us dot pool.ntp.org and so on.","Now, here's what our file looks like with our modifications. The rest of the commented lines show us time sync these default options, the fallback NTP will be used if nothing is specified in the NTP line. Hence that was used before we made our modifications, the route distance MAC sec entry specifies the maximum time in seconds that it can take the NTP server to send network data to our machine. If the NTP server exceeds this time, then the time sync, the utility will move on to a different NTP server. The pole intervals specify how often the time sync the utility should contact NTP servers.","For example, we can see a minimum pole interval of 32 seconds here and a maximum of 2048. This means that it should wait at least 32 seconds to ask for new data, but it shouldn't wait more than 2048 seconds. After making its first request time, sync, D will adjust the pole interval between these two minimum and maximum values. So it can be something random like 248 seconds. It will choose the poll interval based on the clock drift that it observes.","If the server's clock doesn't drift too much, it can pull less often. So let's save our file and we will restart the service to apply our new settings. Now, if we type timedate CTL, then add a space character and press the tab key two times. We can see a few other commands that can help us here, show time sync and time sync status. So to check if the NTP servers we provided are actually used, we can type in timedate CTL, show time sync and there they are.","And we can also run timedate CTL time sync status. And here we can see both the current pole interval and the root distance for the NTP server used. And this wraps up our lesson about time synchronization. So I'll see you in the next one."],"Networking/11":["To manage remote Linux servers. We need two things, an SSH client on our local computer and an SSH daemon running on the remote Linux server. The SSH daemon is also called an SSH server. Since it serves user requests with the client running locally, we can connect to the open SSH daemon running on the remote server. Our Linux machine is already running the open SSH daemon.","So let's jump straight into how we can configure that the config file can be found at Etsy SSH SSH D underscore config notice the D letter in this name. It's placed there because the config refers to the SSH daemon. There's also a config file for the SSH client which can be found at SSH underscore config note that this doesn't have the D after the S SHA subtle difference to be aware of when editing SSH configs for the server part and for the client part, because the Linux operating system usually includes both programs, the SSH client and the SSH daemon. So let's go through how we can configure the SSH server or DAEMON now and we'll go ahead and put that D back on there and we'll open up the file. The first important setting we'll notice is the port number which the SSH daemon accepts incoming connections.","As we saw in previous lessons. This line is commented so it's not actively used. But we can see that the default port is 22. If we want to change this, we'd uncomment the line by deleting the preceding number sign and then change the number to whatever we want. And the next line looks like this address family.","Any. Now, this setting name is a bit obscure, but it gives us a chance to find out how we can get more info about some config options here. Let's exit the editor and then consult the manual for SSH D underscore config now if we type a forward slash and type in family, we'll find this description. Now we understand that this setting allows us to pick which IP protocols we want enabled for our SSH daemon. Any enables both IPv4 and IPv6 int just enables IPv4 and int six enables IPv6 only if we want to change password related settings.","We can search for the word password throughout this document. Since the manual is open in the last pager, we may remember that we can press N to move on to the next match. If we search for password in this manual press n a couple of times we'll end up with this result. And this is password authentication and it specifies whether password authentication is allowed. And the default is yes.","Now that we know how to find and understand these config options. Let's get back to our file. If we only want to accept incoming connections on IPv4, we would uncomment this and change it and for IPv6, we would change it to int six. Now, by default, the SSH daemon listens for incoming connections on all of its available IP S but let's say we have two IP S on our server. We have 203.0 0.113 0.1 and that one is connected to the internet and we have 10.11 0.12 0.9 that's only connected to our internal network at the office.","If we need SSH to only be accessible to employees from within our building connected to that internal network, we could tell our daemon to listen on only that IP address so we could uncomment this line and change it to listen address. And the IP. Now another option that is commonly tweaked is this one permit root login by default. It allows the root user to log in but only if authentication is done without a password. For example, using SSH keys.","That's why prohibit dash password is enabled by default here to disallow the root user from logging in through SSH entirely. We can change this to permit root login. No, now SSH supports many login mechanisms, but the two most popular are passwords and SSH keys. Now if we scroll down, we'll see this now, by default, password logins are allowed, but some administrators prefer to disable these by changing password authentication to know. However, there's another option that might still make password logins possible.","Further down, we can see this, we have KBD interactive authentication. The difference between these is subtle with password authentication set. TS users can log in by using a password but the SSH daemon won't check if the users actually type the password. For example, the user can just use an automatic way of sending the password without actually typing it at the keyboard, KBD, interactive authentication. On the other hand, ensures that the user actually types the password while logging in.","So if we disable both keyboard, interactive authentication and password authentication, we disable password logins entirely, which means users would only be able to log in with SSH keys which are usually much more secure than passwords. These keys are basically files with a very large unique code inside. Only the person that has this unique code can log in as that user. And we'll see how that works. Later in this lesson on Linux, we have the option to run a graphical application on a server but show its window on our local screen.","It's basically like teamviewer, but one for an application window instead of the entire desktop, this is called X 11 forwarding. And here we can see it's currently enabled under X 11 forwarding equals yes Now, the settings we looked at are global that is they're applied to all users that try to log in through SSH, but we can define specific settings for specific users. Now, let's say we use this global setting password authentication. No, no user would be able to log in without a password. But let's say we want to make an Exception for a single user named Aaron.","We could scroll down and see an example of how we could do this. And it's right here at the bottom example of overwriting settings on a per user basis. So for our purpose, we could say Matchy or Aaron password authentication. Yes. And this would create an Exception for Aaron.","Now, this user can log in with his password while all the other users can't let's exit the editor. Now, every time we change this config file, we need to reload the SSH daemon so that it can apply our new settings. Now, it's also worth noting that some additional files might override the settings you just added. For example, in my case, I couldn't disable password logins just by changing the main config file. And that's because in this SSH D underscore config dot d directory, there is a file called 50 dash cloud dash init.com.","And in that file contain the following config line password authentication. Yes. So basically, even if I set password authentication to no, in the main config file, this additional configuration file would be loaded next and reenable password authentication. So explore this directory first to make sure that nothing is clashing with the settings that you want. Now, let's jump over to the other half of the story.","We've changed settings for the receiving part, the SSH daemon or server. But what about the part connecting to the server? The SSH client, it also has some configurations we can adjust. Now, windows, 10 Mac Os and Linux operating systems all come with an SSH client preinstalled on Windows. We can open up the command prompt.","While on Mac Os and Linux, we open up a terminal and then we then type SSH and we're actually executing the SSH client, which is a text based program. The client keeps some of the files in the SSH directory of each user. This client keeps some files in the SSH directory of each user. For example, if we're logged in and the user has a home directory in slash home slash Jeremy, these files will be in slash home slash Jeremy slash SSH. Now here I use the tilde for a shortcut, but as you can see, it's slash home slash Jeremy slash SSH.","This applies to a Unix like operating system such as Linux or macOS in Windows. This directory will be at C users, Jeremy dot SSH. Now, back to Linux, there are usually no config files created by default for the SSH client and I mean locally for each user. This is because there's a config file for the SSH client, but it's applied globally for all users on this machine. And we'll get to that later in this lesson, we can manually create a custom SSH client configuration for our current user.","We just need to create a file called config and place it in the dot SSH directory to see what options we can place in this file, we can enter this command notice there's no D after the SSH for demo purposes, we can take a look at our servers. IP and we'll use this in our next config let's change to our user's home directory and it looks like I'm already here and then we're going to create a config file. Now, whenever we log into an SSH server, we need to use a command like SSH, Jeremy at 10.0 0.0 0.186. Now, this is not an issue when we manage one or two or three servers. But if we need to connect to many more, we'll probably forget what username and IP address to use for each connection so we can use the config file and write this inside.","Now, if you're following along with this exercise, replace the IP that I put in here with the IP of your server and replace Jeremy with your username. OK. We'll save this file. Now, after we save this file, we'll need to change the permissions like this. The SSH client requires that this file cannot be read by other users for security purposes.","But now we don't need to remember the IP and username for each server. We just have to remember the name for the host that we defined in our config we can connect to our server with this command. And here this is the first time we're connecting. So it doesn't recognize the fingerprint and we'll say yes. And we're in now, let's loglog out from the second SSH session.","Moving on to our next topic, we saw how to create custom settings for the SSH client for a specific user. But what about global settings for the SSH client that's applied to all users? Well, that can be found in this file. Now, even though these lines are commented, they show us the default values. For example, we can see port 22.","That means by default, the SSH client will try to connect to port 22 on the remote server. But let's say that our internal network of servers uses an alternate port number 229. Well, we can uncomment this and change it to 229. Now, whoever is using the SSH client installed on our Linux machine when they enter in SSH, Jeremy at an IP address, they'll be connected to port 229 on the remote server instead of port 22. But it's not recommended to modify this file.","A future upgrade of the SSH client might overwrite the changes. So a better way is to add a new config file in this directory. Now, remember this directory that we saw earlier, let's create a new config file and here we can define our new default port and that's it. And this is a great way to put in custom settings for all of the users. Now, on to our next topic, what if we want to log in with SSH keys instead of passwords?","First, we need to generate a private and public key pair on the client side, which is our local computer. We'll do this directly on our Linux server. But it's useful to know that this command will work the same on mac Os or Windows. And the command to generate the key pair is SSH dash keygen. Now, at the prompts, we can simply press enter even for the passphrase.","Although in a real scenario, we would want to pick a passphrase too. The pass phrase is generally a password that can be used to encrypt the SSH private key. And this can protect against a scenario where someone steals that file without the password. They cannot decrypt the file that contains the key so they still can't log into your servers. And now you can see that we've created this ID underscore E 25519 and ID underscore 25519 dot pub.","Now, if we want to be able to log in to our server with our private key we have to copy the public key to that server and we have two options. The easiest is to run this command on our local computer SSH dash, copy, dash ID, Jeremy at and then the IP address. Now, in this case, the content of dot SSH id underscore ed 25519 dot pub would be copied over to Jeremy's home directory and it would be stored in a file called dot SSH slash authorized keys. We can add many more public keys to that file. Let's go ahead and do this.","All right. And it says number of keys added. Now, try logging into the machine and we'll do that and I'm able to log right in. Now, let's check that authorized keys file. And there's my key.","Now, we can add a lot more public keys to this file. Like if we have three employees that should be able to log in as Jeremy to the server, we just add all three of their public keys to this authorized keys file. Now, sometimes the SSH dash copy, dash id command cannot be used in that case. The alternate solution is to edit the authorized underscore keys file manually. We will exit from here.","We're back on our client machine and we need to copy the contents of our public key which is stored on our local computer in that dot pub file. So we'll copy this information then SSH into our server and we would add it in here and we'll pretend that we didn't run that command earlier. So this file will be blank and we would just paste it in here. And after we save the file, we need to change its permissions and that's it. Now, whoever has the private key that corresponds to the public key that we just added can log in through SSH to that user's account.","Now, as a note, I just hit enter when it came to putting in a passphrase so I can just SSH directly into this machine. I wouldn't recommend that if you type in your passphrase, then you must have that key and then the pass phrase to get into the server. Now, it's also worth noting another important file in the dot SSH directory. Now, you might have noticed the first time we log into an SSH server, we get a message like this where it says the authenticity of the host cannot be established. Now, once we accept this fingerprint, it will be stored in a file called known underscore hosts.","The next time we log in, it goes right in because the SSH client compares the fingerprint, it's saved with the fingerprint announced by the server. If they match that means we're connecting to the same server. If they don't, either it's someone Impersonating our server or in the most typical scenario, they just reinstall the operating system for our server for security purposes, the SSH client won't let us log in. If the fingerprint does not match, we can remove an old fingerprint from that file with a command like this SSH dash keygen dash uppercase R and the IP address, this will remove the fingerprint stored for the server with the IP address 10.0 0.0 0.2 51. And there it's prompting for a fingerprint again.","Now if for some reason we want to remove all fingerprints, we can delete the entire file. And that concludes this lesson on configuring SSH servers and clients."],"Storage/2":["Now let's look at how to configure and manage swap space in Linux. In this lesson, we'll discuss how we can create a so called swap partition. Swap is an area where Linux can temporarily move some data from the computer's ram to understand this mechanism. Let's go through an overly simplified scenario. Let's imagine we have a computer with four gig of ram.","We open a video editor and this uses up two gig of Ram. We open an audio editor and this needs another two gig. Now we have no more free memory but we have a two gig swap partition. Although no more Ram is available, we can still open Chrome. Well, how is this possible?","Well, when we want to open Chrome, the following happens, Linux sees no more ram is available but it also sees that we didn't use the video editor in the last hour. It's basically just sleeping there inactive. So it decides that it can move the data and memory used by the video editor to our swap partition by moving data from Ram to swap it, freed up two gig of ram. So Chrome can now use that free memory. Now let's see some commands to check if the system uses any kind of swap areas.","We can use this command swap on dash dash show since we don't have swap set up yet, the command will show nothing at this point. So let's set up a partition to be used to swap. First, we take a look at what partitions we have available. Now, in the previous lesson, we created the partition at B db three specifically to be used to swap and this is currently empty with no data inside before it can be used to swap. It has to be prepared.","Now, if you've ever formatted a USB stick with a file system like FAT32 or NTFS, this is a similar process. It basically writes some small header data on the partition and labels it. So the system knows this is meant to be used as a swap area to format it as swap. We use this command and now we're telling Linux use this partition as swap. The verbose option makes the command output more details about what it's doing and it seems like everything worked perfectly.","We can check with swapon dash dash show once again and this time the command will list our swap partition here. However, there's still a problem if we reboot this system slash DEV slash V db three won't be used to swap anymore because the change we made is temporary. But we'll see how we can tell Linux to automatically use this as a swap every time it boots up. And another lesson when we learn how to mount partitions to stop using our partition as swap space. We can enter this command instead of partitions.","We can also use simple files as swap. In fact, if you install Ubuntu on a personal computer, that's what it'll do by default. Nowadays, it'll set up swap on a file to do this manually. First, we need to create an empty file and fill it with zeros. That is binary zeros.","Because in binary, everything is either a zero or a one, we'll use a utility called DD and we'll have these parameters in the command. Now this tells it to use the input file at slash DEV slash zero. That's a special device that generates an infinite number of zeros. When an application reads from it. After we read from that file, we need to tell DD rare to write the output.","Basically, it will copy the input file to the output file. So we'll tell it to write the contents in a file that we store at this path slash swap. Then we specify the block size to be one megabyte. Then combined with the next parameter count equals 128. This tells DD to write a one megabit block 128 times.","So we'll get 100 and 28 megabytes in total. This file is small. So it will get written fast. But if we have a large file. We might have to wait a while and DD won't show us how much percentage is written to do this manually.","First, we need to create an empty file and fill it with zeros. That is binary zeros. Because in binary, we can either have a zero or a one. We'll use a utility called DD and we'll use this command, this tells it to use the input file at slash DEV slash zero. It's a special device file that generates an infinite number of zeros when an application reads from it.","After we read from that file, we need to tell DD where to write the output. Basically, it will copy the input file to the output file. So we'll tell it to write the contents in a file that will store at FL swap and of stands for output file. Then we specify the block size to be one megabyte. Now, this combined with the next parameter tells DD to write a one megabyte block 128 times.","So we'll get 128 megabytes in total. And our final command will look like this. Now, this file is small so it will get written fast. But if we have a large file, we might have to wait a while. And DD won't show us how much of a percentage it is written, but we can add another parameter status equals progress.","This will make it display the progress it's making while writing to that file. And here's an example, command writing two gig of data, regular users should not be allowed to read the swap file. That's because this potentially gives them access to the memory contents of programs that other people might be using. So we'll set the permissions to only allow the root user to read and write to this file formatting. This as swap is the same as before.","But instead of using a file such as slash DEV slash V db three, that points to a partition, we just use a regular file in our command. And now we can use this as swap on our system and we can check the active swap areas again and we'll see it's indeed used as swap note how we can use multiple things to swap at the same time. In our case, we use the B db three partition to swap, but also our file and we can use multiple files or multiple partitions if we want to."],"Storage/3":["To store files and directories inside a partition. First, we need to create a file system on it by default, red hat operating systems use the XFS file system and Ubuntu uses the EXT four file system. So we'll learn how to work with. Both note that these defaults can change in the future as they have also changed in the past to format a partition with the XFS file system. The simplest command we can use is this pseudo MKFS dot XFS slash DEV slash S db one.","And as you know, slash DEV slash S db, one is the partition that we're targeting to create an ext four file system. We changed the suffix to ext four. Now MKFS comes from the words Makefile system. Any file system has settings that we can adjust with the commands above the default settings would be used which are acceptable for most purposes. If we don't want to use the defaults, we can adjust things with some command-line options.","We can see what file system settings can be changed by reading the commands manual. Now here I'm typing man, mkfs dot XFS. By using that dot XFS extension, it tells us that this is the manual for MKFS specific to XFS. Now, for example, here, we can see that we have the dash L option to choose a label for this file system. But let's see how this should be used.","We press forward slash then type dash uppercase L to search for this option and then we'll press N to find the next one we see this here. We see that the label has to be rather short 12 characters at most. Let's set the label to backup volume. Let's do a quick listing to see our file systems here. OK?","And we've got several of them here. But S db is the device that we're going to be working with today. Note how it shows us the default options that it used to create this file system. Instead of going through the manual, we also have a quicker way to look at adjustable file system options for XFS. If we write in the command without any arguments, now we'll complain that no device name was specified, but it will also show us a condensed list of options that can be used here.","We see we can choose a custom I node size in an I node. Things such as file attributes are stored for example, file creation date. So with larger I node sizes, we can store more attributes such as extended attributes which we'll explore in a later lesson to use an I node size of 512 bytes, we can pass the dash I option. Ok. And the utility complains here that a file system already exists.","Now, this is actually good since it can prevent us from accidentally overwriting the wrong file system. But if we're certain that we don't need the data on this partition and we just want to overwrite whatever exists there, we can force a format with the dash F option. We can also combine multiple options. We can force the format set an inode size to 512 bytes and also set the label for the file system. Now, if we type in XFS and press tab, we'll see a list of XFS utilities XFS admin can be used to change things on already created file systems.","Let's say we have one that we labeled wrong. We can display the current label with the dash L option and it's a lowercase L. There we go to change the label. We use the uppercase L as we did with the mkfs command. Now let's take a look at the other popular file system, the one that's used by default on Ubuntu ext four and we can see the options available for EXT four similar to how we did it before we type in man, MKFS dot ext four.","Now, in here, we'll see a manual for command called Mke two FS. And this can be confusing at first glance, but that's the original name for the command for dealing with EXT file system creation, at least since EXT version two. Now we can ignore this discrepancy since using MKE two FS or MKFS dot EXT four, both point to the same thing. They both reference the same program and mkfsext four is easier to remember as a command. We also have a quick way to see the available options just by typing in the command with no arguments.","And the usage is very similar to MKFS dot XFS to create an EXT four file system with default options. We would type in this pseudo MKFS dot ext four slash DEV slash S db two. And there we go. And if the file system is already created, you'll get a warning for that as well. Like say, we try to run it again.","It's going to give us a warning where he says proceed anyway. Yes or no. So that's a good way to know that you're not overwriting a file system by accident to pick a specific number of I nodes. We would type in this. Let's talk about I nodes on ext four.","A weird thing can happen. We can have hundreds of gigabytes of free space but discover that we can't create any new files. That's because we can run out of inodes. Each file or directory uses an I node. And if we run out of I nodes, then no more files or directories can be created.","Now on a system where we expect to create many small files. We can choose a large number here with XFS. We had XFS underscore admin to change properties of an existing file system. What utility can we use for? Ext four?","Well, we can open up the manual again and we'll scroll all the way down to the end and we'll see ext four related utilities suggested here and tune two FS is the one that we need to see the current properties of an EXT four file system. We use the lowercase L option. Now, this same utility is used to change file system properties. For example, to change the label, we use an uppercase L and we're going to name this one second FS for second file system. Now, if we run CFDISK, we can examine our slash DEV slash S db device and verify here.","We can see the labels and file system types for our new file systems. Here. We have first FS which is using the XFS file system. Here. We have second FS that's using EXT four and I hope that helps.","And that concludes our demo on file systems."],"Storage/4":["Now, let's explore how to mount file systems both manually but also automatically at boot time. In a previous lesson, we explored how to create file systems. But you might have noticed there's still no way for us to access those file systems. How do we create files and directories on them? Well, to make a file system accessible, we first must mount it.","Mounting. Basically means attaching or plugging in a file system to one of our directories and here's how it works. First, we can take a look at a directory often used for temporarily mounting a random file system. Now this is currently empty. But let's say we want to mount the XFS file system created in one of our previous lessons.","This is stored on the partition at slash dev slash B db one to mount it. We use this command now with the file system mounted at M and T, we can now easily create a file on it. And we can see that our previously empty file system now has one file inside. And we can use the LSBLK method to confirm that our file system is indeed mounted to unmount a file system. We use the U mount command.","Now, we'd expect it to be unmount with an N but it is U mount without the N letter to unmount. We specify the directory where our file system is mounted. Now, LSBLK confirms that this is unmounted and we can see that slash M and T is once again an empty directory with no contents inside. We noticed that in the lsblk command that some file systems on some partitions are already mounted, for instance slash D slash BD A two is mounted in the boot directory. When a Linux operating system boots up, it automatically mounts some file systems.","It does this according to instructions in a file. Let's see how we can make it mount our XFS file system at slash DEV slash VDB one. Now, first, we'll create a directory that we intend to use as our mounting point. Etsy F stab is the file where we can define what should be mounted automatically when the system boots up, let's edit it now, align and FS tab has six fields. Let's take a look at what each one represents.","The first. Usually points to the block device file that represents a partition or some kind of storage space in the line. We will add, this will be the block device file slash DEV slash V db. One representing the first partition of our second virtual disk to be specific by writing slash DEV slash B db. One in our first field, we're telling Linux mount the file system contained at the partition at slash DEV slash B db one.","The second field specifies the mount point in our case, this will be the directory where we want to mount our file system to slash backups. In the third field, we specify the file system type. This is XFS in our case. But if we'd want to automount slash DEV slash B db two instead of slash DEV slash B db one, we'd type ext four. Since we have an ext four file system on the other partition, the fourth field will contain the mount options.","We'll type defaults here to use default mounting options. But we'll take a brief look at custom mount options in one of our next lessons. The fifth field determines if a utility called dump should back up this file system. Zero means backup disabled. One means backup enabled dump is rarely used these days.","So we can just set this to zero. File systems can sometimes get corrupted. And the last field decides what happens when errors are detected. We can have three values here 01 or two zero means the file system should never be scanned for errors. One means this file system should be scanned first for errors before the other ones.","Two means this file system should be scanned after the ones with a value of one have been scanned. In practice, we should often write one here for the root file system where the operating system is installed and we should write two for all the other operating systems. Now, if we would have wanted to mount our other file systems stored on the second partition, we would have used a line like this, but we won't use this here since it would interfere with our previous line that also automounts in the my backups directory. Let's save this file. If we edit F stab and don't intend to reboot the system system D the daemon that takes care of important things in the operating system won't know about our changes with this command.","We can force it to pick up any changes we made to important configuration files including F stab. If we reboot, it will pick up these changes automatically. Now, if we look at this directory, LS my backups, we'll see it's empty as expected. Also, lsblk will show that nothing is mounted in my backups. But if we reboot and then SSH back in and enter this command, we'll see the file we created on our XFS file system at the beginning of this lesson.","Also, LSBLK will confirm that everything worked as expected. Our file system was automatically mounted to the my backups directory. When our operating system booted up, we can usually figure out what we need to write in Etsy FSTAB just by looking at existing entries. But if you ever forget what each field represents, remember the man FSTAB command can be very helpful. In a previous lesson, we created a swap partition at slash DEV slash B db three.","It would be useful if this would be mounted automatically at boot time. So let's edit FSTAB again. And we'll add this line. We see that this line is similar to the line we created for our XFS file system. There are only two notable differences in the 2nd and 3rd field.","The second field should normally contain the mount point, the directory in a file system, it should be mounted in but we specify none here. Since swap is not meant to be mounted in any directory. In the third field, the file system type, we specify this as swap. And finally, we use 00 in the last two fields because swap is not meant to be backed up or scanned for errors. If we reboot and then use this command, we would see that our swap partition was automatically mounted at boot time in the Etsy F stab file.","We can also notice lines like these, note the comment above the slash DEV slash disc line. These lines can be equivalent to something like slash DEV slash VD A two mounted in the boot directory. So the lines above could have also been written this way. So why was a UUID a universally unique identifier used instead of a block device like slash DEV slash VD A two? Imagine a real server with two S sds connected to some S ata ports on the motherboard.","The first SSD could be found at slash DEV slash SD. A second SSD at slash DEV slash SS db. But if we remove the static cables and connect them in reverse order on the motherboard, the first SSD could be found at slash DEV slash S db instead of slash DEV slash SD A the next time we boot. So Linux assigns these slash DEV slash VD A or slash DEV slash V db device names in order that they're connected to the motherboard, mounting the wrong thing in the wrong place because the device name is changed can lead to bad results. Therefore, UUID S can be used.","Instead, these remain the same. Even if we connect our storage devices in a different order on the motherboard to check the UUD of a block device, we can use this command. Also, if we want to use a device name in the form of slash DEV slash disk by UUD, we can just explore the directory with an LS dash L command and we'd see the unique identifiers and what block devices they actually point to next. We could pick up what we need from here and use the slash DEV slash disk by UUD format, followed by the unique identifier in the first field of one of our F stab lines."],"Storage/5":["Now let's look at how to adjust file system and mount options. We saw how the lsblk command gives us a nice overview of what is mounted where it's short and to the point. But sometimes we also want the details, details like what file system is mounted here and what are the mount options for this file system? Of course, first, we'll need to understand what the mount options are, but we'll get to that in a few seconds. First, let's look at another command that shows everything that's mounted on this system.","Find MNT. We can spot our slash DEV slash B db one partition with the XFS file system mounted in the my backups directory. This command will output many lines usually double of what we can see here. We've truncated output in this example so that it will fit our screen now, it can look very messy and hard to read, especially if we don't need all this information. Find out shows all mount points including some virtual stuff.","For example, proc is a virtual file system mounted in the slash proc directory. This file system does not exist on some storage device somewhere. It's only in the computer's memory. In our situation, we don't need to see all this virtual stuff. So we can tell find mount to only show us real file systems.","For example, we can say show only XFS and EXT four file systems that are mounted to do that. We use the slash T command-line option. For example, proc is a virtual file system located in the slash proc directory. This file system does not exist on some storage device somewhere only in the computer's random access memory. In our situation, we don't need to see all this virtual stuff so we can tell fine mount to only show us real file systems.","For example, we can say show us only XFS and EXT four file systems that are mounted to do that. We use the slash T type command-line option. Now that looks much cleaner and it's easier to read. An important note to make is that find mount only shows us file systems that are currently mounted, those that exist on some partition somewhere but are not yet mounted won't show up in this output. Note the options column here.","This is where find mount shows us the mount options. What are mount options? It will be easier to understand if we look at the effects of these. For example, we see slash DEV slash V db. One is mounted with an option called RW.","That means we can read and write to this file system. We see that it's true since we can create a new file on it. But what if we would use an option called RO instead of RW that would make the file system read only. We can mount a file system with specific options with the dash O switch passed to the mount command. For example, we can run a command like this.","And let's check our mounted file systems again, we'll run fine mount T with our file types and we can see our mount option was applied and now we can also test it. Let's try to create a file as expected. This action is denied since our file system is mounted in read only mode. So we can draw the general conclusion that mount options in a way, tell the file system how to behave and what rules it should follow while it's mounted. Let's unmount this sometimes options like noexec and no S UI D are used to improve security on the file systems that should not have programs on them.","For example, these are used on Android phones in the storage area that should contain photos, videos and similar data. Now, if we plug the phone into an infected computer and a virus would try to replicate on the system, it would have no effect. The virus would only be able to write infected files but they'd be saved on a noexec and no S UI D mountpoint. That way they cannot execute or do any harm. They're just a bunch of files sitting there doing nothing to mount our file system.","With these options, we run the command you see on the screen, these options should be separated only by commas without any spaces between them. And once again, we can confirm our options were applied. Now, let's say we want to write to this file system. We should change the RO mount option to RW if we try this, it wouldn't work. Mount complains that this is already mounted, but we can tell it to remount the file system with the new options with a command like this.","Basically, we just threw remount in there as yet another mount option. Now the options we used up until this point are called file system independent. That's because they can be used on almost any file system. Most of these mount options are described in the manual of the mount command when you type in man mount. But other options are file system specific.","Some options only work on XFS, others only on EXT four and so on. To see file system specific mount options. We need to consult the manual of that file system. For example, man XFS, we can scroll down and reach the mount options section where we can see these described. For example, let's say we want to use the Alex size option described here.","Well, first unmount dev VDB one since it's already mounted in our scenario and it's not recommended to use mount dash O remount. In this case, with file system independent options, it's ok. But with file system dependent options, these might not be applied if the file system is already mounted. So here's how we would use the Alex size option and set it to 32 kilobytes. And remember to see file system specific mount options for ext four, just type in man ext four.","Now up to this point, we mounted our file systems manually, but let's remember that these can also be mounted automatically at boot time. How do we apply mount options there? Let's open up Etsy F tab. Remember how in a previous lesson, we said we'll use the default mount options on this line. Now, if we want to use custom options such as RO and noexec that we explored earlier, we can rewrite the line like this.","If we save the file and reboot, we can see our mount options were automatically applied at boot time."],"Storage/6":["In our previous lessons, we learned how to work with local file systems and local block storage devices. Basically data that exists on the same system that we're logged into. But sometimes we need data that resides on a different system. In this lesson, we'll learn how to use remote file systems. And one of our next lessons how to use remote block devices.","Let's go through these scenarios. In order there are multiple protocols that allow us to access remote file systems. Now a protocol in a way is a language that a client and server use to communicate. They need to speak the same language so that they can understand each other. Negotiate a connection and transfer data.","Linux supports many protocols for the purpose of sharing file systems, but for sharing data between two Linux computers, the network file system protocol is most often used. This is also abbreviated as NFS. Let's see how we can use NFS on Linux, there are two parts to using NFS, setting up the NFS server and then setting up the NFS clients on the server. We configure everything to allow it to share a file system with the world. And on the client side, we configure everything to allow them to mount the remote file system from that server.","Let's jump right into the first part, setting up the NFS server. Then we'll explore how to use NFS on the client side as well. First, we type the path into the directory. We want to share. It could be something like slash SRV slash homes or slash NFS slash disc one slash backups or whatever we might wanna use here.","Usually we'll want to point to the path where a file system is mounted, but we can also point to a subdirectory belonging to that file system. Next, we need to choose who or what can access the share. In other words, we specify which NFS clients should be allowed to use this remote file system. In this example, we can see hostname one, that's the hostname of a computer on our network. These host names can potentially take more complex forms such as example.com or server one.example.com instead of host names.","You can also use IP addresses here. So we could replace hostname one in this highlighted line with something like 10.0 0.0 0.9 or whatever the IP address of the computer accessing the share would be. And there's a third option as well. If we want to allow an entire range of IPs to access the share, we can use the Cidr notation which we learned about in our networking lessons. For example, if we want to make this share accessible to any computer with an IP address between 10.0 0.16 0.1.","The way to 10.0 0.16 0.2 55 we can type in 10.0 0.16 0.0 slash 24 here. So we could replace hostname one in this highlighted line with something like 10.0 0.0 0.9 or whatever the IP address of the computer accessing this share would be. And there's a third option as well. If we want to allow an entire range of IP S to access the share, we can use the cidr notation which we learned about in our networking lessons. For example, if we want to make this share accessible to any computer with an IP address between 10.0 0.16 0.1 all the way to 10.0 0.16 0.2 55.","Then we can type in 10.0 0.16 0.0 slash 24. Here. Finally, between parentheses, we include the export options. In this case, they are RW sync and no subtree check. What's interesting in this highlighted line is that we can enumerate multiple hosts that should be able to access the share and we can do it on the same line.","In this case, we noticed that the slash SRV slash homes directory is also shared with a second host called hostname two. And this includes slightly different export options notably, this uses the RO option instead of RW. Now let's explore what these export options mean. The important options to know are RWROR sync async, no subtree check and no root squash RW stands for read write. This allows clients mounting the share remotely to both read and write to this file system or subdirectory RO stands for read only.","This allows clients to read but not write. Sync is for synchronous rights of data. That is NFS ensures that data written by a client is actually saved on the storage device. Before reporting the operation is successful. Async is for asynchronous rights.","A client can issue multiple right requests and NFS can report that the rights are completed even before they're actually saved on the storage device. Async allows clients to do things faster. But it does not guarantee that all the changes are actually stored. So we can end up in a situation where NFS reports to the client that the right is complete, even though it isn't actually committed to the storage device yet, then if the server reboots unexpectedly that right operation could be lost, think is slower, but it guarantees that when a write is reported as being complete, it's actually stored on the device. No subtree check disables subtree checking.","Now, normally with NFS, we'd want to export an entire file system. For example, let's say we have a backup disk on the NFS server and we mount it to slash NFS slash disk one and then we'll export slash NFS slash disc one as an NFS share. But we can also export a subdirectory here like slash NFS slash disk one slash backup slash databases. If subtree checking would be active, the NFS server would always check if the requested file resides in the specific subdirectory that was exported, which can cause a few issues when files are renamed or moved to another subdirectory. Subtree checking is basically intended as an extra security check, but it has some drawbacks and a few performance implications.","That's why by default, no subtree check will be assigned to all NFS exports. Even if we don't specify this option ourselves. No root squash allows a root user on the NFS client to also have root privileges on a remote NFS share. They mount by default NFS squashes root privileges. That is if we're root on the client and we mount an NFS share, then we won't be able to read and write as root on that remote file system, we'll be squashed or downgraded to regular user privileges, all read and write requests as root on the client will be mapped to a user called nobody on the server.","And this is a mild security measure that ensures that root users on NFS clients cannot do anything they want on remote file systems. If we want to deactivate this behavior, we can also use the Noroot squash option. That way root on the client will be treated as a root user on the remote file system to see information about the Etsy exports file including other mount options and syntax specifications. We can use this command man exports now that we understand how to define an NFS share on our server. Let's say we want to add our own line.","What would we write if we wanted to share the slash Etsy directory with a computer with the IP address? 127.0 0.0 0.1. And what if we also wanted that computer to only be able to read files from the share but deny right access to it pseudo export, FS dash R, the dash R option stands for re export. You can think of it as doing a refresh based on the Etsy export file. It will share what's defined in that file but also unshare whatever has been removed.","Dash V stands for verbose and that is detailed output. This will show us what shares are currently active but also the export options that are associated with each one. Notice that in our case, we only used ro as our option, but there are many more options active for this share. That's because the NFS server will assign some options by default, even if they are not explicitly defined by the administrators in the slash XC slash exports file to wrap up. This section here are a few tips.","We can also use wild cards in the hostname field. In our Etsy exports file. For example, to share this directory with all computers that have a hostname ending in example.com, we could add a file like this. The asterisk is the wildcard sign that matches anything that may come before example.com. So clients with host names like server one.example.com or mail.example.com will be allowed to access the share.","The asterisk is the wildcard sign that matches anything that may come before example.com. So clients with names like server one.example.com or mail.example.com will be allowed to access the share and to share with any client. We can simply use the asterisk sign alone. Now, it's important to remember not to add any extra spaces between the asterisk and the mount options. Same rule applies when you use a hostname or an IP address the field where we define the clients allowed to access, the share should be glued together with the open parentheses that includes the options.","Now let's move on to the client side. Things are much simpler here. First, we need to ensure that we have the utilities necessary to mount NFS shares. Now that we already shared the Etsy directory on our server. How would we mount it from a remote client?","Well, we already learned about the mount command in previous lessons. All we need to do is adapt it to this use case mount something from an NFS server with IP address 127.0 0.0 0.1 mount, the Etsy file system or directory from that server. Finally, if we want this NFS share to be automounted at boot time, we'd go through familiar steps. First, we'd open the FSTAB file for editing. Then we'd add a line like this.","The only differences compared to what we did in FSTAB and earlier lessons is that the source of this mount specifies an IP address and a remote directory. And in the field where we normally specify EXT four or XFS as the file system type, we entered NFS. The rest is similar where we specify mount options. We can write defaults to let NFS auto configure its mount options. Of course, if you need specific mount options, you can just enumerate them here like we did in our previous lessons, multiple values separated by commas.","Finally, since this directory is not local, we don't need to check the file system for errors. So our last two fields should be 00. This covers the essentials of creating NFS servers and mounting remote file systems on NFS clients. Let's jump into our next lesson."],"Storage/7":["In this lesson, we'll explore how to mount block devices from remote servers. Now, in previous lessons, we learned about special files that can reference our storage devices. For example, slash DEV slash SD A or slash DEV slash VD A points to our entire first storage device on the system and slash DEV slash SD A one or slash DEV slash BD A one points to the first partition on the first disk. These are block special files that reference block devices, network block devices do a similar thing, but instead of pointing the storage devices plugged into our system, they point to a storage device on an entirely different computer. For example, imagine this setup, we have one server with two storage devices referenced by the slash DEV VD A and slash DEV V db block special files.","Then we have another server with two storage devices referenced by similar files with network block devices. We can essentially add a third disk to our first server NBD. Utilities create a special file called slash DEV slash NBD zero. As far as applications are concerned slash DEV slash NBD zero looks and behaves just like any other block device but whenever something reads or writes to this device, the requests are sent to the real block device on server two. So any right request to slash DEV slash NBD zero is redirected to slash DEV slash V db on server two.","But things will be even easier to understand if we look at this in practice. So let's see how we can actually use network block devices. Also called NBD. When abbreviated. When we deal with NBD, we have two locations that need to be configured.","The NBD server containing the real block device that will be shared through the network and the NBD client where we will attach the remote block device. We want to add to our system on the server where we want to share a block device. From first, we need to install the NBD server package. So we'll type in pseudo app, install NBD dash server. Next, we'll open up the default configuration file.","We'll type in pseudo BM Etsy NBD dash server. Config now by default, the NBD daemon will be run under a user called NBD, but this user does not have privileges to read and write from block devices such as slash DEV slash SD A. However, the comments here are helpful, we can see that we can configure the NBD daemon to run with root privileges. We can either specify the root user for the user and group variables or we can comment both of these lines achieving the same effect. Let's add a hash sign to comment out these lines since it's easier to do so.","Next, we'll add this under the include line in the generic section. Allow list equals true. This will allow NBD clients to list what exports a server has available. Essentially, we can see what block devices it can share with us. Next, we'll need to add the export definitions.","This is where we specify what block devices we want to share. So we'll go to the end of our file and press enter the Vim editor will automatically add a hash sign at the start of our line. So we need to make sure to remove that. Otherwise the line will be commented and have no effect. Now, the simplest export definition, we can create needs at least two lines.","We'll add this tech now between the square brackets, we specified an identifier for this particular export, we called it partition two, but we can use any name we want next under export name. We specified what particular block device we want to export. In this case slash DEV slash SD A two points to the second partition of our first disk. After we save our file, we need to restart the NBD daemon so that it picks up on these settings. But what other options can we use in this NBD config file?","Well, in the beginning of our course, we learned that we can look up different manual sections and section five is the one dealing with file formats and conventions. So we can type man five NBD server and get help for the NBD server config file. And we can see options for the generic section. That's the section where we commented out the user and group lines. This generally refers to settings for the NBD server daemon itself.","And further down, we can see options for the export section. That's where we created an export named partition two. So part of the manual explains options we can assign to each export or shared block device. The options marked boolean can have a value of true or false for options with string values, we can write any text that applies. For example, our export name equals slash DEV slash SD A two was a string option and slash DEV slash SD A two was a string.","Now let's move on to the second part of our demo configuring the client machine. Now we're here on our client machine to be able to attach a remote block device to this machine. We'll also need some network block device utilities. But this time the client side utilities we can install with pseudo apt install NBD dash client. OK.","And next, we need to load something called a kernel module. This module extends the kernel functionality so that it becomes able to handle remote network block devices. However, this only loads it for the current boot each time we restart our operating system. We would need to manually load the module again, which isn't an ideal scenario, but we can tell Debian and Ubuntu based operating systems, what additional modules to load at each boot, we only need to edit this file. And in here, we can type in NBD and then save the file.","That's because the NBD dash client utility needs root privileges to create slash DEV slash NBD block special files. The next command needs two arguments. First, the IP address of the system hosting the remote block device. And second, with the dash N option, we'll specify the name of the export. We want to attach from the remote NBD server.","When we set it up, we named our export partition two. Awesome. Now note that this outputs the name of a block device slash DEV slash NBD zero. This special block file will be associated with the remote block device. So whenever we use slash DEV slash NBD zero locally, this will actually access slash DEV slash SD A two from the remote server, we can now use slash DEV slash NBD zero as any other block device.","For example, we could type in this command. This mounts the file system for the remote block device into the M and T directory. And if we type in LSM and T, we'll see the expected files and directories. Now let's say we want to detach this network block device from our system. First, we would unmount it, then we can take a look at our existing block devices with LS be OK and we'll see slash DEV slash NBD zero.","In this list showing up with a nonzero size to detach or disconnect this network block device, we pass the dash D option to the NBD client utility followed by the path name of the device. Let's type it in. Ok. Now if we list our block devices again, we'll see the NBD zero shows up with a zero byte size, this signals that it was successfully disconnected and it doesn't point to a real remote block device anymore. It's useful to know that when we use NBD client, we can also specify a hostname instead of an IP address.","But since we are the ones that set up the NBD server, we knew the name of the export we wanted to use. But when some other administrator sets that up, we might not know. However, we can list exports available on an NBD server with the dash L option. That's why we added the option allow list equals true to the generic section of our NBD server configuration file. Without that listing NBD exports wouldn't be possible.","Now, this wraps up the core concepts around using network block devices. Now let's move on to our next lesson."],"Storage/8":["To understand one of the main advantages of LBM, the logical volume manager, let's first take a look at a common problem. Imagine we have this set up one partition at the beginning of the disk, one in the middle and some free space. At the end, it's unpartitioned space. We wanna make partition two bigger we can easily do. So we just resize it and expand it to the right since it has all that free space available next to it.","But what if we want to grow partition one? Well, this is a problem. There's no free space at the left or the right, we can't resize it. But with LDM, this is easy to solve. Instead of directly partitioning the disk, we first add it into L VM.","Now imagine we have the same setup but in an L VM managed disk, if we want to grow the first partition, the following will happen. The logical volume manager searches for some free space. It sees that there's some space at the end of the disk. So it just makes the first partition use the first part of the disk, same as it did before. But it also attaches the free space at the end of the disk to the same first partition.","This is how the disk is really used, but this is how L VM presents it to the system. Basically the first partition is made out of two different areas on the disk now. But to the operating system, L VM will present this as a regular continuous partition. This way, Linux can use it normally without any problems and we as administrators can resize our partitions without any headaches. But this is just the core concept of LBM.","This tool can do much more. So let's go through some practical exercises to understand it better. Now, we'll need to add three virtual disks to our virtual machine to have something to work with these three disks will each be five gig in size. Now, as you can see on most Ubuntu systems, this package is already preinstalled. We'll often see four terms related to LBM PV, BGLV and pe these abbreviations stand for physical volume, volume group, logical volume and physical extent.","It's a good idea to memorize the abbreviations and what they represent. That's because most L VM commands start with one of these two letters. So let's analyze them in order PBS first physical volumes, these represent the real storage devices that L VM will work with. A PV will often consist of an entire disk or SSD. Sometimes A PV can also be a partition to examine what PBS we can use, we enter this command.","This gives us a nice view of what disks and partitions are available and also what is already used in LBM structures. For example, we can see here that slash DEV slash SD A three is already used as an LBM physical volume that was created automatically when we installed Ubuntu. So we'll ignore it in our lesson. Let's add two of our new disks as physical volumes to LBM. All we need to do is pass the block device files that point to these disks or partitions type in pseudo PV, create slash DEV slash S DC slash DEV slash SDD.","Now to see what physical volumes are currently attached to L VM P size shows us the total size of the PV P three shows us how much of this is still unpartitioned or not used by logical volume. Now LDM has the physical devices where it can store data, the PV S. Next, we need to tell it how to use the storage capacity to do. So we add PV S to AB G which is a volume group. The command we'll use to do so is called BG create.","Now, its syntax is BG create the name of the group and the name of the PV S to use, for example, to add the slash DEV slash S DC PV to a volume group called my volume. We would use something like this but let's do something more interesting and add two PV S at once. Instead of just one, we can think of a volume group as sort of a virtual disk in L VM. In our scenario, we added two disks to L VM slash DEV S DC and slash DEV SDD. These are two separate five gig disks.","But once we add them to a volume group, they will be seen as a single continuous 10 gig disk. Now, why is this useful? Because we can keep growing our VG indefinitely just by adding more disks to the server and then adding them to this VG or volume group. It's like having a single disk that can keep growing as time goes on. And our server needs to store more data with a real disk with a fixed size.","We would have to replace it if our data doesn't fit anymore. This means powering off the server moving data around which is inconvenient with a VG. We can expand our storage without ever needing to power off the server. For example, let's say that 10 gig is not enough for our VG. We need more space to expand our VG.","First, we need to add a new physical volume to L VM. Then we use the BG XTEND command and add this new PV to the volume group we created earlier. So now we can take a look at our available volume groups and there's our volume, the output tells us that it uses three PV S and it has 14.99 gig in total size and 14.99 still free because there are zero LV logical volumes on it to remove a physical volume from a volume group. We use the BG reduce command. Of course, we can only remove it if L VM didn't store any data on it yet.","Now, if we don't need this PB anymore, we can also remove it from LBM by using PV, remove. OK. So we went from PV S to BGS and a VG is like a single disk in L VM. What's missing this disk or VG is not partitioned yet. So we need to add an L VA logical volume is similar to a partition in L VM.","Now to create our first LV, we'll use this command. We've created a two gig lb with a simple name partition one. At the end, we specified the volume group where we want to create our logical volume on. Now, if we inspect our volume groups, we see we now have one LV in this VG and around eight gig of free space left. Now let's create a second volume group.","Now let's remember the problem that we had earlier with a partition scheme like this, we couldn't grow partition one because it had no free space on its right. In L VM, we have a similar setup. Now the first LV is two gig. The second one is six gig on our 10 gig VG, we should still have two gig of free space left. So how do we grow our first LV to use all available space data?","And L VM is divided into multiple pe S or physical extents. For example, imagine we have two incredibly small disks, four megabytes each. And we add these two PV S to 18 megabyte VG. We create a logical volume of eight megabyte on this BG. And now we write an eight meg file to the application.","It looks like it writes this file to a file system that exists on a single contiguous disk. But in reality, L VM has split this data into two physical extents. A four meg pe will be written on the first hard disk and another four meg pe will be written on the second hard disk to tell L VM to expand our first logical volume to all the extents that it has available. We'll use this command note the 100% BG there with dash dash extents, 100% BG. We basically said use 100% of the storage space available in this VG or volume group.","At the end my volume slash partition one, we specified the name of the volume group followed by a slash and then the name of the logical volume, we can see our first LV called partition one has been properly resized. Notice how easy it was to resize a partition logical volume on the LDM compared to a normal partition. Let's shrink our first LV back to the original size two gigabytes with the dash dash size option. We can shrink or enlarge an LV to any custom size. We want not empty logical volume is like an empty partition.","It's not useful in any way to be able to store files and directories on an LV. We need to create a file system on it. We know that partitions can be found at locations like slash DEV slash SD A one. But where are logical volumes found a path to an LV is pretty intuitive. It kind of looks like this slash DEV slash name of volume group slash name of logical volume.","Now, we saw how easy it was to resize an LV. But now that we have a file system on it, we need to be more careful if we want to resize an LV with a file system on it. We should not use a command like this. This would only resize the LV from two gig to three gig, but the ext four file system would still use two gig. So we can tell L VM to resize both the LV and the file system on it.","If we pass the dash dash resize FS option. Keep in mind though some file systems can be enlarged but they can't be shrink if you ever forget all these commands either open up the LVM manual and scroll all the way to the end of it or you can think about what you want to work with. For example BG A volume group, you can type that in a terminal BG, then press tab twice to get useful suggestions. And this concludes our lesson on managing and configuring LDM storage."],"Storage/9":["In this lesson, we'll look at how we can monitor the performance of our storage devices just like CPU and RAM storage devices have their limits. If a process is overusing the CPU, keeping it at 100% the system will slow down considerably. And the same can happen if a storage device is overused. Now, we have utilities like top and HTOP to look at how processes are using the CPU in a ram. But what about storage devices?","How can we see what's reading and writing to these? There are many tools that can monitor, read, write operations. Let's look at some of the simpler ones. There's a package called cat that contains a few such tools and we can install it with the usual command pseudo app install CSAT. This contains a few other system statistic utilities.","But the ones we're interested in right now are called iostat. And Pidstat, iostats name comes from IOS statistics and IO is short for input and output because we can input data to a storage device when we're writing to it and the storage device outputs data when we read from it. Pidstat is short for process ID statistics. Each process on Linux has a unique identifier. So this tool will show us statistics for each process on our system along with their ID numbers.","Let's start with the simplest way to see a summary of how storage devices are used. We can use the IOSTAT command with no arguments. So if we type in Iostat at the command-line, we'll see output like this. Now, let's figure out what this output shows us. First of all, this displays historical usage of our storage devices.","That is, it tells us how the devices were used since the system was booted up. Not necessarily how they're used currently. And this is a bit tricky to understand when looking at the perse fields here. But let's simplify these fields show an average of usage divided by the total time since the system was started. For example, let's say the system was booted three seconds ago in the 1st 2nd, something read from the disk at 100 kilobytes per second.","In the next second, something read from the disk at 200 kilobytes per second. In the 3rd 2nd, nothing was read from the disk anymore. So the usage was at zero kilobytes per second. If we add 100 plus 200 plus zero, and then divide by the total number of seconds. Since the system was booted three seconds, we get an average usage of 300 divided by three or 100 kilobytes per second.","So I oat might show us 100 in the kilobytes red field. If another second passes and nothing reads from the disk iostat would show something different the next time we run it. So now we have 100 plus 200 plus zero plus zero, divided by four total seconds since the system is up. So we might see 75 in the kilobytes red field, the fields which are calculated this way are here, TPSKB, read slash SKB written slash S and KB DS CD slash s. When we're looking at what's using the storage devices, we're interested in the first three fields.","TPS stands for transfers per second. We can think of it in terms of how many times did the system tell this device to read or write something KB reads slash SS refers to kilobytes, read per second, KB RT N slash S shows kilobytes written per second. The fields KB red and KB written are much more straightforward. They simply show the total number of kilobytes read or written since the system booted, they don't depend on time. So there's no average to calculate something to note here.","Is that what I OAT shows us does not always reflect the exact amount of data per second written or read by a process. For example, a process can request to write one kilobyte every second, but IOSTAT might not show the device is used at thousands of kilobytes per second during that time. That's because IOSTAT tries to figure out these numbers by looking at what the device is reporting, not the process and the device might work with larger blocks of data. Even if a process sends just one kilobyte, the device might report it updated a much larger block. So estimates can be inflated here, especially for small transfers, but they become more accurate for larger transfers.","A storage device can be overused or stressed in two different ways. Something reads or writes to it very often or something reads and writes to it at a very high speed, large volumes of data are transferred. If something uses the devices, often we'll see a high TPS number. And for large volumes of data, we'll see large numbers under these two fields, the KB read and write fields. So even if something is writing very little data to the device, say one kilobyte at a time.","If it does this hundreds of times per second, it's still stressful for the device, potentially reaching its limits because each storage device can process a certain maximum amount of requests per second. If one process does a high number of transfers per second, there's not much left for other processes to use. So one process can starve many other processes by overusing the storage device. The same goes for large transfers. If the device can only write two gigabytes of data per second, and a process is already writing at 1.98 gigabytes, there's very little left for other processes.","So they will wait for a long, long time until they can finish writing their data, which can lead to a very slow unresponsive system. And this is where Iostat and Pidstat can team up and help us investigate scenarios where processes overuse our storage devices. Let's put it on our detective hats and see how by looking at some commandss in action. First, we'll open up a process that will write constantly to a storage device and we can use the following command for this purpose. If stands for input file, we picked slash dev slash zero here for a file that will generate an infinite number of zeros of stands for output file.","We'll write our data to a file called delete me BS equals one tells our DD command to write data one byte at a time count equals 1 million. We'll make DD write that one byte a million times. This BS plus count combination makes DD write for a long time. So we can test our scenario. Now, O flag equals D sync makes DD avoid using cache with right caching.","The right operation could be finished way too early. But with this argument, the right operation will go on for a long time. And then finally, the ampersand sign at the end makes this command right in the background. So we can continue to use the command-line without waiting for DD to finish. Now, let's imagine we don't know what is writing to the storage device and we want to find out what should we do?","Well, first, we'll make use of the Iostat command, but in a new way than we learned previously, we'll type this at the command-line, Iostat space one. This makes Iostat output how our storage devices are used every one second. Basically, it refreshes every one second to refresh every five seconds. We could write five at the end or whatever we need for our purposes. It's important to know that.","Now, Iostat doesn't show the average usage since the system booted up. So KB underscore W RT N slash s here doesn't show us the average right speed since the system booted. Instead, it shows us the average right speed for the last one second. Now, as we leave our IO stat command running, we'll keep seeing output like this and it helps us identify our first clues. We can see that DM slash zero and SD A are being written to.","We'll find out later how to identify what DM dash zero belongs to at this point. All we know is that something is writing to DM dash zero and slash DEV slash SD A. But we don't know what process is actually doing this. That's where the next command pidstat can step in to help with our investigation. First, we'll press control C to quit our IO stat program.","Then we'll type this command pidstat dash D to remember the dash D option. Think of devices. If we forget to add this option, pidstat won't show us how processes are using our storage devices. Now, the output of pidstat shows us our next clue. It's easy to figure out that KB underscore RD slash S column refers to kilobytes, read per second and KB plus wr slash S refers to kilobytes written per second.","Now note that the KB underscore Wr slash S column, we can see that the DD command is the one with the largest value in this field. It's the process writing the most kilobytes per second. We should note and remember this process ID by looking at the PID column. In our example, we'll have to remember the pid 1411. It might be useful to know that Pidstat also supports showing stats every X seconds if we need that feature.","So with a command like this, we can make it output io statistics for processes every one second, which can be useful if our previous command didn't catch anything interesting because pits that is a bit similar to I oat. When you use it with no command-line options, it outputs average read, write transfers per second and its historical average values. But if you want live results so to speak, then use the command like pidstat dash D one to constantly refresh data every one second with control C, we can exit from pidstat as well. Just like we did with IOSTAT. At this point, we have two clues, DM dash zero and slash DEV slash SD A are being written to the Pid doing the most intensive rights is 141.","So now for the last step, what is this DM zero device? Well, DM stands for device mapper. This was actually created by the logical volume manager as we learned about in a previous lesson and we can find out what this maps to with the DM setup command. All right. And if we follow this up with an lsblk command, we'll see this.","Now in the first command, we can see that DM dash zero maps to something called Ubuntu dash dash BG Ubuntu dash dash lb. It's an L VA logical volume created when we installed our operating system. And in lsblk, we can see that this logical volume is created under slash DEV slash SD A three. So it's a logical volume created on the slash DEV slash SD A disk. Now we know why both DM dash zero and SD A showed up as being actively used in our Iostat command at the end of the day SD A is the real device being used since DM zero is just an abstraction that uses SD A under the hood.","So our detective story is now concluded and we now know the following DD is a process writing to slash DEV slash SD A, the first storage device on the system. The PID of DD is 1411 in our example. Now, in our case, we could use a command like PS 1411, which shows us the exact command that was used to start this process. And finally, if we want to gracefully stop this process, we can type in kill 1411. And if that doesn't work, we can force quit it with kill dash 91411.","This sends the sigkill signal which abruptly terminates the process and this means the process won't be able to terminate gracefully save its data and so on. So only use this as a last resort if the first command doesn't work and that kind of wraps up our detective lesson. And it's a good example of how we usually investigate things on a Linux system. On many occasions, we have to use more than one tool and collect clues from all of them to reach a conclusion. To end this lesson.","Here are a few command-line options that might be useful. FOST dash D makes it show only the device utilization report. You may have noticed that normally it also displays some CPU usage at the beginning with slash D. We can omit those details. Another useful option is slash H to make it display IO statistics in a human readable format.","Normally, IOSTAT shows us the stats in kilobytes. And if we see a kilobytes written value of 11311196, it can be annoying to figure out how many megabytes or gigabytes. This means dash H will automatically adjust those values to kilobytes, megabytes or gigabytes, whichever makes sense for the output. So instead of a long stream of numbers, we might see 12.7 gig, which is much easier to read. The equivalent of this option in PIDS status is human, which means we can use a command like this to see stats in a human readable format.","Don't use dash H with pidstat since dash H is assigned to a different thing in this case. And finally, you might have noticed that iostat shows us activity for entire storage devices, but not for each partition on them. For example, we can see here how slash DEV slash SD A was used, but we can't see how slash DEV slash SD A one or slash DEV slash SD A two was used to check out individual partitions as well. We can use either of these commands I osad dash P all to see all partitions and devices or I osad dash PSD A to see all partitions on the SDA device. And remember you can always use man iostat and man pidstat to refresh your memory on the command options you can use.","Now, I hope this was interesting and helpful. Now, let's move on to our next lesson."],"Conclusion/1":["Congratulations on completing the LF CS course. Over the past few weeks, we've journeyed together through the intricate world of Linux system administration, equipping you with the skills and knowledge needed to Excel in this vital field. Whether you started as a beginner or sought to deepen your expertise. This course has covered a comprehensive range of topics to ensure that you're well prepared for the LF CS certification and beyond. Reflecting on our LF CS journey, we covered essential commands for system navigation and file management operations.","Deployment was emphasized automating tasks, managing processes and fine tuning kernel parameters, user and group management along with security configurations ensured robust access control, networking skills were honed in on IPv4 and IPv6 setups, packet handling and SSH management storage expertise encompassed partitioning file systems, remote storage and permissions, ensuring data integrity. These skills form a solid foundation for successful Linux system administration endeavors. But our journey wasn't limited to theory through practical hands on labs. You had the chance to apply your knowledge immediately. These labs simulated real world scenarios, reinforcing your learning and ensuring that you're ready to tackle actual system administration tasks with confidence and precision.","I want to extend my deepest gratitude to each and every one of you for your dedication, enthusiasm and commitment throughout this course, remember, learning is a continuous journey and the knowledge you've gained here is just the beginning. Continue to explore experiment and embrace the ever evolving world of Linux and open source technologies. If you're looking to boost your skills and credentials in the field of system administration, I highly recommend checking out the Red Hat certified system administrator or Rh CS A course available here on code cloud. This course is designed to help you master the essentials of Red Hat enterprise Linux and prepare you for the Rh CS A certification exam. As you move forward, know that the skills you've acquired here have the potential to significantly impact your career and the future of technology.","So go for it with confidence, curiosity and courage. The world of Linux System Administration awaits and I have no doubt that you're ready to make a mark. Thank you once again and best of luck on your journey ahead."]};
